#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""tk-queue — deterministic gate for a project's pending-items queue.

The ONLY writer of the two auto-memory queue files:

  next-steps.md   open items only (the queue /tk:kickoff dispatches)
  done-log.md     what left the queue, when, and how (feeds progress reports)

An item leaves next-steps.md the moment it is resolved or discarded — `done`
and `cancel` move it to done-log.md in one command, log written first: a crash
between the two writes can leave the item in both files (rerun-visible), never
lost. That is what keeps finished work from silently accumulating in the queue.

Items are identified by sequential IDs (**T001**, **T002**, ...) unique within
a project (both files scanned, at the positions where a WRITER puts an ID, so
a closed item's ID is never reused and an ID merely quoted in prose is not
mistaken for an allocated one). Two tolerances at that position, both one-way —
they can only make MORE IDs count as taken, never fewer: DECORATION between the
box and the ID is read through (`- [x] ✅ **T020**`, `~~**T012**~~`,
`**~~T012~~**` — a word is not decoration, so prose still allocates nothing),
and the CHECKBOX is not read at all, so an ID parked in done-log.md under an
open box is spent all the same. Open items predating this script may lack IDs;
`migrate` assigns them (legacy [x]
items move to the log verbatim, ID-less, and — carrying no `- YYYY-MM-DD —`
prefix — stay outside `report`'s scope; read the log itself for that history).
The same command FOLDS a legacy item's `**Field:** value.` chain up onto the
first line, the one position every gate reads it at — and prints the items it
left alone rather than folding a chain it would have had to guess at.
`migrate --dry-run` prints that entire report and writes no queue, no log and
no briefing: on a file that holds the user's own prose and has no other copy,
the preview comes before the rewrite.

Priority is the file's ORDER, global — no score, no hidden heuristic. `add` puts a
new item at the end and `bump` moves one to the top; the package modes take the
filtered top — `pack` is that filter made executable, and `tk-queue pack --help`
carries its rules and the shape it prints.

The DECISION class parks the queue until the user is back, so it is never reached
by omission: `add --class DECISION` and any `edit` that sets that class demand
`--deferred <justification>`, which lands in the item as a **Deferred:** field.
The default path is the other one — ask the decision at birth, write it into the
item's text and Criterion, and the item is AUTONOMOUS.

WHERE an item runs is a field of its own, orthogonal to the class: **Env:**
names an environment from the site file's roster (`~/.claude/tk/env`, read by
tk_site.py beside this script), and its ABSENCE means the machine that owns the
queue. The value is validated by exact equality against that roster, and refused
when it is not in it — an environment nothing validated is a phantom one, an
item no machine ever picks up with no error to say why. No roster, no `--env`:
the flag is refused with the file to create, because the plugin ships no
machine's name of its own.

WHERE the item CAME FROM is a pair of optional fields, written by whoever imports
a forge ticket into the queue: **Ticket:** names the issue the item implements
(it feeds the `closes` line of its PR) and **Spec:** names the spec that issue
belongs to. **Spec:** is the one `pack` reads — it decides the item's LANE, and
`tk-queue pack --help` carries the rule. Both are forge references (`<repo>#<n>`)
and both are validated by SHAPE, for the reason `--env` is validated against the
roster: neither failure reports itself. An item born in conversation has neither.

WHERE THE WORK LANDS is a third optional field, and it is a different question
from the two above: **Ticket:** and **Spec:** name the TRACKER the issue is filed
in, and the tracker is routinely another repository than the code's. **Repo:**
names the code's. The gate is a WHITELIST of the shapes this account addresses
repositories by — an https/ssh/scp URL, `file://`, or an absolute path — and
REPO_RE carries the list and the reasoning. A remote name and a relative path are
not on it, because both resolve against a cwd, and the cwd of whoever reads this
queue is the queue's own directory — routinely a clone of something else. Run from
there, `git ls-remote origin` was measured exiting 0 with no output (#198). `pack`
returns the value. An item carrying none is an item whose repository its caller
names from outside, as every caller did before.

WHO is working on it is a fourth field, and the only one this script writes on
its own: `claim <id> --as <name>` marks the item under the queue's exclusive
lock, and a second `claim` is REFUSED naming the owner and the moment — that is
how two sibling sessions on one queue stop executing the same item (collisions
measured 2026-08-18). The mark leaves with the item on `done`/`cancel`, and
`release <id>` hands it back to the queue without closing it. It is deliberately
NOT an `edit` flag: `edit --claimed <other>` would take a held item in a second
command, which is precisely what `claim` refuses in one.

Size ceilings: an item is a PENDING ACTION, not an essay. Durable context
belongs in a memory file or the wiki, linked from the item. TWO ceilings, and
which one a flag answers to depends on the flag's NATURE:

  block (CEILING)   the whole item. `add` always. `edit` whenever a PROSE flag
                    is used (--text, --criterion, --risk, --deferred) and the edit
                    grows the item. Exempt: the short fields below — that exemption is
                    what keeps a legacy oversized item taggable without --force.
  field             each field VALUE, on add, edit and the closes alike.
                    FIELD_CEILING_SHORT for --class/--effort/--project, which
                    are bounded by construction (enum, "M (~30min)", a slug);
                    FIELD_CEILING for prose.

Neither ceiling holds alone: with only the block one, a single field edit was
exempt and reached 1014 chars; with only the field one, three prose fields in a
single call reached 709 past a 700 ceiling. --force raises both.

A THIRD ceiling bounds the queue itself rather than an item: the WIP cap. `add`
is refused once the OPEN items reach `max-open-items` in the site file, counted
across every queue on this machine's roster (which is what stops `--dir` from
walking around it). It has NO bypass — --force does not reach it — because the
only honest way to make room is to take an item out, with `done` or `cancel`;
the refusal names both. Unset, there is no cap, exactly as before the gate: the
number is the user's, chosen on the machine that lives with it. What stays open
at the cap is the pair of sinks that write no new item — `edit --text` on an item
that already exists, and `handoff`.

Every mutating command prints the resolved memory dir on stderr before acting:
the target queue is inferred from --dir or the cwd, and an unseen inference is
an unchecked one.
"""

import argparse
import contextlib
import datetime
import os
import re
import shlex
import sys
import tempfile
import time
import unicodedata

try:
    import fcntl
except ImportError:      # non-POSIX; see queue_lock
    fcntl = None

# the site file's reader, beside this script. Inserted explicitly rather than
# relied on: sys.path[0] is the script's directory only when Python launched
# THIS file, and the suite also loads it as a module
sys.path.insert(0, os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))
import tk_site      # noqa: E402  (the path above is what makes it importable)

# `tk-roster` carries no `.py` suffix, so it is loaded by path — the same four
# lines `tk-hygiene` beside it uses, and for the same reason: the roster already
# answers which queues exist on this machine, and a second sweep written here
# would be a second answer to it. The WIP cap needs that answer (see check_wip).
# The two copies do NOT behave alike, and saying so is cheaper than implying it:
# `tk-hygiene` loads at import and lets a failure raise, this one loads lazily
# and reports through `fail()`. One shared loader would settle that, and it is a
# change to `tk-hygiene` as much as to this file — out of this slice.
import importlib.machinery  # noqa: E402
import importlib.util       # noqa: E402


_ROSTER = None


def roster():
    """The `tk-roster` module, loaded ON FIRST USE and never at import time.

    Only the WIP cap reads it, and only on a machine that set a cap. Loaded at
    import, it would make every `list`, `done` and `add` — on every machine,
    capped or not — depend on a file none of them reads. Measured before this
    was lazy: with `tk-roster` deleted or carrying a syntax error, `list` and
    `done` died in a raw traceback, `done` being the very remedy the cap's
    refusal prints.

    A load that fails is REPORTED rather than raised, for the same reason the
    site file's reader reports: the broken file is an installation of this
    plugin, and a traceback names a line of Python instead of the file to fix.
    """
    global _ROSTER
    if _ROSTER is None:
        path = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)), "tk-roster")
        loader = importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader("tk_roster", path)
        spec = importlib.util.spec_from_loader("tk_roster", loader)
        mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
        try:
            loader.exec_module(mod)
        except Exception as e:
            fail(f"`tk-roster` beside this script could not be loaded "
                 f"({e.__class__.__name__}: {e}), and the WIP cap is summed over the "
                 f"queues it reports. Restore that file, or take `{WIP_KEY}` out of "
                 "the site file to run with no cap at all.")
        _ROSTER = mod
    return _ROSTER

CLASSES = ("AUTONOMOUS", "DECISION", "BLOCKED", "EXTERNAL", "RECURRING")
# Class VALUES a human wrote in Portuguese, and the enum each names. The field
# NAME in Portuguese was never the problem — `Classe` and `Esforço` are read as
# the fields they are — but the VALUE is what every gate compares against
# CLASSES, so `**Classe:** EXTERNA.` leaves the item invisible to `pack` and to
# every afk package. Measured 2026-09-06 across the twelve real queues: 26 items
# in five of them, spelled AUTÔNOMA, BLOQUEADA, DECISÃO and EXTERNA.
#
# An explicit map and nothing wider. A rule ("strip the accents, take the stem")
# would translate a word nobody wrote — and a value outside this map is a value
# `migrate` NAMES rather than guesses at, which is the whole difference between
# a migration and a rewrite.
NO_CLASS_MAPPING = ("no Portuguese-to-enum mapping carries this spelling, and inventing "
                    "one would be inventing a class")
CLASS_ALIASES = {
    "AUTONOMA": "AUTONOMOUS", "AUTÔNOMA": "AUTONOMOUS",
    "AUTONOMO": "AUTONOMOUS", "AUTÔNOMO": "AUTONOMOUS",
    "DECISAO": "DECISION", "DECISÃO": "DECISION",
    "BLOQUEADA": "BLOCKED", "BLOQUEADO": "BLOCKED",
    "EXTERNA": "EXTERNAL", "EXTERNO": "EXTERNAL",
    "RECORRENTE": "RECURRING",
}


def class_alias_summary():
    """The map above, spelled the way the `pack` repair line prints it.

    DERIVED, never respelled beside it: the printed remedy is the whole of T134,
    and a remedy that promises a translation the command does not make is the
    same defect in the other direction. Add a spelling to the map and the line
    offers it; take one out and the line stops.
    """
    by_enum = {}
    for pt, enum in CLASS_ALIASES.items():
        by_enum.setdefault(enum, []).append(pt)
    return "; ".join(f"{'/'.join(v)} → {k}" for k, v in by_enum.items())


# The class that PARKS the queue: nothing moves on a DECISION item until the user
# is back. So it may not be reached by omission — `add --class DECISION` and any
# `edit` that sets that class demand `--deferred <justification>`, recorded in the
# item as a field. The default path is the other one: ask the decision at birth and
# write it into the item's text and Criterion, and the item is AUTONOMOUS.
DEFERRABLE_CLASS = "DECISION"
# Reserved word that CLEARS an optional field instead of setting it. `--risk ''`
# cannot do the job: argparse hands an empty string, every writer treats it as
# falsy, and the command is a silent no-op — so a Risk line written when it was
# true (a branch that has since been merged, a migration already run) was
# unremovable, and a stale Risk keeps the item out of every afk package forever.
# A reserved value reuses the existing parser: no new flag to forget, no new
# precedence rule between "set" and "clear".
FIELD_CLEAR = "none"
# Fields the reserved word may DELETE. All four are true-when-written and go
# stale: a Risk whose branch has since merged, a deferral whose class has since
# changed, an Env naming the machine an item needed before it was re-sliced, a
# blocker that was cancelled rather than closed — a dependency the queue outlives.
# None is ever deleted in a way that opens a gate — see cmd_edit.
#
# **Claimed:** ages exactly like these and is deliberately ABSENT: this set is
# consulted by `edit`'s field loop, and a field that loop can CLEAR is one it can
# also SET — `edit <id> --claimed bravo` would take an item somebody else holds,
# in one command, which is the thing `claim` exists to refuse. It is cleared by
# `release`, which reads the same chain and splices with the same helper.
CLEARABLE = frozenset(("Risk", "Deferred", "Env", "Blocked-by"))
# everything else takes the queue lock. `migrate --dry-run` is in the second set
# and writes no queue, no log and no briefing — only the empty lock below: it takes
# the lock to READ a queue no other writer is mid-rewrite of, since a preview of a
# torn snapshot previews a state that never was
READERS = frozenset(("list", "report", "pack"))
# The ONE statement of what a preview does and does not write. The banner an
# operator reads and the `--help` they look it up in both read it, so the two
# sites a machine can keep in step cannot drift: the lens campaign found this
# same claim out of sync across five independent prose sites, a new one each
# round. The three that remain in prose (the comment above, `queue_lock`'s
# docstring, and `tk/reference/queue.md` — the kickoff SKILL.md carried it until
# the prune of #191 moved the queue contract there) cannot read a constant —
# they are named here so the next writer knows the full list.
DRY_RUN_WRITES = ("no queue, no done-log and no briefing — only an empty "
                  "`.tk-queue.lock`, which never carries a byte")
LOCK_TIMEOUT = 10                         # seconds a mutating command waits for that lock
# read once, at import: os.umask can only be read by setting it, and doing that
# mid-run would race any other thread writing a file (the suite writes from six)
UMASK = os.umask(0)
os.umask(UMASK)
CEILING = 700          # chars per ITEM BLOCK; enough for title + fields + 1 link
CEILING_FORCED = 2000
# The WIP cap's key in the site file (`~/.claude/tk/env`, read by tk_site.py).
# SITE configuration and never a constant here: the machine carrying a hundred
# open items and the one carrying four run the same plugin, and a number shipped
# in this file would refuse every `add` on the first of them the day it landed.
# UNSET MEANS NO CAP — the behaviour every queue had before this gate — so the
# number is chosen by the user, once, on the machine that has to live with it.
WIP_KEY = "max-open-items"
# The other two halves of the same gate, spelled where tk_site spells them: the
# cap asked of ONE queue, and the discount `max-open-items = auto` derives the
# total with. The names travel from the module that validates them, so a rename
# there cannot leave this file naming a key nothing reads.
PER_QUEUE_KEY = tk_site.WIP_PER_QUEUE
DISCOUNT_KEY = tk_site.WIP_DISCOUNT
# The encodings BEYOND UTF-8 that the cap is looked for in when the site file
# will not parse (see site_names_the_cap). A file saved as "Unicode" by a
# Windows editor is UTF-16, and this house runs one — the desktop.
WIDE_DECODINGS = (("utf-16", "strict"), ("utf-16-le", "strict"),
                  ("utf-16-be", "strict"))
# chars per FIELD VALUE. The block ceiling cannot be the only one: the fields a
# legacy oversized item must stay able to gain are exempt from it (see cmd_edit),
# so without a second ceiling the first is bypassed by choosing another flag —
# measured before this existed: `edit <id> --criterion "<900 chars>"` took a
# 100-char item to 1014 and returned 0.
#
# The fields split by NATURE, and that split is what decides which ceiling each
# one answers to (see cmd_edit for the block half):
#   SHORT   --class (enum), --effort ("M (~30min)"), --project (a slug) are bounded
#           by construction. Small ceiling, and exempt from the block ceiling —
#           that exemption is the whole point of T071: tagging a legacy oversized
#           item must not require --force.
#   FREE    --criterion, --risk, --source, and the close flags, are prose. A field
#           ceiling alone does NOT bound them: three of them at 199 chars each, in
#           ONE call, took an item to 709 chars past a 700 block ceiling with no
#           --force. So these also put the resulting BLOCK through the block
#           ceiling, exactly as --text does.
#   NEITHER --env, alone, answers to no ceiling here, and that is not an oversight: its
#           value must EQUAL an entry of the site file's roster, and a roster entry is a
#           bounded slug (tk_site.NAME_RE) — a gate strictly tighter than either ceiling
#           above, applied to the file the value comes from rather than to the flag. Do
#           not add it to check_field_ceilings "for consistency": that would put a
#           second, looser bound on a value the roster already decides, and the day a
#           legitimate machine name crossed it the queue would refuse a name its own
#           roster accepts. Like the short fields, it is exempt from the block ceiling
#           (it is absent from FREE_TEXT_FLAGS), for the same T071 reason. The same
#           answer covers ensure_single_line and ensure_no_embedded_marker, which it
#           also skips: a newline and a `**Field:**` shape are both unspellable inside
#           NAME_RE, so those guards would be re-checking what the roster settled.
FIELD_CEILING_SHORT = 60
FIELD_CEILING_SHORT_FORCED = 150
FIELD_CEILING = 200
FIELD_CEILING_FORCED = 500
SHORT_FLAGS = frozenset(("classe", "class", "effort", "project"))
# the free-text item fields: an edit touching any of them measures the block
FREE_TEXT_FLAGS = ("text", "criterion", "risk", "deferred")
PROJECTS_ROOT = os.path.expanduser("~/.claude/projects")

DONE_LOG_TEMPLATE = """---
name: done-log
description: log de execução — itens que saíram da fila next-steps, com data e ponteiro
metadata:
  type: project
---

# Done log

Escrito somente pelo `tk-queue` (done/cancel/migrate). Uma linha por item;
o ponteiro aponta para o endereço mais durável (PR/commit > wiki/doc >
arquivo de memória). A linha deve fazer sentido sozinha se o ponteiro morrer.
"""


def fail(msg):
    sys.exit(f"tk-queue: {msg}")


def memory_dir(explicit):
    """This queue's directory: `--dir`, or the one the cwd encodes to.

    The encoding is `tk_site.project_slug`, not a regex of its own. It was one
    here and one there, and the roster runs the SAME rule BACKWARDS — it walks
    `~/.claude/projects` and recovers the directory each name encodes. Two
    copies of an encoder and its decoder are two answers to "which queue is
    this", and the day they disagree the disagreement is silent: a queue the
    roster counts under one name and this command writes under another.
    """
    if explicit:
        d = os.path.abspath(os.path.expanduser(explicit))
    else:
        d = os.path.join(PROJECTS_ROOT, tk_site.project_slug(os.getcwd()), "memory")
    if not os.path.isdir(d):
        fail(f"memory dir not found: {d}\n(use --dir, or run from the project's directory)")
    return d


def read(path):
    """The file's text, normalised at the door — see `normalize_source`.

    The bytes are read RAW and handed there, rather than opened with an encoding
    argument, because one argument answers only one of the three questions a
    queue file poses: which encoding, a BOM that is not at byte 0, and a blank
    that is not a blank. All three are the same failure — a character no reader
    can see — and answering them in one place is what lets every reader and
    every writer downstream work on one text.

    The head of that story is the oldest: utf-8-sig, not utf-8, asymmetric with
    `write_atomic` (which stays utf-8, so nothing here ever EMITS a BOM). Every
    reader of these files anchors with `^` under re.M — ITEM_START, ITEM_ID_RE,
    LOG_ID_RE — and a BOM surviving at byte 0 is a CHARACTER sitting between `^`
    and the first line's marker, so that one line stops matching. The vector is a
    hand edit from an editor that writes one (Windows), on a file that syncs
    between machines.

    Measured on this script before the fix, on a next-steps.md whose first line
    is an item: `list` printed everything BUT that item; `edit` on its ID
    answered "T001 is allocated but appears in neither next-steps.md nor
    done-log.md. Another writer very likely removed or clobbered it" — a
    confident wrong answer sending the caller after a writer that never existed;
    and with that item alone in the file, `add` handed its number OUT AGAIN,
    which is the duplicate ID this whole grammar exists to prevent. Reading a
    file with no BOM is byte-identical to before: utf-8-sig strips one only at
    the head, and is plain utf-8 everywhere else.
    """
    if not os.path.exists(path):
        return None
    with open(path, "rb") as f:
        return normalize_source(f.read(), os.path.basename(path))


def write_atomic(path, text):
    """Write via a temp file in the same directory, then os.replace — a reader
    sees the whole old file or the whole new one, never a half.

    The temp name must be UNIQUE. A fixed one (`path + ".tk-queue.tmp"`) is
    shared state between processes: two concurrent writers open and truncate
    the same temp, and whichever calls os.replace second renames the other's
    half-written bytes into place. Measured on this script before the fix: the
    queue file came back truncated to one item and ID allocation restarted at
    T001 — while both `add` calls had reported success.
    """
    d = os.path.dirname(path) or "."
    fd, tmp = tempfile.mkstemp(dir=d, prefix=os.path.basename(path) + ".tk-queue.",
                               suffix=".tmp")
    try:
        with os.fdopen(fd, "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
            f.write(text)
            f.flush()
            os.fsync(f.fileno())
        # mkstemp creates 0600 — carry over the mode the queue file already had
        # (or the umask default for a new one), or the rename silently narrows a
        # world-readable memory file to owner-only
        try:
            mode = os.stat(path).st_mode & 0o7777
        except FileNotFoundError:
            mode = 0o666 & ~UMASK
        os.chmod(tmp, mode)
        os.replace(tmp, path)
    except BaseException:
        with contextlib.suppress(OSError):
            os.unlink(tmp)
        raise


@contextlib.contextmanager
def queue_lock(memdir):
    """Serialize the read-modify-write that every mutating command performs.

    Read → compute → write is not atomic even when the write is: two writers
    interleave, and the second computes from a snapshot that predates the
    first, so the first item is silently lost. The caller is then told "no open
    item" and adds a duplicate — the failure this lock exists to prevent.
    Holding it across the WHOLE command is what makes the read authoritative.

    Advisory (flock), which is enough: the contract already makes this script
    the queue's only writer. Readers (`list`, `report`, `pack`) take no lock — every
    write lands with os.replace, so a reader sees a coherent file regardless.

    Held is not the same as written, in both directions: `migrate --dry-run`
    takes this lock and writes no queue, no log and no briefing, because a
    preview computed from a snapshot another writer is halfway through is a
    preview of a state that never existed. So a command holding the lock may
    still leave every file byte-identical — and an empty `.tk-queue.lock` is
    then the one file it made. The timeout message below says exactly that:
    the holder is a writer OR a preview, never assumed to be a writer.
    """
    if fcntl is None:
        # No flock (non-POSIX): the unique temp name and the honest miss report
        # still hold; only cross-process serialization is unavailable.
        yield
        return
    path = os.path.join(memdir, ".tk-queue.lock")
    fd = os.open(path, os.O_CREAT | os.O_RDWR, 0o644)
    try:
        deadline = time.monotonic() + LOCK_TIMEOUT
        while True:
            try:
                fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
                break
            except OSError:
                if time.monotonic() >= deadline:
                    fail(f"another tk-queue still holds {path} after {LOCK_TIMEOUT}s "
                         "— a concurrent command holds this queue: a writer, or a "
                         "`migrate --dry-run` preview, which holds without writing. "
                         "Nothing was changed; re-run `tk-queue list` and retry.")
                time.sleep(0.05)
        yield
    finally:
        os.close(fd)       # closing the fd releases the flock


# --- item parsing ---------------------------------------------------------
# An item block starts at a top-level "- [ ]" / "- [x]" line and runs until
# the first blank line, next item, or heading. Everything else in the file
# (frontmatter, headings, prose) is preserved verbatim.

# --- the two grammars, ONE spelling each ----------------------------------
# Two shapes carry an ID in these files, and every reader of one is BUILT from the
# strings below — ITEM_ID_RE, ID_RE and cmd_edit's canonical head alike, none of
# them respelling the grammar: a next-steps item from MARKER (+ DECOR + ID_SLOT
# for its ID), a done-log entry from LOG_LINE. Two readers of the same shape
# spelling it differently is not a style question — it is how the same line came
# to allocate an ID in one file and not in the other (see the allocation note
# below), and how `list` came to leak a raw `T012~~**` into a title.
MARKER = r"- \[( |x)\] "                        # group 1: the box
# What a human may put between the box and the ID, and what the ID itself may be
# wrapped in. The DISCRIMINATOR is prose, and prose is WORDS: DECOR admits only
# characters that are neither alphanumeric nor a line break, at most DECOR_MAX of
# them, so "✅ " and "~~" get through and "feito junto com o " cannot — one word
# character and the match is over. Emoji before the ID is the real house style,
# not a hypothetical: a real queue carries
# `- [x] ✅ **T042** — item text ...` today, and a slot that
# tolerated only `~~` made that allocation invisible — the direction that hands
# the number out twice. The ceiling is what keeps DECOR a decoration rather than
# a wildcard; it fits an emoji plus a space plus a strikethrough (4 chars), with
# room for a variation selector or a ZWJ sequence.
DECOR_MAX = 6
DECOR = r"[^\w\n]{0,%d}" % DECOR_MAX
# group 2 after MARKER (+ DECOR): the ID. The strikethrough may sit on either
# side of the bold — `~~**T012**~~` is DECOR + slot, `**~~T012~~**` is the slot's
# own inner pair. Both are how a human ticks off a legacy item, and reading only
# one of them left the other free to be handed out a second time.
ID_SLOT = r"\*\*(?:~~)?T([0-9]{3,})(?:~~)?\*\*"
LOG_LINE = r"- ([0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}) — "  # group 1: the date

ITEM_START = re.compile("^" + MARKER, re.M)
# the same bold ID ANYWHERE in a block — a citation, not an allocation. Built
# from ID_SLOT so a form the allocator reads is a form item_title strips: spelled
# separately, `**~~T012~~**` was an ID for one and raw text for the other, and
# `list` leaked the fragment `T012~~**` into the title.
ID_RE = re.compile(r"(?:~~)?" + ID_SLOT + r"(?:~~)?")

# --- where an ID is ALLOCATED, as against merely MENTIONED -----------------
# An ID counts as taken only where a WRITER of these files PUTS it. There are
# exactly three such positions, one per writer:
#
#   next-steps.md  the item marker        "- [ ] **T007** — ..."   compose_item, migrate
#   done-log.md    an entry's ID column   "- 2026-08-13 — FEITO — T072 ..."  append_done_line
#   done-log.md    a legacy [x] line moved verbatim by `migrate`
#
# Everywhere else a T-number is PROSE: a --note reading "atribuiu IDs até T022",
# a --summary, an outcome naming a sibling ticket still open in ANOTHER project,
# an item's own text quoting a duplicate. Scanning the whole text of both files
# counted every one of those as allocated and BURNED the next number — measured
# 2026-08-14 on this queue: a note saying an item "virou T0NN" made the following
# `add` skip that number. It is not hypothetical residue either — the real files
# still carry T054 and T086 as prose-only ghosts, counted as taken by nothing.
# And the damage is unbounded in one direction: a single HIGH id quoted in prose
# (a sibling tracker's "T900") jumps the counter for good.
#
# The same rule decides the diagnostic (see missing_item_message): an ID nobody
# ever handed out must be reported as never allocated, not as one a concurrent
# writer clobbered — that answer sends the caller hunting a writer that never
# existed. `id_in_done_log` already worked this way; these make the rest of the
# script agree with it.
#
# The first and third positions are the SAME LINE, before and after `migrate`
# moves it — so they must be the same regex, or the file it currently sits in
# decides the answer. Reading the legacy line loosely (any bold ID anywhere on
# it) gave exactly that split: `- [x] feito junto com o **T900** do outro
# tracker` allocated nothing in next-steps and burned T900 the moment `migrate`
# moved it verbatim, and `- [x] ~~**T012**~~ — legado` did the opposite, leaving
# T012 free to be handed out a second time while it sat in next-steps. ONE
# regex, ITEM_ID_RE, therefore reads the marker's ID SLOT in both files: the ID
# is at the head of the line, DECORATION tolerated (a strikethrough, an emoji —
# that is how a human ticks off or flags a legacy item) but never a word, because
# anything after a word of prose IS prose. It stays blind to the box on purpose
# (one regex, not two): an ID parked in the log under an OPEN box — `- [ ]
# **T005**` sitting in done-log.md — is still spent, and refusing to see it is
# the direction that hands the number out twice. Both tolerances are one-way:
# they can only make MORE IDs count as taken, never fewer.
ITEM_ID_RE = re.compile("^" + MARKER + DECOR + ID_SLOT, re.M)
LOG_ID_RE = re.compile("^" + LOG_LINE + r"\S+ — T([0-9]{3,})\b", re.M)
# the head `edit --text` rewrites: the same marker + ID slot, plus the separator
# compose_item writes. Built here, not respelled in cmd_edit, so a head the
# allocator accepts is a head --text can rewrite — a legacy item whose head
# carries an emoji is editable, and no second grammar can drift from this one.
# The box is not re-checked: cmd_edit only ever sees a block find_open_item
# returned, and those are open by construction.
CANONICAL_HEAD_RE = re.compile(MARKER + DECOR + ID_SLOT + " — ")


# --- THE DOOR: what a queue file may carry that no reader can SEE ---------
# These files are written only by `tk-queue` (reference/queue.md), so nothing
# below was ever produced from inside. The vectors are hand edits from outside:
# an editor that writes a BOM, a paste that carries a non-breaking space, a file
# that came back from a Windows tool as UTF-16.
#
# What makes them the worst shape a queue file takes is that they are INVISIBLE.
# The item does not fall over — it disappears, and the ID it was holding is
# handed out again. Measured on this script, each one separately: an NBSP after
# the checkbox made `list` print "(queue empty)" with the item in the file and
# the next creation re-emit T001; a BOM glued to a marker in the middle of the
# file turned that item into prose of the block above, and its ID was invisible
# to the allocator; UTF-16 raised a raw UnicodeDecodeError, a traceback naming a
# line of THIS script, which is the one place the caller cannot fix.
#
# NORMALISED AT THE MARKER HEADER, AND NOWHERE ELSE. The rule is not "clean the
# file": a queue file is the user's own prose, and a reader that tidied it would
# be editing what it was asked to display. Only the header — the bullet, the
# box, the decoration and the ID slot — is repaired, only on a line that the
# strict reader FAILS on, and the repaired text is what every reader and every
# writer downstream sees, so the next write stores it repaired.
BOM = "﻿"
# NBSP, narrow NBSP, figure space, zero-width space: what a paste out of a
# browser, a spreadsheet or a chat leaves where a plain blank was meant. They
# are mapped to a blank rather than deleted — the marker's grammar wants a blank
# in each of those positions, and deleting one would break a header that a
# substitution repairs.
INVISIBLE_BLANKS = "   ​"
# a marker header the STRICT reader would miss, spelled from the same pieces as
# MARKER + DECOR + ID_SLOT so the two cannot drift: the same bullet, box, slot
# and decoration, with the invisible characters admitted where a blank belongs.
# Anchored at the head of the line, like every other reader of these files.
LOOSE_MARKER_RE = re.compile(
    f"{BOM}*" + r"-[ \t" + INVISIBLE_BLANKS + r"]\[( |x)\][ \t" + INVISIBLE_BLANKS + r"]"
    + DECOR + ID_SLOT)
# THE SAME DOOR, ON THE OTHER FILE'S HEAD. A done-log ENTRY is where a SPENT id
# lives, so the allocator reads both files (see `done_log_ids`) and a BOM glued to
# an entry hides that id exactly as one glued to an item marker hides an open
# item's. Measured, with no warning anywhere: `done_log_ids` answered [] for an
# entry the file plainly carries, and the next creation handed its number out a
# second time — T163's own failure, on the file the repair had not reached.
#
# Spelled from LOG_LINE's pieces for the reason above: read only as far as the ID
# SLOT, because everything after it is the entry's free text — a `--note` a human
# wrote — and the door does not touch prose.
LOOSE_LOG_RE = re.compile(
    f"{BOM}*" + r"-[ \t" + INVISIBLE_BLANKS + r"][0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}[ \t"
    + INVISIBLE_BLANKS + r"]—[ \t" + INVISIBLE_BLANKS + r"]\S+[ \t" + INVISIBLE_BLANKS
    + r"]—[ \t" + INVISIBLE_BLANKS + r"]T[0-9]{3,}\b")
# (loose reader, strict reader) — a line is repaired only where the first sees a
# head the second misses. The two never both match: one wants a checkbox where the
# other wants a date.
HEADS = ((LOOSE_MARKER_RE, ITEM_ID_RE), (LOOSE_LOG_RE, LOG_ID_RE))

WARNED = set()


def warn_once(msg):
    """One warning per message per run. `read` is called several times by a
    single command — the allocator, the finder and the display each ask — and a
    warning repeated four times reads as four separate files being wrong."""
    if msg in WARNED:
        return
    WARNED.add(msg)
    print(f"tk-queue: warning: {msg}", file=sys.stderr)


def decode_source(data, path):
    """The bytes of a queue file as text, or a refusal that NAMES what failed.

    UTF-16 is read rather than refused, because refusing would leave the caller
    with a file this script cannot open and no way to convert it — but it is
    read out loud: `write_atomic` emits UTF-8, so the next command that touches
    the file CONVERTS it, and that has to be said before it happens rather than
    discovered after.

    Anything else that will not decode is a refusal naming the FILE, the BYTE
    and the encoding tried. A raw UnicodeDecodeError names a line of this script
    instead, which is the one place the caller cannot act on.
    """
    for bom, encoding in ((b"\xff\xfe", "utf-16"), (b"\xfe\xff", "utf-16")):
        if data.startswith(bom):
            warn_once(f"{path} is UTF-16 — read as UTF-16, and the next write stores "
                      "it as UTF-8, which is what this script emits.")
            return data.decode(encoding)
    try:
        return data.decode("utf-8-sig")
    except UnicodeDecodeError as e:
        fail(f"{path} is not valid utf-8: byte {e.start} is {data[e.start]:#04x}. "
             "Nothing was read. These files are written only by `tk-queue`, so a "
             "file it cannot decode came from somewhere else — convert it to UTF-8 "
             "and run the command again.")


def normalize_marker_headers(text):
    """(the text with every unreadable marker header repaired, how many).

    A line is touched only when it WANTS to be one of the two heads an ID lives
    in — an item marker or a done-log entry — and the strict reader misses it.
    The strict reader is asked FIRST, so a head that already parses is left byte
    for byte. Everything after the ID slot is the user's own text and is never
    looked at.

    Both heads, not just the item's: the allocator reads next-steps.md AND
    done-log.md, so an id hidden in either is an id handed out twice.
    """
    repaired, out = 0, []
    for line in text.splitlines(keepends=True):
        for loose, strict in HEADS:
            m = loose.match(line)
            if not m or strict.match(line):
                continue
            head = m.group(0)
            fixed = head.replace(BOM, "")
            for ch in INVISIBLE_BLANKS:
                fixed = fixed.replace(ch, " ")
            if fixed != head:
                line = fixed + line[len(head):]
                repaired += 1
            break
        out.append(line)
    return "".join(out), repaired


def normalize_source(data, path):
    """The text a queue file's bytes mean — decoded, and its marker headers made
    readable. Called at the door by `read`, so the whole rest of this script
    works on ONE text: the offsets a reader hands a writer are offsets into it,
    and the parse's round-trip invariant is stated over it."""
    text = decode_source(data, path)
    text, repaired = normalize_marker_headers(text)
    if repaired:
        warn_once(f"{path}: {repaired} line head(s) carried a character no reader can "
                  "see (a BOM, a non-breaking or zero-width space) and were read as if "
                  "they did not. The next write stores them repaired.")
    return text


def split_blocks(body):
    """Return a list of (kind, text) where kind is 'item-open'/'item-done'/'other'."""
    blocks, buf, kind = [], [], "other"
    for line in body.splitlines(keepends=True):
        m = ITEM_START.match(line)
        if m:
            if buf:
                blocks.append((kind, "".join(buf)))
            buf, kind = [line], ("item-done" if m.group(1) == "x" else "item-open")
        # `line.strip("\r\n")`, not `line.strip()`: a line of only SPACES looks
        # blank and is not one. Closing the block on it stranded every field
        # below it — measured, `list` showed `?` for the class and `migrate`,
        # the one command that repairs the shape, could not see the item to
        # report it, while `claim` diagnosed a missing class and would have
        # written a SECOND one, orphaning the real fields for good.
        elif kind.startswith("item") and (line.strip("\r\n") == ""
                                          or line.startswith("#")):
            blocks.append((kind, "".join(buf)))
            buf, kind = [line], "other"
        else:
            buf.append(line)
    if buf:
        blocks.append((kind, "".join(buf)))
    return blocks


def item_id(text):
    """The block's OWN id — the one in its marker's ID slot, never one its text
    or a continuation note happens to quote. Group 2: group 1 is the box."""
    m = ITEM_ID_RE.match(text)
    return int(m.group(2)) if m else None


def item_label(text):
    """How the item's ID is DISPLAYED — its OWN spelling, never a re-rendering of
    the number that spelling parses to. `T001` and `T0001` are two items and one
    number (`int("0001") == 1`), so a label rebuilt as f"T{iid:03d}" printed the
    SAME line for both: a caller reading `list` could not even see that two items
    existed, let alone which one a command would touch. The literal is also what
    a human greps for in next-steps.md.

    Canonical spelling is zero-padded to three, so for every item a WRITER of
    these files produced this returns exactly what f"T{iid:03d}" returned — T000
    included, which is an ID and must not be shown as the `----` reserved for an
    item that carries none. Nothing here narrows what the allocator sees: the ID
    is still read by ITEM_ID_RE and still counts as taken (see the allocation
    note above).
    """
    m = ITEM_ID_RE.match(text)
    return ("T" + m.group(2)) if m else "----"


# CLI-facing ID parser: the queue DISPLAYS "T006" everywhere and the contract
# writes `done <id>`, so the displayed form must be accepted. ASCII digits only
# ([0-9], never \d): Python's int() would also swallow surrounding whitespace,
# underscores ("1_0" → 10) and Unicode digits ("０６" → 6), which is a looser
# grammar than `done N`, not the same one.
ID_INPUT_RE = re.compile(r"[Tt]?([0-9]+)")


def parse_id(raw):
    m = ID_INPUT_RE.fullmatch(raw)
    if not m:
        raise argparse.ArgumentTypeError(
            f"invalid id: {raw!r} (expected T006, t006, 006 or 6)")
    return int(m.group(1))


def validate_blocker(value):
    """Refuse a **Blocked-by:** value that is not an item id, and hand back the
    ONE spelling of the one that passes.

    The shape is `ID_INPUT_RE`, the same one every command takes an id in, so
    `--blocked-by 6`, `t006` and `T006` are the one blocker they name — and the
    stored form is `T006`, because `pack` prints the value in the exclusion
    reason and a caller reading `blocked by 6` has to guess what to look up.

    It is not asked whether the id EXISTS. A blocker closed and archived leaves
    the queue, and a field pointing at it is then the truthful record of a
    dependency that is satisfied — refusing it would make `pack` the only reader
    that could ever answer, and would refuse the field on the very queue where
    it has already done its work.
    """
    if value is None:
        return None
    if clears_field(value):
        return value
    m = ID_INPUT_RE.fullmatch(value.strip())
    if not m:
        fail(f"--blocked-by {value!r} is not an item id: the shape is the one every "
             "command takes (T006, t006, 006 or 6), and it names an item of THIS "
             f"queue. `{FIELD_CLEAR}` deletes the field.")
    return f"T{int(m.group(1)):03d}"


def ids_at(content, *positions):
    """The IDs really carried by the given structural positions in `content`.
    Group 2 in every one of them — group 1 is the box or the date."""
    return {int(m.group(2)) for rx in positions for m in rx.finditer(content)}


def read_done_log(memdir):
    """The done-log's text, or "" where the file is not there yet — the one read
    every question about the IDs that log has HANDED OUT goes through, so a
    caller already holding the content hands it on instead of taking a second
    snapshot of the same file.

    Not every read of the file: `close` and `migrate` open it to WRITE, and there
    a missing file is DONE_LOG_TEMPLATE and not "" — an empty string would give
    those two a log with no header to insert under. Claiming all of them would be
    a sentence a reader could check and find false."""
    return read(os.path.join(memdir, "done-log.md")) or ""


def done_log_ids(memdir, log=None):
    """Every ID the done-log has really handed out: an entry's ID column, and
    the marker's ID slot on a legacy [x] line moved verbatim by `migrate`.

    Two callers, and they must not disagree: `max_id` decides which ID the next
    `add` gets, `id_in_done_log` decides whether a missing one was closed. Two
    copies of this loop is how one of them would keep a position the other had
    already dropped.

    `log` is that file's text when the caller already read it — the same rule
    `append_done_line` and `remove_block` state for the writing side, asked here
    of the reading one. `missing_item_message` puts BOTH questions above to this
    log in one breath, and reading the file once per question answers one
    decision from two snapshots: a close landing between the two reads leaves
    the first saying "not closed" and the second saying "allocated", and the
    diagnosis that comes out sends the caller hunting a writer that clobbered
    nothing. Without it the file is read here, for the caller that holds a
    directory and nothing else.
    """
    return ids_at(read_done_log(memdir) if log is None else log,
                  LOG_ID_RE, ITEM_ID_RE)


def max_id(memdir, open_items=None, log=None):
    """The highest ID either file has handed out.

    Both texts may come from the caller, for the reason `done_log_ids` gives:
    the error path already holds the queue it just walked and the log it just
    asked about, and re-reading either here would let this answer come from a
    different state of the file than the answer beside it."""
    if open_items is None:
        open_items = read(os.path.join(memdir, "next-steps.md")) or ""
    return max(ids_at(open_items, ITEM_ID_RE) | done_log_ids(memdir, log), default=0)


def ensure_single_line(**fields):
    for name, val in fields.items():
        if val and "\n" in val:
            fail(f"--{name} must be single-line — a newline would break the queue's block format")


def ensure_filled(**fields):
    """argparse's required=True only proves the flag was typed. An empty string
    satisfies it and would write a field-less item — the exact gap a mandatory
    field exists to close."""
    for name, val in fields.items():
        if not (val or "").strip():
            fail(f"--{name} cannot be blank")


def ensure_no_embedded_marker(**fields):
    """Refuse a field-marker shape (`**Project:**`) inside free text.

    Once composed into the block, an embedded marker is indistinguishable from
    the real field it precedes, and every reader takes the FIRST match:
    item_title truncates the title there, the project tag is read off the next
    word, `list` then groups the item under it, and `edit` rewrites
    the prose instead of the field. That is the whole T063 incident — an item
    whose text said "**Project:** para o done-log" acquired the tag "para".

    The trigger is deliberately narrow: the literal bold-colon shape with a
    capitalised field name, OUTSIDE a code span. Refusing beats escaping: an
    escape would need a matching unescape in every reader and in the display
    path, each one a fresh chance to reintroduce this same bug.

    It asks `markers`, the tokenizer the READER asks, and that identity is the
    whole of T134: the remedy `pack` prints for an item whose text carries a
    marker is `edit --text "<the whole text>"`, and while this guard kept a
    grammar of its own that remedy was refused by the very guard that had to
    accept it — a prescription that could not repair the item it addressed. The
    quoted form this message names is now the form that gets through.
    """
    for name, val in fields.items():
        if val and markers(val):
            fail(f"--{name} contains a field-marker shape (e.g. **Project:**), which would "
                 "be read as the real field and silently hijack it. Rephrase without the "
                 "bold '**Word:**' shape — e.g. 'the Project field', or `Project:` in a "
                 "code span.")


FIELD_VARIANTS = {
    "Class": r"Class(?:e)?",
    "Effort": r"(?:Effort|Esforço)",
    "Risk": r"(?:Risk|Risco)",
    "Criterion": r"(?:Criteri(?:on|o)|Critério)",
    "Project": r"(?:Project|Projeto)",
    "Source": r"(?:Source|Fonte)",
    "Deferred": r"(?:Deferred|Adiado)",
    "Env": r"(?:Env|Ambiente)",
    # DEPENDENCY between two items of this queue, and the third field `pack`
    # gates on. One spelling, for the reason the three below have one: it
    # postdates the hand-written era, so an alias nothing ever wrote would be a
    # second grammar to keep in step.
    "Blocked-by": r"Blocked-by",
    "Claimed": r"(?:Claimed|Posse)",
    # PROVENANCE, written by whoever imports a ticket into the queue (`add
    # --ticket/--spec`). One spelling each, and deliberately: the Portuguese
    # alias every field above carries is there because the queues predate this
    # script and a human had written the field by hand — these two have no such
    # population, and an alias nothing writes is a second grammar to keep in step.
    "Ticket": r"Ticket",
    "Spec": r"Spec",
    # the third of the group, and the only one that is no forge reference: it
    # names the REPOSITORY the item's code lands in, which the two above do not
    "Repo": r"Repo",
    # BIRTH DATE, written by this script and by nothing else: `add` stamps today,
    # `migrate` backdates it from **Source:** when that field states a date, and no
    # flag sets it. One spelling, for the reason Ticket and Spec have one — these
    # three postdate the hand-written era, so an alias nothing ever wrote would be
    # a second grammar to keep in step.
    #
    # It is NOT **Source:** with a stricter parser. Source is provenance and is
    # prose by design ("wrap-up 19/08", "[[sharepoint-m365-fonte-canonica]]",
    # "tracker"): three of every four values in this queue's own file state no date
    # a machine can read, and the ones that do state it inside a sentence. A field
    # that only ever holds `YYYY-MM-DD` is what lets `list` subtract two dates
    # instead of guessing at a sentence.
    "Born": r"Born",
}
ANY_FIELD = "|".join(FIELD_VARIANTS.values())
PROJECT_RE = re.compile(r"\A[a-z0-9][a-z0-9_-]*\Z")
# single source for reading a tag back out: the warning and the grouping must
# see the same tag, or an item warns as unprecedented and then groups elsewhere
PROJECT_TAG_VALUE_RE = re.compile(r"\s*([^\s.*]+)")
# same field, but anchored to end-of-line, for reading the tag back out of a
# done-log entry's FIRST line only (see log_entry_tag). An entry's remaining
# lines are a free-text --note that can itself quote "**Project:**" in prose —
# a real case in this queue's own log — and searching the whole entry would
# read that prose as the tag, which is precisely the T063 failure
LOG_TAG_RE = re.compile(r"\*\*(?:" + FIELD_VARIANTS["Project"] + r"):\*\*\s*([^\s.*]+)\.\s*$")


FIELD_MARKER_ANY_RE = re.compile(r"\*\*(" + ANY_FIELD + r"):\*\*")
# the marker and the blank after it, for reading a segment's VALUE back out.
# Spelled once: two readers stripping the same prefix their own way is how one of
# them comes to print a value the file does not carry
FIELD_BODY_RE = re.compile(r"\A\*\*[^*]+:\*\*\s*")


def canonical_field(raw):
    """Map a matched field word ('Risco', 'Criterio') to its canonical name."""
    for name, variants in FIELD_VARIANTS.items():
        if re.fullmatch(variants, raw):
            return name
    return None


# --- ONE PARSE, ONE STRUCTURE, ONE WRITER ---------------------------------
# Every reader below asks `parse_item` for a block, and every writer that
# changes an item's field chain hands the structure back to `render_item`. What
# that buys is not tidiness. The chain's ambiguity used to be decided TWICE per
# command — once by the reader that LOCATED the field, once by the writer that
# spliced it — and the two answered differently: measured on one item, in one
# command, each exiting 0, `edit --class AUTONOMOUS --project tk` overwrote four
# words of the item's own title, and `--risk none` DELETED the segment that
# title's prose was imitating. A writer that can only hand the structure back
# cannot splice a position the parse never named.
#
# The invariant the suite pins: `render_item(parse_item(b)) == b`, byte for
# byte, over every block of every real queue on this machine and of the
# fixtures. It holds BY CONSTRUCTION — `Item` is a partition of the block, not
# a description of it — and that is the whole design decision: a renderer that
# recomposed an item from its parts would normalise a blank, a period or a
# field order somewhere, and the first command to touch a legacy item would
# rewrite text nobody pointed at.


def markers(text, field=None):
    r"""Every field marker of `text` a reader may READ, as (spelling, start, end)
    with positions relative to `text` — of `field` alone when one is named.

    THE CODE-SPAN RULE, spelled once for every reader that asks "is there a
    marker here": a `**Field:**` inside a Markdown code span is never a field.
    Outside one nothing changes — the POSITION rule still decides which of the
    markers on the first line are the item's real fields, and `edit` still
    refuses a bare marker in prose rather than guessing at it.

    Why a code span and not an escape. An escape (`\*\*Class:\*\*`) would need a
    matching unescape in every reader AND in the display path, each one a fresh
    chance to reintroduce the bug it is fixing, and no legacy item carries one —
    the migration would have to invent them. A code span is standard Markdown, it
    renders as the quotation it IS, and the refusal in `ensure_no_embedded_marker`
    has been printing it as the way out since before this rule existed.

    Asked per LINE, because a code span does not cross one.
    """
    out, base = [], 0
    for line in text.split("\n"):
        spans = code_spans(line)
        for m in FIELD_MARKER_ANY_RE.finditer(line):
            if in_code_span(spans, m.start()):
                continue
            if field is None or canonical_field(m.group(1)) == field:
                out.append((m.group(1), base + m.start(), base + m.end()))
        base += len(line) + 1
    return out


def marker_value(text, field, value_re):
    """The value the FIRST readable marker of `field` in `text` is followed by,
    read with `value_re`, or None.

    One spelling for the two whole-block reads that want a value rather than a
    position — the class `list` falls back to, and the project tag it groups by.
    Both used to search the block with a regex of their own, so a marker the
    chain refuses to read still answered them: that is the whole T063 incident,
    an item whose text said `**Project:** para o done-log` acquiring the tag
    "para".
    """
    for _, _, end in markers(text, field):
        m = value_re.match(text, end)
        if m:
            return m.group(1)
    return None


def field_segments(line):
    """The `**Field:** value` segments of `line`, as (spelling, start, end).

    A segment runs from its marker to the NEXT readable marker, or to the end of
    the line. `*` is not a terminator, and that is T258: the old grammar cut the
    value at the first asterisk, so `--criterion 'A: o glob *.md casa'` broke the
    chain BEFORE **Class:** and the item left `pack` with no error visible
    anywhere and an age of `?`.

    It also makes the chain's old "anchored" condition structural rather than
    checked: the last segment now ends the line by construction, so what remains
    of the rule is the period walk in `chain_segments`.
    """
    marks = markers(line)
    return [(m[0], m[1], marks[i + 1][1] if i + 1 < len(marks) else len(line))
            for i, m in enumerate(marks)]


def code_spans(line):
    """The (start, end) ranges of `line` that are Markdown code spans.

    CommonMark's rule, and the safe half of it is the half this file needs: a
    backtick run OPENS a span only where a run of the SAME length closes it
    later on the line. An unmatched backtick is literal text and opens nothing.
    That direction is not a detail — the other one would let a single stray
    backtick swallow the rest of the line, which is the `*` defect (T258) with a
    different character: there the value's grammar stopped at the first `*` and
    the chain broke behind it, and the item left `pack` with no error at all.
    """
    runs = [m.span() for m in re.finditer(r"`+", line)]
    spans, i = [], 0
    while i < len(runs):
        opener_start, opener_end = runs[i]
        width = opener_end - opener_start
        for j in range(i + 1, len(runs)):
            closer_start, closer_end = runs[j]
            if closer_end - closer_start == width:
                spans.append((opener_start, closer_end))
                i = j
                break
        i += 1
    return spans


def in_code_span(spans, pos):
    """Whether the character at `pos` sits inside one of `spans`."""
    return any(start <= pos < end for start, end in spans)


class Field:
    """One `**Field:** value` segment of an item's chain, with its provenance.

    Two names, deliberately, and collapsing them is a data loss: `name` is the
    spelling the FILE carries (`Esforço`, `Critério` — the queues predate this
    script and a human wrote those by hand), and `canonical` is the name every
    reader asks by. A structure that kept only the canonical one would put
    `**Effort:**` back into an item nobody asked to translate.

    `text` is the segment VERBATIM, blanks and terminator included, and it is
    what `render_item` writes back — the reason the round-trip is byte-exact
    rather than nearly so.

    There is no `quoted` flag any more, and its absence is the rule: a marker
    inside a code span is not a field, so no Field can carry one. It was a
    provenance while the reader still read those markers.

    The offsets keep the match's own spelling (`span()`, `start()`, `end()`) on
    purpose: every reader that measures an offset then reads it the same way it
    always did, and no anchor of the mutation list is rewritten for a rename.
    They are the position in the block this field was PARSED from, so a writer
    invalidates them the moment it changes the item — see `Item.stale`.
    """

    __slots__ = ("name", "canonical", "value", "text", "offsets")

    def __init__(self, text, offsets=None):
        self.rewrite(text)
        # AFTER the rewrite, which forgets them: a Field built by a reader knows
        # where it sits, and one composed by a writer sits nowhere until the
        # block it went into is parsed again
        self.offsets = offsets

    def rewrite(self, text):
        """Take a new segment, verbatim, and re-read the three names from it.

        Read with the same tokenizer as the file, so a value a WRITER composes
        is a value the reader will give back: an asterisk or a code span in it
        is text, and a second marker in it is refused as the two segments it is.
        """
        segs = field_segments(text)
        if len(segs) != 1 or segs[0][1] != 0 or segs[0][2] != len(text):
            raise ValueError(f"not a field segment: {text!r}")
        self.text = text
        self.name = segs[0][0]
        self.canonical = canonical_field(self.name)
        self.value = FIELD_BODY_RE.sub("", text)
        self.forget_offsets()

    def forget_offsets(self):
        self.offsets = None

    def span(self):
        """This segment's (start, end) in the block it was parsed from.

        A stale offset is REFUSED, never answered. The one corruption this file
        has measured on an offset — a branch that spliced the item with the
        field's own offsets in names the caller read as the file's — truncated
        the frontmatter mid-word and duplicated the item, with the whole suite
        still green. An offset that no longer means anything must not be
        readable as one that does.
        """
        if self.offsets is None:
            raise ValueError(
                f"**{self.name}:** was written since the parse, so its offsets name "
                "a position this item no longer has — re-parse the rendered block")
        return self.offsets

    def start(self):
        return self.span()[0]

    def end(self):
        return self.span()[1]

    def __repr__(self):
        return f"Field({self.text!r})"


def make_field(text):
    """The Field a WRITER composed — it sits nowhere until the block is parsed
    again, so it carries no offsets at all."""
    return Field(text)


class Item:
    """A queue block, PARTITIONED — `head` + `title` + the field chain + `prose`.

    Concatenated in that order the four give the block back byte for byte, which
    is the whole point of storing them rather than describing them:

      head    the marker, the ID slot and the separator `compose_item` writes
              (`- [ ] **T007** — `), or "" for a block whose head is not
              canonical — a legacy item with a different separator, or a line
              that is no item at all.
      title   the item's own free text, up to where the field chain begins.
      fields  the chain, contiguous and ENDING the first line by construction
              (see `chain_matches`), so nothing sits between them or after them.
      prose   everything from the first line's newline on, raw: the continuation
              lines with their own indentation, their hard breaks and their
              nested lists, which no command of this file may touch.
    """

    __slots__ = ("raw", "box", "head", "title", "fields", "prose")

    def __init__(self, raw, box, head, title, fields, prose):
        self.raw = raw
        self.box = box
        self.head = head
        self.title = title
        self.fields = fields
        self.prose = prose

    # --- reading ---------------------------------------------------------

    def field_at(self, offset):
        """The chain field whose segment STARTS at `offset` — how a writer names
        the field a reader located, without the two re-parsing separately.

        A refusal, not a fallback, when the offset names no chain field: a
        writer reaching for a marker OUTSIDE the chain is the destructive bug
        this whole structure exists to make unspellable, and answering it with
        something plausible is how it came back the first time.
        """
        for f in self.fields:
            if f.offsets is not None and f.offsets[0] == offset:
                return f
        raise ValueError(f"no field of this item's chain starts at offset {offset}")

    # --- writing ---------------------------------------------------------

    def stale(self):
        """Every offset this item handed out now names a position it has not
        got. Called by each writer below, immediately."""
        for f in self.fields:
            f.forget_offsets()

    def rstrip_first_line(self, chars=None, pad=""):
        """Take the trailing blanks off whatever piece now ENDS the first line's
        text, and put `pad` back — the field before the gap, or the title, or,
        for an item whose title is blank, the head itself.

        One implementation, because the two callers repair the same joint from
        opposite sides: a field REMOVED leaves the blank that preceded it
        dangling at end of line, and a field APPENDED needs exactly one blank in
        front of it however much or little was there.
        """
        if self.fields:
            self.fields[-1].rewrite(self.fields[-1].text.rstrip(chars) + pad)
        elif self.title.rstrip(chars):
            self.title = self.title.rstrip(chars) + pad
        else:
            self.title = ""
            self.head = self.head.rstrip(chars) + pad

    def set_field(self, field, name, value):
        """Rewrite one segment in place, keeping the blank that separates it from
        the next field: the segment's body is greedy, so the blank is INSIDE the
        text being replaced, and a writer that dropped it glued two fields."""
        trailing = field.text[len(field.text.rstrip()):]
        field.rewrite(f"**{name}:** {value}." + trailing)
        self.stale()

    def drop_field(self, field):
        """Remove one segment, and repair the removal site — the blank BEFORE a
        field that was last on the line is what dangles once it goes.

        Repaired exactly at the site: a sweep over the whole block would also
        eat a two-space Markdown hard break on an unrelated continuation line,
        silently rewrapping text this command was never asked to touch.
        """
        i = self.fields.index(field)
        del self.fields[i]
        if i == len(self.fields):
            self.rstrip_first_line(" \t")
        self.stale()

    def insert_field(self, index, segment):
        """Put a composed segment INTO the chain, with the blank that separates
        it from the one it displaces."""
        self.fields.insert(index, make_field(segment + " "))
        self.stale()

    def append_field(self, segment):
        """Put a composed segment at the END of the first line, where the chain
        lives. Appending after a continuation line writes the field OUTSIDE the
        chain — i.e. produces on the spot the very shape every gate in this file
        refuses to read."""
        self.rstrip_first_line(pad=" ")
        self.fields.append(make_field(segment))
        self.stale()


def chain_segments(line):
    """The item's REAL field chain, as (spelling, start, end) on its first line:
    the maximal run of `**Field:** value.` segments ENDING that line. Empty when
    no such run exists.

    Why a chain, and not "the last marker in the block". An item block absorbs
    its continuation lines, and a legacy item — precisely the population the
    ceiling exemption exists to serve, and the one `ensure_no_embedded_marker`
    never protected, since it only guards items added after it — can carry the
    marker SHAPE in prose: before the fields, or on a note line after them.
    Taking the last match then edits the NOTE and leaves the real field alone,
    while reporting success. Measured on `--risk none`: the real Risk survived
    untouched and the note's text was deleted instead — a no-op on the field the
    command claimed to change, plus destruction of text nobody asked it to touch,
    on the DELETION path.

    The rule was two conditions and is now one, because the other became
    structural:

      anchored   the run must reach the end of the line — the fields are a suffix
                 of the first line, by construction (compose_item). Since
                 `field_segments` runs the last value to the end of the line, no
                 segment can stop short of it, and the condition is a fact rather
                 than a check. It USED to fail on an asterisk, which is how T258
                 knocked an item out of `pack` with no error anywhere.
      periods    each segment's value ends in '.', because compose_item writes
                 `**Field:** value.` — the ONE exception being Source, which is
                 written last and without one. Contiguity cannot stand in for
                 this: the body is greedy, so a prose marker's match runs right
                 up to the next marker and looks contiguous too. The period is
                 what tells `**Project:** para o done-log **Class:** ...` (prose)
                 from `**Project:** tk. **Source:** ...` (a real field).

The period does not separate prose that carries a REAL FIELD NAME in
    bold-colon and ends in a period — `o item cita o **Project:** de outra fila
    inteira. **Class:** …` — from the field it imitates: it is contiguous, it
    ends in a period, so it joins the run. That is why no writer LOCATES a field
    in this chain raw — the POSITION rule (real_fields) is what separates the
    two, and both directions are measured on the shape above: `edit --project tk`
    overwrote those four words and `edit --risk none` DELETED the imitating
    segment whole, each exiting 0 and printing "updated", with no other copy of
    the text anywhere. The way OUT of that ambiguity is the code-span rule (see
    `markers`): quoted, the prose is prose to every reader, which is what lets a
    legacy item be repaired instead of cancelled and re-added.

    Ambiguity is left for the caller to resolve (see cmd_edit), never guessed:
    a refusal costs one command, deleted text is not recoverable.
    """
    segs = field_segments(line)
    if not segs:
        return []
    chain = [segs[-1]]
    for seg in reversed(segs[:-1]):
        ends_field = (line[seg[1]:seg[2]].rstrip().endswith(".")
                      or canonical_field(seg[0]) == "Source")
        if not ends_field:
            break
        chain.insert(0, seg)
    return chain


def parse_item(block):
    """The block read ONCE, into the structure every reader and every writer of
    this file works from. See the note above the class for what that buys."""
    line = block.split("\n", 1)[0]
    chain = chain_segments(line)
    head_m = CANONICAL_HEAD_RE.match(block)
    head = head_m.group(0) if head_m else ""
    box_m = ITEM_ID_RE.match(block)
    return Item(
        raw=block,
        box=box_m.group(1) if box_m else None,
        head=head,
        title=line[len(head):chain[0][1] if chain else len(line)],
        fields=[Field(line[s:e], (s, e)) for _, s, e in chain],
        prose=block[len(line):],
    )


def render_item(item):
    """The block `item` spells — the ONE writer of an item's first line."""
    return item.head + item.title + "".join(f.text for f in item.fields) + item.prose


def field_chain(block):
    """The item's REAL field chain, as `Field`s — one parse, `chain_matches`'s
    rule. Spelled here because every reader in this file asks for the chain, not
    for the whole item, and none of them may build a second parse of its own."""
    return parse_item(block).fields


def clears_field(value):
    """Whether this flag value is the reserved word that DELETES the field.

    Case-insensitive and whitespace-tolerant, so `--risk None` and `--risk ' none '`
    do not silently write a Risk line reading "None" — a Risk field whose content
    is the word for "no risk" is never what the caller meant.
    """
    return (value or "").strip().lower() == FIELD_CLEAR


def clear_field_segment(block, segment):
    """`block` with one field SEGMENT removed, and the removal site repaired.

    ONE implementation, two callers — `edit --<field> none` and `release` — because
    neither half below is obvious, and a second copy is how one caller would keep a
    rule the other had already dropped.

    The field is named by its OFFSET and the item is re-parsed here, rather than
    the caller carrying a structure in: the caller's own `start` holds the item's
    offset in the FILE, and a branch that put the field's own offsets into names
    spelled the same way truncated the frontmatter mid-word and duplicated the
    item — measured on `--risk none`, with the whole suite still green, because
    every clearing test asserted `assertNotIn("**Risk:**")` and a corrupted file
    satisfies that too. Nothing below holds a file offset, and `Item.field_at`
    REFUSES an offset that names no field of the chain instead of repairing
    something else.

    The removal and the repair of its site are `Item.drop_field`'s.
    """
    item = parse_item(block)
    item.drop_field(item.field_at(segment.start()))
    return render_item(item)


# the value HALF of the two whole-block reads. The marker is found by `markers`
# — which is what keeps a quoted one out of both answers — and these two say
# only what a value looks like once one has been found.
CLASS_VALUE_RE = re.compile(r"\s*([A-Za-zÀ-ÿ]+)")


def item_class(block):
    """The class word `list` DISPLAYS, or None. One spelling for the display and
    for the gates wherever the item has a chain to read it from: a class the
    display shows and the gate does not is how the two come to disagree about what
    an item IS.

    Read from the FIELD CHAIN, through `chain_class`. Searching the whole block and
    taking the LEFTMOST match — which is what this did — showed
    `item que fala de **Class:** BLOCKED em prosa, mas nao e **Class:**
    AUTONOMOUS.` as BLOCKED on `list` while `pack` packaged that same item,
    correctly, as AUTONOMOUS. Two readers parting company is worse than either
    being wrong alone: `list` is the screen a human reads before dispatching, and
    nothing on it said the gate had answered differently.

    The block-wide search survives as the FALLBACK, and only for a chain that names
    no class at all — the legacy item whose fields sit on a continuation line.
    `migrate` folds those, but folding is a command a human runs, and unfolded
    items exist in every real queue until they run it; showing all of them with no
    class would be a worse display than the one this replaces. Reading the chain
    ALONE is therefore not the fix, and was measured regressing exactly there.
    """
    if real_fields(block, "Class"):
        return chain_class(block)
    return marker_value(block, "Class", CLASS_VALUE_RE)


def chain_class(block):
    """The class the item really carries, read from its FIELD CHAIN — the stricter
    reader the deferral gate uses, and deliberately not `item_class`.

    `item_class` searches the whole block and takes the leftmost match, which is
    what `list` has always displayed. That is too loose to decide a gate: an item
    whose prose says `nota: itens **Class:** DECISION são raros aqui.` before its
    real `**Class:** AUTONOMOUS.` reads as DECISION, and `edit <id> --deferred x`
    then wrote a deferral onto an AUTONOMOUS item — the very state the flag is
    refused for.

    Returns None when the chain names no class — a legacy item whose fields sit on
    a continuation line — and equally when it names TWO, which is exactly what a
    prose marker absorbed into the chain looks like. Ambiguity is refused, not
    guessed: the same answer `edit`'s own field loop already gives for a duplicated
    field, and the gate then asks for `--class` explicitly, which costs one command.
    """
    found = [f for f in field_chain(block) if f.canonical == "Class"]
    if len(found) != 1:
        return None
    value = re.match(r"\*\*[^*]+:\*\*\s*([A-Za-zÀ-ÿ]+)", found[0].text)
    return value.group(1) if value else None


def real_fields(block, field):
    """The chain segments that really ARE `field` — the POSITION rule, spelled once
    for every reader that locates a field to display or to WRITE.

    The chain begins at the **Class:** compose_item writes first, and a segment
    before it is the item's own prose wearing a field's shape (see field_chain).
    Class qualifies AT that anchor; every other field qualifies after it.

    A chain that names NO class anchors nothing, so NOTHING in it is a real field
    — for a reader or for a writer. Answering with the whole chain instead, which
    is what this did, is the original destructive bug alive on one population: on
    `— item, cita o **Project:** de outra fila inteira. **Criterion:** ok.` there is
    no **Class:** to measure from, the whole chain came back as real, and
    `edit --project tk` OVERWROTE those four words while `edit --risk none` DELETED
    the imitating segment whole — each exiting 0, printing "updated", on text with
    no other copy.

    That fallback was written to keep `edit --class` reaching the very items it
    repairs, and it never had to: **Class:** is not IN the chain of a class-less
    item, so this returns [] for it either way, and `cmd_edit` writes the anchor
    itself — AHEAD of whatever run already ends the line, since T169 (see
    `write_class_segment`). What the fallback bought was only the destruction
    above. The class-less item stays repairable, and its other fields stay
    unreachable until it is repaired — see the refusal in cmd_edit, which says so
    and names what to run.

    `qualified_fields` used to add this rule a second time; it now reads it here,
    because two copies is how one of them keeps a position the other dropped.
    """
    chain = field_chain(block)
    names = [f.canonical for f in chain]
    if "Class" not in names:
        return []
    lo = names.index("Class") + (0 if field == "Class" else 1)
    return [m for i, m in enumerate(chain) if names[i] == field and i >= lo]


def qualified_fields(block, field):
    """(the chain segments of `field` that QUALIFY, how many `field` markers the
    whole block carries) — the position rule every gate reads its field through.

    A real field is a chain segment that FOLLOWS the **Class:** compose_item writes
    first. "Somewhere in the chain" is not enough, and the difference is a bypass
    in one direction and a DELETION in the other: field_chain absorbs any run of
    `**Field:** value.` segments that ends the line, so an item's own prose can
    OPEN that run — `— item, ver a **Deferred:** nota de contexto. **Class:**
    AUTONOMOUS. …` parses as a five-field chain. Both halves are measured, on this
    script, one slice apart:

      the gate     `edit <id> --class DECISION` went through with no justification
                   at all, the gate having read the prose as the field — and the
                   later auto-clear then DELETED that prose from the item's text.
      the deletion `release` on `— ver a **Posse:** do item com alguém. **Class:**
                   …` reported success and cut those four words out of the item's
                   own title, while `claim` had refused the item naming an owner
                   made of three words of that same title.

    The marker COUNT is what keeps the other direction honest: a marker the
    position rule refuses to read is not thereby harmless. The caller is told, and
    nothing is guessed — see deferral_for_edit and claim_segment, which is where
    the two gates part company (one refuses only when the command depends on the
    field; the other always does, since every claim command depends on it).

    The rule itself lives in `real_fields` — including the part this file once
    added here, that a chain with no **Class:** anchors nothing. It moved down
    there when the same missing anchor was measured DESTROYING prose through the
    writer, which reads `real_fields` directly: a rule the gates applied and the
    writer did not is exactly the asymmetry that bug was made of.
    """
    return real_fields(block, field), len(markers(block, field))


def append_to_first_line(block, segment):
    """`block` with `segment` appended at the end of its FIRST line — the only
    place a command may WRITE a field it did not find, because the chain lives
    there. Appending after a continuation line writes the field OUTSIDE the chain,
    i.e. produces on the spot the very shape every gate in this file refuses to
    read.

    One spelling for three callers — the anchor with no chain to sit ahead of, the
    field `edit` did not find, and the claim segment. Three copies of a two-line
    partition is how one of them keeps a rule the other two dropped, and each copy
    already carried its own comment saying this same thing.
    """
    item = parse_item(block)
    item.append_field(segment)
    return render_item(item)


def fields_off_first_line(block):
    """Whether a field marker sits on a CONTINUATION line of `block` — the
    question that decides whether the FOLD has to come before anything else.

    Asked from the regex `fold_chain_onto_first_line` asks it from, and that is
    the point: the fold and the three messages that name it as a remedy must agree
    about which items it is for. A remedy printed for an item the fold does not
    lift is a dead end; one withheld from an item it does lift sends the caller to
    `cancel` + re-add for nothing.
    """
    return bool(markers("\n".join(block.rstrip("\n").split("\n")[1:])))


def write_class_segment(block, value):
    """(`block` with a **Class:** segment, the field names that segment just made
    readable) — the ONE writer of the anchor every other field is measured from.

    POSITION, not text, is what this function is for. `real_fields` measures from
    the **Class:** the chain begins at, so a class written at the END of the line
    leaves every field already on that line BEHIND the anchor, where no gate and
    no report may read it. Measured end to end, and each step exited 0: a legacy
    item whose fields sat on a continuation line went through `migrate`, which
    folds them onto the first line, and then through the `edit <id> --class
    AUTONOMOUS` that `pack` itself prints as the repair — and `pack` went on
    showing that item's Effort as `?` for ever, `list` its age as `?`, with
    nothing anywhere saying why. The class therefore goes AHEAD of the chain the
    item already carries — the position `compose_item` writes it in too, so the
    anchor lands where every reader already looks for it.

    NOT byte-identical to an item `add` would have written, and the difference is
    not cosmetic: `compose_item` stamps a **Born:** this never writes, and it
    normalises the field ORDER, while this preserves the legacy chain's. What the
    two share is the anchor's POSITION, which is the whole of what this decides.

    An item with no chain has nothing to sit ahead of, so its class is appended at
    the end of the first line and becomes the chain.

    The second half of the return is what the caller WARNS about, and it is the
    price of this position. A chain with no anchor can be the item's own prose
    wearing a field's shape — `field_chain` absorbs any run of `**Field:** value.`
    segments that ends the line — and putting the anchor in front of that run
    makes the prose a field. Naming what became readable is what lets the caller
    see it; guessing which segments are prose is the guess this file refuses
    everywhere else. The two silences are not equal, which is why the guess is not
    simply avoided by appending: a field left behind the anchor is invisible for
    ever and to everything, while a promoted one is printed by `list` and by
    `pack` the next time either runs.
    """
    item = parse_item(block)
    segment = f"**Class:** {value}."
    if not item.fields:
        return append_to_first_line(block, segment), []
    promoted = [f.canonical for f in item.fields]
    item.insert_field(0, segment)
    return render_item(item), promoted


def translate_class_value(block):
    """(block with its class value written as the enum, `(before, after)`) —
    (block, None) when there is nothing to translate.

    The one writer of a class value that is not a REPLACEMENT: `edit --class`
    rewrites the whole segment, and doing that here would delete the qualifier a
    legacy value carries — `**Classe:** EXTERNA (João/contrato).` says who the
    item waits on, and that clause is the item's substance, not decoration. Only
    the class WORD changes, matched where `chain_class` reads it, so everything
    the user wrote around it stays byte for byte.

    Read through `chain_class`, so a class named in the item's own prose is not
    what gets translated: that reader takes the chain's single Class field and
    answers None where there are two, which is the ambiguity this file refuses
    everywhere rather than guessing at.
    """
    written = chain_class(block)
    if written is None or written in CLASSES:
        return block, None
    enum = CLASS_ALIASES.get(written.upper())
    if enum is None:
        return block, None
    item = parse_item(block)
    field = next(f for f in item.fields if f.canonical == "Class")
    field.rewrite(re.sub(r"(\*\*[^*]+:\*\*\s*)" + re.escape(written),
                         lambda m: m.group(1) + enum, field.text, count=1))
    return render_item(item), (written, enum)


def chain_breaker(candidate):
    """The field segment that stops the chain from reaching back to **Class:** — the
    one a refusal has to NAME, because it is the one the caller must repair.

    Asked of the CANDIDATE, like the gate itself: the segment that breaks the chain
    is only identifiable once the claim is in place, since appending it is what moves
    the period rule's free pass. Naming "the last field on the line" instead was
    wrong whenever the break sat further left — on `**Class:** AUTONOMOUS, **Effort:**
    M.` the culprit is Class, and the last field is Effort, which is already correct.
    """
    line = candidate.split("\n", 1)[0]
    chain = field_chain(candidate)
    if not chain:
        return None
    before = [seg for seg in field_segments(line) if seg[1] < chain[0].start()]
    return canonical_field(before[-1][0]) if before else None


def claim_readback(candidate):
    """The claim segments a composed block gives BACK — the gate itself.

    Compose, then require the reader to find exactly what was composed. That is the
    only form of this question that is not a guess about the reader: three separate
    fixes asked a proxy for it (does the chain carry a Class?) and the round-1 bug
    survived all three, because appending the claim CHANGES the chain it is asked
    about. An item came back claimed in the file, shown FREE by `list` and impossible
    to release — the exact state the check exists to prevent.
    """
    return qualified_fields(candidate, "Claimed")[0]


def deferral_field(block):
    """(segment, stray) — the item's REAL Deferred field, and whether a
    `**Deferred:**` marker sits anywhere else in the block. The position rule that
    decides "real", and the two incidents that bought it: qualified_fields."""
    segs, markers = qualified_fields(block, "Deferred")
    seg = segs[0] if segs else None
    return seg, markers > (1 if seg else 0)


def deferral_gate_message(cmd):
    """Why the command was refused, and BOTH ways out — a message naming only the
    flag teaches the flag, and the flag is the exception, not the default path."""
    return (
        f"`{cmd} --class {DEFERRABLE_CLASS}` requires --deferred. A {DEFERRABLE_CLASS} "
        "item parks the queue until the user is back, so parking it must be deliberate. "
        "Two paths:\n"
        "  ASK the decision now and write it into the item's text and --criterion — the "
        "item is then AUTONOMOUS and an unattended session can run it (the default path);\n"
        "  or DEFER it on the record: --deferred \"<why it could not be asked>\" "
        "(an afk session passes --deferred afk).")


def deferral_class_message(subject_is):
    """Why a deferral was refused on a class that is not DECISION. One wording for
    `add` and `edit` alike: two sites saying this in two ways is how one of them
    came to be guarded and tested while the other was neither."""
    return (f"--deferred qualifies --class {DEFERRABLE_CLASS} and nothing else — it records "
            f"why a decision could not be asked, and no reader honours it elsewhere. "
            f"{subject_is} Pass `--class {DEFERRABLE_CLASS}` in the same command, or drop "
            "--deferred.")


def validate_env(value):
    """Refuse an `--env` the site file's roster does not carry.

    EXACT equality, and nothing is normalised. The roster is the only thing that
    says an environment exists, so a value outside it is a typo — and a typo
    accepted is worse than one refused: the item is filtered out of every
    package on every machine, forever, with no error anywhere to say why. That
    is the phantom environment the roster exists to prevent, which is why the
    answer here is a refusal and not a warning.

    The reserved clear word is let through untouched: DELETING the field names
    no environment, so it consults no roster — a machine with no site file at
    all must still be able to un-pin an item.
    """
    if value is None or clears_field(value):
        return
    try:
        site = tk_site.load()
    except tk_site.SiteError as e:
        fail(str(e))
    if site is None:
        fail("--env: " + tk_site.missing_file_message())
    if value not in site.environments:
        fail(f"--env {value!r} is not in the roster of {site.path}: "
             f"{', '.join(site.environments)}. The match is exact, capitals included — "
             "a near-miss would pin the item to an environment that does not exist, and "
             "no machine would ever pick it up. Fix the value, or add the environment to "
             "the roster.")


# --- claim: who holds the item, and since when ----------------------------
# A claim is COORDINATION between sibling sessions on one queue — it is not a
# security boundary. That is what decides the two liberties below, and both are
# deliberate: `release` does not demand the owner's name, because a session that
# died holding a claim would otherwise leave the item unreachable (a lock with no
# timeout); and `done`/`cancel` do not consult it either, because they already
# record the outcome in the done-log, and demanding `--as` on every close would
# make a crashed session's item unclosable. What keeps a wrongful release honest
# is that the command PRINTS whose claim it dropped.
#
# An owner is a bounded token, and that bound IS the validation: the value is
# written verbatim into a one-line field right beside a timestamp, so a name
# carrying a blank could not be told apart from the moment it was claimed, and one
# carrying a newline or a `**Field:**` shape would break the block format. The
# shape gate is strictly tighter than the single-line, embedded-marker and field
# ceilings the prose flags answer to — the same answer `--env` gets from the
# roster — so those are not repeated on it. Capitals, dots and underscores are
# allowed where a roster name refuses them: an owner is a session or host label
# ("alpha.local", "sess-3"), not a name matched by equality against a file.
OWNER_RE = re.compile(r"[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]{0,31}\Z")
# ONE spelling of the value's grammar, composed and read back from the same
# constant: two of them is how a refusal comes to name an owner the file never
# carried.
CLAIM_SINCE = " since "
CLAIM_VALUE_RE = re.compile(r"\*\*[^*]+:\*\*\s*(\S+)" + CLAIM_SINCE + r"(\S+?)\.?\s*\Z")


def now_stamp():
    """UTC, to the second.

    Not the date-only stamp the rest of the script writes: a claim is read over
    the interval two sessions collide in, which is minutes, and a field that says
    only "today" answers none of the questions it exists for. UTC because one
    queue is shared by more than one machine by construction — that is what
    **Env:** is for — and two local times from two machines do not compare.
    """
    return datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc).strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ")


def validate_owner(name):
    """argparse's required=True only proves --as was typed; an empty string
    satisfies it and would claim the item for nobody."""
    if not OWNER_RE.match(name or ""):
        fail(f"--as {name!r} is not a valid owner name: it STARTS with a letter or a "
             "digit, and may then carry letters, digits, '.', '-' or '_', at most 32 "
             "chars, no spaces and not empty — so '.local' and '_alpha' are refused for "
             "their first character alone. The name sits beside the timestamp in a "
             "one-line field, and one with a blank in it cannot be told apart from the "
             "moment it was claimed.")
    if clears_field(name):
        fail(f"--as {FIELD_CLEAR!r} is refused: {FIELD_CLEAR!r} is the word that DELETES a "
             "field everywhere in this script, so an item claimed under it would read as "
             "one nobody holds. Hand an item back with `tk-queue release <id>`.")


# The three shapes that keep a reader from finding an item's **Class:** in its
# field chain. Named constants, not bare strings: two callers branch on them.
CLASS_SHAPE_NONE = "no-class"        # no **Class:** marker anywhere in the block
CLASS_SHAPE_OFF_LINE = "off-line"    # one, but not on the first line (a continuation)
CLASS_SHAPE_BROKEN = "broken-chain"  # on the first line, and the chain misses it anyway


def class_shape(block):
    """Which of the three shapes it is — the classification two messages answer
    differently, and the reason it is a function rather than two copies.

    The CONDITION is common to the claim's diagnosis and the package's exclusion
    line; the messages are not — one is a paragraph naming a remedy for a single
    item, the other is one line inside a list. Spelled twice, it cost the mutation
    harness three anchors the day the second copy was written: an anchor is a
    SUBSTRING count, so a second site carrying the same text silently stops the
    entry from proving anything.

    The discriminator is where the MARKER sits, never how far the chain reached.
    "The chain has no Class" is the broader condition, and it also catches an item
    whose fields ARE on the first line and whose Class merely lost its period —
    which then got a message saying its fields sat elsewhere (false) and a remedy
    that rewrote the item and left the same refusal standing.
    """
    if not markers(block, "Class"):
        return CLASS_SHAPE_NONE
    if not markers(block.split("\n", 1)[0], "Class"):
        return CLASS_SHAPE_OFF_LINE
    return CLASS_SHAPE_BROKEN


def claim_unwritable_message(block, candidate):
    """Why the item cannot hold a claim the reader would find, and the remedy that
    actually works for its shape.

    Three shapes, and printing the wrong remedy is a dead end. For an item
    whose fields sit on a CONTINUATION LINE, `edit --class` is itself refused (by
    the marker-outside-the-chain guard), so a message naming it sends the caller
    straight to a second refusal — measured on the real root queue, where 31 of 155
    open items carry that shape. That shape already blocked every per-field `edit`
    before `claim` existed: it is not a limitation this command introduced, and
    folding the fields onto the first line lifts both at once — for 28 of those 31,
    measured. The other three keep a marker and its value on different lines, so the
    fold leaves the field empty; they fall through to the third shape, whose answer
    is `cancel` + re-add, and the message says so rather than promising the fold.

    One shape it does NOT lift, and the message says so rather than sending the
    caller into it: an item whose own TEXT carries a `**Field:**` shape. `edit
    --text` anchors its tail on the first marker ANYWHERE in the block, prose
    included, so on that item it duplicates the prose instead of folding the chain
    (the pre-existing defect this queue already carries as an open item). There the
    answer is `cancel` + re-add.

    The third shape NAMES where the chain stops and refuses to guess WHY. Three
    rewrites tried to diagnose the cause and prescribe a per-field repair, and each
    was wrong for some shape: it read "does not end in a PERIOD" onto a break that
    was a gap of prose, and onto **Source:**, which is period-exempt by design; and
    the `edit --<field>` it prescribed is itself refused when the field is duplicated
    in the chain, when it is a deferral on a non-DECISION item, or when it is an Env
    and the machine has no site file — besides repairing only ONE broken field per
    run. The space of malformed chains is larger than any branch table for it, so the
    message states the fact it can prove and gives the remedy that always works.

    The item is named by its OWN spelling (item_label), because this message HANDS
    THE CALLER A COMMAND: a remedy addressed to `T001` while the item is `T0001`
    is a paste that acts on a different item, and the one it prints replaces
    everything between the head and the first field.
    """
    label = item_label(block)
    head = (f"{label} cannot hold a claim the reader would find: a claim is the chain "
            "segment written after **Class:**, and on this item it would not land there. "
            "Nothing was changed — written anyway, the item is held in the file, shown "
            "FREE by `list` and impossible to release. ")
    shape = class_shape(block)
    if shape == CLASS_SHAPE_NONE:
        if fields_off_first_line(block):
            # the class alone is REFUSED on this item (cmd_edit), and it would be
            # the wrong first command even if it were not: the anchor appended to a
            # chain-less first line strands the fields below it for good
            return head + (
                "It has no class, and its fields sit OUTSIDE the first line, so the "
                "class cannot come first: appended to a line with no chain on it, the "
                "anchor leaves those fields unreadable for good and stops `migrate` "
                "from folding them. `tk-queue migrate` folds them up, every item "
                "carrying this shape in one pass, and names the ones it could not "
                f"fold; then `tk-queue edit {label} --class AUTONOMOUS`. If the fold "
                "does not lift this — three real items keep a marker and its value on "
                "different lines — close the item with `cancel` and re-add it clean.")
        return head + f"Give it a class first: `tk-queue edit {label} --class AUTONOMOUS`."
    if shape == CLASS_SHAPE_OFF_LINE:
        return head + (
            "Its fields sit OUTSIDE the first line, which already blocks every per-field "
            f"`edit` on this item (it did before `claim` existed). `tk-queue migrate` folds "
            "them onto the first line for you, every item carrying this shape in one pass, "
            "and names the ones it could not fold. Per item, the same fold is "
            f"`tk-queue edit {label} --text \"<the item's whole text>\"` — read the "
            "item first, because --text replaces everything between the head and the first "
            "field, continuation prose included. If the item's own text carries a "
            "`**Field:**` shape, --text duplicates it instead of folding. If the fold "
            "does not lift this refusal — three real items keep a marker and its value on "
            "different lines — close the item with `cancel` and re-add it clean.")
    return head + (f"The unbroken run of fields ending that line stops at "
                   f"**{chain_breaker(candidate)}:** and never reaches **Class:**, so a "
                   "claim cannot be positioned after it. The usual causes are a field "
                   "value that does not end in a PERIOD — the period is what tells a field "
                   "from prose — and prose sitting between two fields. Which one it is, "
                   "this message does not guess: close the item with `cancel` and re-add it "
                   "clean. No per-field `edit` repairs this reliably; several are refused "
                   "on the very field at fault.")


def claim_segment(block):
    """The item's REAL **Claimed:** field — the one in its FIELD CHAIN — or None.

    Read through qualified_fields, so a marker sitting BEFORE the **Class:** — the
    item's own prose, absorbed into the chain because it happens to end in a period
    — is not the claim. That is not a hypothetical: `release` on such an item cut
    four words out of its title and reported success.

    Refuses rather than guessing, in both directions, because this reader decides a
    GATE and the deferral field had already paid for both lessons:

      ambiguous   two qualifying Claimed fields: which one holds the item is
                  unanswerable, and picking either lets the other be overwritten.
      stray       a `**Claimed:**` marker anywhere the position rule will not read
                  — the item's own prose, or a continuation line. Reading it as the
                  field would let prose hold an item forever; ignoring it would have
                  `release` report a free item while that text still says otherwise.

    Unlike `edit`, this refuses UNCONDITIONALLY rather than only when the command
    depends on the field: `claim` and `release` are commands about the claim and
    nothing else, so there is no unrelated edit here to over-refuse.

    Both populations are legacy only: ensure_no_embedded_marker refuses the marker
    shape in every free-text flag, and Claimed is a field it knows.
    """
    label = item_label(block)
    segs, markers = qualified_fields(block, "Claimed")
    if len(segs) > 1:
        fail(f"{label} carries {len(segs)} **Claimed:** fields in its field chain, so "
             "who holds it is ambiguous. Nothing was changed. Close it with `cancel` and "
             "re-add it clean.")
    seg = segs[0] if segs else None
    if markers > (1 if seg else 0):
        fail(f"{label} carries a **Claimed:** marker away from the position a claim is "
             "written in (after **Class:**) — in the item's own prose, or on a continuation "
             "line. Nothing was changed: reading it as the claim would let prose hold the "
             "item forever, and ignoring it would report a free item while that text says "
             "otherwise. Close it with `cancel` and re-add it clean.")
    return seg


def claim_value(segment):
    """(owner, since) carried by a Claimed segment.

    A value that does not parse comes back as (the raw value, None) and NEVER as
    "unclaimed": "held, and I cannot tell by whom" is still held, and reading it as
    free is exactly how the second session takes an item the first is working.
    """
    m = CLAIM_VALUE_RE.match(segment.text)
    if m:
        return m.group(1), m.group(2)
    return segment.value.strip(), None


def claim_mark(owner, since):
    """One wording for the refusal, the release report and `list` alike."""
    return f"claimed by {owner}" + (f" since {since}" if since
                                    else " (with no readable timestamp)")


def displayed_claim(block):
    """The claim `list` shows, or None — read from the FIELD CHAIN, the same place
    the gate reads it from, so the display and the refusal cannot disagree about
    who holds an item.

    Tolerant where the gate refuses, and that is the difference between a reader
    and a gate: `list` must not die on one malformed item and stop showing the
    other twenty. What the two DO agree on is the only thing that matters here —
    the POSITION rule, which is not relaxed: a display reading the item's own prose
    as a claim would show an owner made of three words of the title.

    Where they part is the STRAY marker, and there only in the safe direction: an
    item carrying a real claim AND a marker elsewhere is displayed as claimed —
    which is true, somebody holds it — while the commands refuse it over the marker,
    a defect in the item rather than an answer about who holds it. A reader who
    skips it loses nothing, and one who tries to take it is refused.

    Ambiguity is NOT tolerated the same way, because there the tolerant answer is
    the dangerous one: two qualifying claims mean two owners are on the record, and
    printing no mark would show the item FREE — to a reader, and to the afk package
    that is built from exactly this display.
    """
    segs, _ = qualified_fields(block, "Claimed")
    if len(segs) == 1:
        return claim_mark(*claim_value(segs[0]))
    return "claimed ambiguously — `tk-queue claim` says why" if segs else None


# A forge reference, `<repo>#<n>` — the shape of both provenance fields. Bounded
# by construction on both halves (GitHub caps a repo name at 100 chars; nine
# digits is more issues than any repo this queue serves will ever hold), so the
# gate is strictly tighter than the single-line, embedded-marker and prose-field
# ceilings the free-text flags answer to: a newline, a blank and a `**Field:**`
# shape are all unspellable inside it. That is the same answer `--env` gets from
# the roster and `--as` from OWNER_RE, and it is why these two flags are not
# added to check_field_ceilings — a second, looser bound on a value this already
# decides would only be able to disagree with it.
#
# `owner/repo#n` is refused along with the rest. The queue's tickets live in the
# repos one account owns, the ticket writes `<repo>#<n>`, and admitting a second
# spelling of the same reference is how the lane comes to read two names for one
# spec and open two branches for it.
REF_RE = re.compile(r"[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]{0,99}#[0-9]{1,9}")


def canonical_ref(value):
    """The one spelling of a forge reference this queue stores and compares.

    Two spellings of one reference is the mechanism this slice's review kept
    finding a new door into, and equality is where it bites: `pack_lanes` counts
    tickets per spec by raw string equality, so `Ambiente#171` and
    `ambiente#171` — measured — were two specs of one ticket each, both under
    the floor, both dispatched `avulso`, two branches and two campaigns for one
    spec. And `ambiente#0171` beside `ambiente#171` produced the other symptom:
    a reason naming the item's own spec as the one occupying the lane.

    Both halves are canonicalised, and neither is a guess about the forge:

      repo    lower-cased. A forge repo name is case-insensitive to look up and
              case-preserving to display, so two casings ARE one repo — the one
              thing a queue comparing them by `==` cannot know.
      number  through int(), which drops leading zeros. `#0171` and `#171` are
              one issue; `#0171` is not even a form the forge's own `#N` syntax
              resolves.

    Applied at the WRITER, so the file only ever holds the canonical form, and
    again at the reader, because a hand edit is not the writer.

    PRECONDITION: `value` has already passed `REF_RE.fullmatch`. Both callers gate
    on it in the statement before, and this is written to that contract — `int()`
    raises on anything else, and a reader that raises is worse than one that
    refuses. Do not call it on an unvalidated value."""
    repo, _, number = value.rpartition("#")
    return f"{repo.lower()}#{int(number)}"


def validate_ref(flag, value):
    """Refuse a provenance value that is not a forge reference, and return the
    one spelling of the one that passes (see canonical_ref).

    A typo here is silent everywhere it matters: `--ticket` feeds the `closes`
    line of a PR, so a malformed one closes nothing and the ticket stays open
    with the work merged; `--spec` decides the lane, so a malformed one puts the
    ticket in a lane of its own — a second branch and a second campaign for a
    spec that already has both. Neither failure reports itself, which is what
    makes the shape a gate rather than a warning.
    """
    if value is None:
        return None
    if not REF_RE.fullmatch(value):
        fail(f"--{flag} {value!r} is not a forge reference: the shape is `<repo>#<n>` "
             "(e.g. ambiente#172) — the repo's own name, no owner prefix, "
             "and the issue number after '#'. The match is exact: a near-miss feeds a "
             "`closes` line that closes nothing, or names a lane after an issue that "
             "does not exist.")
    return canonical_ref(value)


# The repository an item's code LANDS in — the address `**Repo:**` carries, and a
# different question from the two forge references above. What the shape refuses is
# the whole reason the field exists: a value that resolves against a CWD.
#
#   remote name     `origin`, `upstream`. Measured (#198): `git ls-remote origin`
#                   run from the queue's directory — a clone of another repo —
#                   exits 0 and prints nothing, which reads exactly like "the
#                   branch is free" and admits an item over live work.
#   relative path   `../claude-skills`, `tk`. The same failure with a different
#                   spelling, and the one a rule about "remote names" alone
#                   would have let through.
#
# So the accepted shapes are the cwd-independent ones, and they are the shapes
# this account's repositories are actually addressed by: a URL with a scheme, the
# scp-like `git@host:path` git also takes, a POSIX absolute path, and a Windows
# drive-letter path, which is absolute for the same reason and is how the desktop
# in this site's roster names a clone.
#
# The repository an item's code LANDS in, and what may be spelled as one. This gate
# is a WHITELIST — it names the shapes this account addresses repositories by, and
# refuses everything else — and it is a whitelist because four rounds of review
# proved the other design cannot be finished. Each round found a new value that was
# shape-legal and meant something else to whoever received it:
#
#   `../claude-skills`   resolves against the reader's cwd, which is the queue's own
#                        clone. `git ls-remote origin` run there exits 0 with no
#                        output, reading exactly like "the branch is free" (#198).
#   `/a]injetado`        splices a second bracket group into the line `pack` prints,
#                        the way a hand-edited `**Ticket:** repo#1] injetado` did.
#   `--upload-pack=<c>@h:p`  git 2.39.5, in a clone with an `origin`, read this as
#                        the OPTION, fell back to that origin and EXECUTED <c>.
#   `https://u:tok@h/r`  a password, into a file with no other copy, reprinted by
#                        `pack` on every package — and `%3A` for the same thing.
#   `https://ghp_x@h/r`  the same secret with no colon in it at all.
#   `…/r‌.git`            a zero-width character: two addresses one reader cannot
#                        tell apart, on a value whose job is to be read and pasted.
#
# Six instances of ONE mechanism, and a seventh was always next. So the question
# stopped being "which value do we refuse" and became "which do we accept":
#
#   https://<host>[:port]/<path>       the forge over HTTP, with NO userinfo. A
#   http://<host>[:port]/<path>        credential belongs in a credential helper,
#                                      never in a queue file — and refusing the
#                                      whole `<user>@` half is what makes that true
#                                      of a token, a password, and an encoding of
#                                      either, without this file learning to
#                                      recognise any of them.
#   ssh://git@<host>[:port]/<path>     the SSH forms, where `git@` is a fixed
#   git@<host>:<path>                  protocol name and not a secret.
#   file:///<path>                     a local repository, addressed as a URL.
#   /<path>   C:\<path>              a local repository, addressed as a path.
#                                      `~/<path>` is NOT one, and it is the
#                                      instructive refusal: HOME differs between
#                                      machines and between users, so a tilde
#                                      address means a different repository to a
#                                      different reader — the same defect as
#                                      `origin`, wearing a path's clothes. The two
#                                      consumers do not even agree with each other:
#                                      `git ls-remote` expands the tilde itself and
#                                      answers, while `git -C "~/x"` is a plain
#                                      chdir that fails — so the branch check passes
#                                      and the worktree dies three steps later.
#                                      Measured on git 2.39.5.
#
# A URL's path is ASCII: letters, digits and `. _ - /`. That excludes `%`, so a
# percent-encoding cannot smuggle back a character refused above, and it excludes
# `[`, `]`, `*` and every invisible character without naming them. A LOCAL path is
# wider — it is not handed to a transport and carries no credential — but it is
# still refused the four characters that break a reader: whitespace (the chain is
# one line, and `pack`'s columns are runs of blanks), `*` (a field marker would
# become spellable), `[]` (the bracket groups), and the INVISIBLE characters, which
# change what a reader sees without changing what git receives.
#
# What this refuses AND SHOULD: an IPv6 literal host (`[::1]`, brackets), a
# percent-encoded URL path, a colon inside a path, a userinfo other than `git@`.
# Each is a real address somewhere, and none is one this account uses. The
# asymmetry is deliberate: a refusal prints a message naming the shapes and is
# repaired by widening one alternative, while an admission is silent and is
# repaired by a review campaign.
#
# A leading `-` needs no rule of its own any more: no alternative below can begin
# with one. The test that measured it stays, because it is what would catch a
# widening that let one back in.
#
# A trailing '.' is refused, and that is round-trip and not taste: compose_item
# writes `**Repo:** <value>.` and field_value strips ONE trailing period, so a
# value ending in one comes back a character short of what was written.
REPO_INVISIBLE = "\u00ad\u061c\u180e\u200b-\u200f\u202a-\u202e\u2060-\u2069\ufeff"
# a host, and the PORT SEPARATELY — because only a URL has a field for one. Spelled
# into the scp-like branch, `git@host:2222:owner/repo.git` reads to a person as a
# host on a non-standard port and to git as the DEFAULT port and the path
# `2222:owner/repo.git`: measured under GIT_TRACE, which showed
# `ssh git@myserver 'git-upload-pack '2222:owner/repo.git''`. That is the silent
# misreading this whitelist exists to end, so the port goes where it can be honoured.
# The scp-like branch refuses the OTHER spelling of the same mistake itself: a first
# path segment that is all digits, `git@host:2222/owner/repo.git`, which git folds
# into the path exactly the same way — measured the same way, one round later.
REPO_HOST = r"[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9.-]*"
REPO_PORT = r"(?::[0-9]{1,5})?"
REPO_URL_PATH = r"(?:/[A-Za-z0-9._-]+)+"
# a local path's characters: everything a reader can see and no reader can be
# fooled by — see the four exclusions above
REPO_LOCAL = "[^\\s*\\[\\]" + REPO_INVISIBLE + "]+"
# NO `.` OR `..` SEGMENT, anywhere, under either separator. The gate cannot
# canonicalise an address — it hands the value to git untouched, and dropping a
# `.git` or a trailing slash would be this file guessing at a forge's routing — so
# the one thing it can do about two spellings of one repository is refuse the
# second. `/a/./b` and `/a/../b` name the path `/a/b` and read as two addresses to
# every reader here, which is what AFK.md's "one lane, one address" then cannot
# decide. It asks only about a segment BETWEEN separators: one at the END of an
# address ends in `.`, which the trailing-dot rule below already refuses, and a
# second question about it was dead — the mutation harness proved that, finding no
# test able to tell the two spellings of this rule apart.
# A trailing SEPARATOR is refused by the same principle — a URL copied from a
# browser's address bar carries one, and `/srv/r/` beside `/srv/r` is the same two
# spellings again. The URL branches refuse it by their segments having to be
# non-empty; the local ones cannot, because a local path is one character class and
# `/` is in it, so the closing rule below asks for both at once.
#
# That rule also SUBSUMES the non-empty tail every alternative already required:
# `/` and `C:/` are not addresses, and a value with an empty tail ends in its own
# separator, so each is now refused twice. Neither rule can be mutated into view on
# its own any more, and the harness says so by having no mutant for the tail — the
# redundancy is left standing rather than thinned, because it is one character and
# it guards the shape that is easiest of all to typo.
REPO_NO_DOT_SEG = r"(?![^\n]*[/\\]\.{1,2}[/\\])"
REPO_RE = re.compile(
    REPO_NO_DOT_SEG +
    "(?:"
    "https?://" + REPO_HOST + REPO_PORT + REPO_URL_PATH +      # the forge over HTTP
    "|ssh://git@" + REPO_HOST + REPO_PORT + REPO_URL_PATH +    # SSH, spelled as a URL
    "|git@" + REPO_HOST + r":/?(?![0-9]+/)[A-Za-z0-9._-]+" + r"(?:/[A-Za-z0-9._-]+)*"  # SSH, scp-like
    "|file:///" + REPO_LOCAL +                                 # a local repo, as a URL
    "|/" + REPO_LOCAL +                                        # a POSIX absolute path
    "|[A-Za-z]:[\\\\/]" + REPO_LOCAL +                         # a Windows drive-letter path
    ")"
    r"(?<![./\\])"
)


def validate_repo(value):
    """Refuse anything that is not one of the addresses this queue accepts, and
    return the one that passes UNCHANGED.

    A WHITELIST, and REPO_RE carries the reasoning: the shapes are named, and
    everything else is refused whether or not anyone has thought of it. Four review
    rounds of refusing values one class at a time is what bought that inversion.

    Unchanged is deliberate: nothing in this script compares two repositories, so
    there is no equality for a canonical spelling to serve — and a normaliser
    (dropping a `.git`, a trailing slash) would be this script guessing at a
    forge's routing rules on the one value it hands straight to git.

    The LENGTH is not bounded by this shape, unlike a forge reference, because a
    path has no length the world gives it — so `--repo` answers to the ordinary
    field ceiling in check_field_ceilings, where the free-text flags answer.
    """
    if value is None:
        return None
    if not REPO_RE.fullmatch(value):
        fail(f"--repo {value!r} is not one of the repository addresses this queue "
             "accepts, which are: https://<host>/<path>, ssh://git@<host>/<path>, "
             "git@<host>:<path>, file:///<path>, or an ABSOLUTE path "
             "(/srv/projects/config, /root/.claude/skills, C:\\clones\\repo — "
             "a `~/` address is refused: HOME differs per machine and per reader). "
             "No trailing '.', and no `<user>@` half on an http(s) URL — a token or a "
             "password would be stored in this file and reprinted by `pack` on every "
             "package, and a credential helper is where it belongs. A remote NAME like "
             "`origin`, or a relative path, is refused because it resolves against "
             "whatever directory the reader is in — which for this queue's readers is "
             "the queue's own clone, where the check comes back clean over live work. "
             "The list is a whitelist: an address that is real and simply not on it is "
             "one line to add, and the reason to add it goes in the comment there.")
    return value


def validate_project(tag):
    if not PROJECT_RE.match(tag):
        fail(f"--project must be a short lowercase slug (letters, digits, '-'/'_', "
             f"no spaces or dots): {tag!r}")


def open_project_tags(content):
    """Project tags carried by currently-open items of a next-steps.md body."""
    tags = set()
    for kind, text in split_blocks(content or ""):
        if kind != "item-open":
            continue
        tag = marker_value(text, "Project", PROJECT_TAG_VALUE_RE)
        if tag:
            tags.add(tag)
    return tags


def group_by_tag(pairs, by_position=False):
    """(tag, payload) pairs → ordered [(tag, [payload, ...])], the untagged group
    LAST — that group is a residue, not a project. Returns None when nothing
    carries a tag, so the caller prints its flat form and an untagged queue looks
    exactly as it did before tags existed.

    `list` and `report` group on different things (open items vs done-log
    entries) under different heading levels, but the ordering rule is one rule
    and belongs in one place. Where they part is WHICH order the groups come in,
    and each follows the axis its file is ordered by:

      by_position   `list`. The queue's order IS its priority, so the groups
                    follow the file: each one sits where its top item sits.
                    Sorting them alphabetically instead made `bump` INVISIBLE on
                    any tagged queue — the item reached the top of the file and
                    still rendered under the third heading.
      sorted        `report`. A done-log has no priority to reflect; alphabetical
                    is the stable reading order there.
    """
    if not any(tag for tag, _ in pairs):
        return None
    groups = {}
    for tag, payload in pairs:
        groups.setdefault(tag, []).append(payload)
    named = [k for k in groups if k is not None]      # dict order == file order
    order = (named if by_position else sorted(named)) + ([None] if None in groups else [])
    return [(tag, groups[tag]) for tag in order]


def one_line(raw, limit):
    """`raw` as a single display line: the head's leftovers off the front, the
    whitespace collapsed, the decoration trimmed, the length capped.

    The head strip is not dead code for a canonical item — it is what a LEGACY
    head needs. `CANONICAL_HEAD_RE` requires the ` — ` compose_item writes, so an
    item whose ID is followed by anything else parses with no head at all and
    arrives here still carrying `- [ ] **T007**`. ID_RE runs over the whole
    string for the same reason it always did: `**~~T012~~**` is an ID to the
    allocator, and leaving the fragment in leaked `T012~~**` into a title.
    """
    t = re.sub("^" + MARKER, "", raw.strip())
    t = ID_RE.sub("", t)
    t = re.sub(r"\s+", " ", t).strip(" —-~*")
    return t if len(t) <= limit else t[: limit - 1] + "…"


def item_title(text, limit=100):
    """The item's title as `list` and `pack` COLUMN it — the first line, up to the
    field chain, from the one parse.

    Two defects lived in the reading this replaces, and both were measured on the
    display of a real shape:

      truncated   it cut at the first READABLE marker instead of at the CHAIN, so
                  an item whose own prose says `o item cita **Project:** de outra
                  fila e segue a frase.` was columned as `o item cita` — the
                  user's sentence cut off mid-word, in both readers, with nothing
                  saying anything had been cut. The code-span rule narrowed that
                  to the UNQUOTED marker and could not close it: quoting is a
                  repair of the file, and the display has to be right on the file
                  as it stands. `Item.title` already ends where the chain begins,
                  which is the only boundary that is not a guess.
      absorbed    with no marker anywhere to cut at, the whitespace collapse ran
                  over the item's CONTINUATION lines and pulled them into the
                  title, so a note the author gave its
                  own line was columned as part of the sentence above it. A title
                  is one line by construction now, because `Item.title` is a slice
                  of the first line and nothing else.

    Where the title ENDS is the POSITION rule, not the raw chain: `Item.title`
    stops at the first segment of the run, and a segment ahead of the **Class:**
    anchor is the item's own prose wearing a field name (real_fields). Stopping
    there put the cut back one marker earlier, on exactly the population the two
    rules above are about. So every segment before the anchor is title again.

    Where the two rules DISAGREE, said rather than left for the next reader to
    measure: a chain that names no class has no anchor, so `real_fields` reads
    none of it as a field — and the title still stops where the chain begins, one
    marker earlier than the position rule alone would put it. That is a choice and
    not an oversight. Nothing there tells the legacy item whose writer put real
    fields on the line and never a class — `— a legacy item **Effort:** S.
    **Criterion:** A: x.`, the shape PACK_SAMPLE documents — from the item whose
    sentence merely quotes a marker, and the parse's own partition is the boundary
    that needs no guess between them. The reader is not left guessing either way:
    `list` prints the class as `?` and `pack` excludes the item naming the missing
    field. Measured over the 19 real queues of 2026-09-06: 271 open items, 21 with
    no class, and NOT ONE of those carrying a chain at all — so the disagreement
    has no population today, and the cost of reading it the other way is the
    legacy line above, columned with its fields inside the title.

    `item_text` is the other projection, for the two callers that must have the
    prose too. Both read the SAME parse: the display and the remedy disagreeing
    about what an item says is the class of defect this pair exists to end.
    """
    item = parse_item(text)
    names = [f.canonical for f in item.fields]
    lead = ("".join(f.text for f in item.fields[: names.index("Class")])
            if "Class" in names else "")
    return one_line(item.title + lead, limit)


def item_text(block, limit=100):
    """The item's WHOLE text — title and continuation prose — as one line, for the
    two callers that rewrite or archive the item rather than column it.

    `edit --text` REPLACES the item's text, so the remedy `handoff` prints has to
    carry every line of it: handed `item_title`'s first line, that remedy would
    run, report success and delete the author's note. The done-log line is the
    item's only surviving record, and the same cut would drop the same prose out
    of it.

    It still stops at the first readable marker, and that is deliberate rather
    than inherited: `ensure_no_embedded_marker` REFUSES a `--text` carrying one,
    so a remedy that included it would be refused by the very command it prints.
    Truncating there is the known dead end T134 names; printing a command that
    cannot run is not better, and choosing between them is not this item's work.
    """
    item = parse_item(block)
    t = item.title + item.prose
    found = markers(t)
    return one_line(t[: found[0][1]] if found else t, limit)


# --- done-log writing -----------------------------------------------------

def log_entry_tag(entry_text):
    """Project tag of a done-log entry, read from its first line only — never
    from the indented --note continuation lines (see LOG_TAG_RE)."""
    m = LOG_TAG_RE.search(entry_text.split("\n", 1)[0])
    return m.group(1) if m else None


def interrupted_close(log, iid=None, moved=None):
    """Whether this item's done-log half is ALREADY written — the queue half of
    a close that a crash interrupted.

    Both closing paths write the log FIRST and the queue second, through two
    separate `write_atomic` calls with nothing spanning them. `reference/queue.md`
    accepts that window on purpose: a crash between the two writes "can duplicate
    a line but never lose the item". Duplicating is what the rerun then does, in
    silence, and the log is the only record the item leaves — so the rerun is
    recognised on the way in, finishes the queue half, and writes no second line.
    Inverting the order instead (queue first) closes the window by trading it for
    a worse one: a crash there LOSES the item.

    Each caller asks by the position IT writes, and the two are not one shape:

    - `done` and `cancel` write an ENTRY, so they ask by `iid`, read through
      LOG_ID_RE — the ID column of an entry, and nothing else. `done_log_ids` is
      the wrong question here: it also sees a legacy `- [ ] **T005**` parked in
      the log, a deliberate tolerance that stops an ID being handed out twice,
      and reading it HERE invents an interrupted close for an item whose entry
      was never written. That close then leaves the queue with no record at all —
      losing the item, which is the outcome the accepted window never causes.
    - `migrate` moves the block VERBATIM, with no entry head, so there is no ID
      column for LOG_ID_RE to find (measured: the second run duplicated every
      moved line and re-inserted it ABOVE the first copy, so the log's order
      stopped being its history). It asks by the block's own TEXT, anchored at
      both ends to a line boundary — a bare substring test would read a block
      whose text merely OPENS a longer line already in the log as already moved,
      and that is the losing direction again.
    """
    if iid is not None:
        return iid in ids_at(log, LOG_ID_RE)
    return ("\n" + moved.strip("\n") + "\n") in ("\n" + log + "\n")


def append_done_line(memdir, content, line, today):
    """`content` is the done-log the caller already read under the lock — the
    same read `interrupted_close` was asked of. Re-reading here would answer the
    two halves of one decision from two snapshots of one file."""
    path = os.path.join(memdir, "done-log.md")
    month_header = f"## {today[:7]}"
    if month_header not in content:
        content = content.rstrip("\n") + f"\n\n{month_header}\n"
    # insert right after the month header so newest month stays where created
    idx = content.index(month_header) + len(month_header)
    nl = content.find("\n", idx)
    if nl == -1:  # header is the last line, no trailing newline
        content += "\n"
        nl = len(content) - 1
    content = content[: nl + 1] + line.rstrip("\n") + "\n" + content[nl + 1 :]
    write_atomic(path, content)


def excise(content, block, idx):
    """Remove the block AT `idx`, collapsing surplus blank lines ONLY at the
    removal site — spacing elsewhere in the file is not ours to touch.

    The offset is passed in, never re-derived with `content.index(block)`: an
    item's text is NOT unique in the file. An item that documents the queue's own
    format quotes another item's line verbatim in its note, and the substring
    search finds THAT copy first — measured on this script: `done T002` deleted
    the quoted copy out of T001's note and left the real T002 open, reporting
    success, and `bump T002` produced a phantom duplicate. Positions come from
    find_open_item, which walks the blocks.
    """
    before, after = content[:idx], content[idx + len(block):]
    if not after.strip():
        return before.rstrip("\n") + "\n"
    nb = len(before) - len(before.rstrip("\n"))
    na = len(after) - len(after.lstrip("\n"))
    if nb + na > 2:
        return before.rstrip("\n") + "\n\n" + after.lstrip("\n")
    return before + after


def remove_block(memdir, content, block, start):
    """`content` is the read find_open_item already made under the lock — the
    authoritative one. Re-reading here would only open a window for the file to
    have changed between the two reads, and re-finding the block by text is the
    bug excise() documents."""
    write_atomic(os.path.join(memdir, "next-steps.md"), excise(content, block, start))


def id_in_done_log(memdir, wanted_id, log=None):
    """Whether the ID really was closed — asked of the positions a close writes
    (see done_log_ids), never of the log's free text.

    `log` is the done-log's text when the caller already read it, and `memdir`
    is what answers for the caller that did not — the sibling bins ask this
    question holding a directory alone.

    Anywhere else the ID is prose. This queue's own log carries a --note saying
    "atribuiu IDs até T022" and an outcome naming a sibling ticket "T054" that
    is still open in another project — a whole-file search reads both as closed
    and answers "already left the queue", a confident wrong diagnosis from the
    error path that exists to prevent confident wrong diagnoses.
    """
    return wanted_id in done_log_ids(memdir, log)


def missing_item_message(memdir, content, wanted_id):
    """Why the ID is not among the open items — the four cases are NOT the same
    answer, and a flat "no open item" is the one that does damage: it reads as
    "never existed", so the caller adds the item again and the queue ends up
    with two. Every branch below says what to do instead of re-adding.

    ONE snapshot of each file answers all four. `content` is the queue the caller
    already walked, and the done-log is read once here and handed to both readers
    that ask about it — the rule `done_log_ids` spells out, and the reason this
    path reads neither file a second time for itself.
    """
    label = f"T{wanted_id:03d}"
    log = read_done_log(memdir)
    for kind, text in split_blocks(content):
        if kind == "item-done" and item_id(text) == wanted_id:
            # THIS branch found the item, so it is named by its own spelling —
            # `migrate` moves a ticked line VERBATIM, so the log will carry that
            # spelling and no other, and "read the log line" has to be greppable
            return (f"{item_label(text)} is in next-steps.md but already ticked [x], "
                    "so it never reached the done-log. Run `tk-queue migrate` to move "
                    "it, then read the log line — do NOT add a replacement item.")
    if id_in_done_log(memdir, wanted_id, log):
        return (f"{label} already left the queue — it is in done-log.md. Read that line "
                "before acting, and do NOT add a replacement item.")
    top = max_id(memdir, content, log)
    if wanted_id > top:
        return (f"{label} was never allocated in {memdir} — the highest ID in use is "
                f"T{top:03d}. Check the ID with `tk-queue list`.")
    return (f"{label} is allocated but appears in neither next-steps.md nor done-log.md. "
            "Another writer very likely removed or clobbered it. Nothing was changed. "
            "Re-read the queue with `tk-queue list` before acting — do NOT add a "
            "replacement item, that is exactly how a duplicate is born.")


# One number, more than one open item. Two ways in, one answer: a genuine
# duplicate (`T005` written twice) and a width collision (`T001` and `T0001`,
# which `int()` reads as the same number) are the SAME failure — two items at
# one address, and only the first reachable. Resolution stays numeric, because
# `done 5`, `done T005` and `done 005` are documented as one call (see
# ID_INPUT_RE); what changes is that the run no longer pretends the second item
# is not there. `list` marks both rows, every command that resolves an ID names
# the occurrence it acted on, and the repair is a human renumbering one of them.
AMBIGUOUS_MARK = "duplicate ID %s"
# the sentence that says what the mark MEANS, spelled once for the two readers
# that print it. `pack` carried neither: a package listing two rows under one
# label said nothing about only the first being reachable, so a caller who
# dispatched the second one addressed the first.
AMBIGUOUS_NOTE = ("duplicate IDs: only the FIRST item under each is reachable — "
                  "renumber the others by hand in next-steps.md.")


def ambiguous_ids(ids):
    """The IDs that more than one OPEN item carries, from the ids in queue order.

    One spelling for the two readers that mark them. `list` computed it and the
    package did not compute it at all — which is how a package listing came to
    show two rows under one label with nothing saying only the first was
    reachable — and two copies of the count is how one of them would come back to
    marking a row the other leaves plain.

    The ID, never the label: `T001` and `T0001` are two spellings of one number,
    so a set of labels would not see the collision at all.
    """
    return {i for i in ids if i is not None and ids.count(i) > 1}


def ambiguous_id_message(wanted_id, blocks):
    """One wording for every command that resolves an ID, so the warning cannot
    drift from `list`'s mark."""
    spellings = ", ".join(item_label(t) for t in blocks)
    return (f"tk-queue: warning: {AMBIGUOUS_MARK % wanted_id} — {len(blocks)} open items "
            f"carry it ({spellings}). Acting on the FIRST, {item_label(blocks[0])} "
            f"\"{item_title(blocks[0])}\"; the rest stay unreachable until one of them "
            "is renumbered by hand in next-steps.md.")


def find_open_item(memdir, wanted_id):
    """(content, block, start) — the file, the item's block, and WHERE it starts.

    Called under queue_lock by every mutating command, so THIS read is the
    authoritative one: no writer can have moved the item since it was taken, and
    every caller writes from this same `content`.

    The offset is what makes the item addressable. Its TEXT is not: split_blocks
    walks the file in order, so the position is exact, while searching the text
    back out finds whichever copy comes first — including one quoted inside
    another item's note (see excise).

    The block is also where the item's NAME comes from: every caller that puts
    this item in a message or a record reads it off the returned block with
    `item_label`, never by re-rendering the number it passed in. The number is
    the ADDRESS (`T0001` and `T001` are one address), the label is the item's own
    spelling — and a message that respells the address names a DIFFERENT item.

    An ID carried by MORE THAN ONE open item is not refused: refusing would
    freeze the whole queue over two lines a human has to repair, and the other
    twenty items are innocent. The first occurrence is acted on — the behaviour
    every caller already had — and now the run SAYS so. Silence was the defect:
    `edit T005` answered "T005 updated", true about the line it touched and a
    lie about the request, with nothing anywhere saying a second T005 existed.
    """
    content = read(os.path.join(memdir, "next-steps.md"))
    if content is None:
        fail("next-steps.md not found in " + memdir)
    pos, found = 0, []
    for kind, text in split_blocks(content):
        if kind == "item-open" and item_id(text) == wanted_id:
            found.append((text, pos))
        pos += len(text)
    if not found:
        fail(missing_item_message(memdir, content, wanted_id))
    if len(found) > 1:
        print(ambiguous_id_message(wanted_id, [t for t, _ in found]), file=sys.stderr)
    return content, found[0][0], found[0][1]


# --- age: how long the item has been standing there -----------------------
# Work parked in a queue has no age unless something writes one down, and until
# it does, "old" is archaeology: the reader reconstructs it from whatever the
# item's prose happens to mention. **Born:** is that stamp, and `list` turns it
# into the one number a reader acts on.
#
# Displayed ALWAYS, and never turned into a question. What a threshold is worth
# is not decided here and deliberately not: no queue has yet been measured with
# ages visible, so a number written today would be a guess wearing the authority
# of a constant. The skills say the same thing in prose — show the age, name the
# cut you used, and let the value be chosen once there is a distribution to
# choose it from.

# Anchored on both sides so a longer run of digits is not read as a date: a
# `20260827-1` in prose is one token, not a date followed by a number.
ISO_DATE_RE = re.compile(r"(?<![0-9])[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}(?![0-9])")
# The reasons `migrate` may decline to backdate an item, one line each. Spelled
# here because the report groups BY reason, and a reason that varies per item is
# a report with one group per item.
NO_BIRTHDATE = {
    "none": "no **Source:** field to read a date from",
    "unreadable": "a **Source:** no reader may use — off the first line, or ahead of "
                  "the **Class:** anchor (`migrate` reports the repair separately)",
    "no-date": "a **Source:** stating no `YYYY-MM-DD` date (a `20/08` has no year, "
             "and inferring one is inventing)",
    "two": "a **Source:** stating two different dates",
    "future": "a **Source:** date later than today",
}


def source_birthdate(value, today):
    """(the date a **Source:** value STATES, or None; why not, when None).

    Never a guess. The queue this was written for carries `wrap-up 19/08`,
    `kickoff 20/08` and `tracker, 2026-07-22` side by side, and the first two are
    exactly where a parser earns its reputation: `19/08` is a real day in a real
    year that the text does not name, so reading it means choosing the year — and
    an item stamped with the wrong year reports an age nobody can tell from a
    right one. An item left undated says "unknown" out loud instead.

    A full ISO date inside a sentence still counts (`PR #63, 2026-08-13`): the
    year is written down, so nothing is being inferred. TWO different ones do not,
    for the same reason as `19/08` — picking either is a choice the text does not
    make. The same date twice is one date, not two.
    """
    seen = []
    for m in ISO_DATE_RE.finditer(value or ""):
        try:
            d = datetime.date.fromisoformat(m.group(0))
        except ValueError:
            continue                    # `2026-13-40` is the shape, not a date
        if d not in seen:
            seen.append(d)
    if not seen:
        return None, "no-date"
    if len(seen) > 1:
        return None, "two"
    # An item cannot have been born after today, so a later date is not a birth
    # date that needs honouring — it is a typo or a plan, and stamping it would
    # make `list` print a NEGATIVE age, the one output a reader has no reading for
    if seen[0] > today:
        return None, "future"
    return seen[0], None


def backdate(block, today):
    """(`block` with a **Born:** stamp read off its **Source:**, why not otherwise).

    `migrate`'s half of the field: `add` stamps what it witnesses, and this reads
    what an older item wrote down about itself. The alternative was stamping the
    day of the migration on every legacy item, and it is worse than leaving them
    blank — the whole queue would come out one day old, which is not "unknown", it
    is a wrong answer that looks like a right one.

    Returns "stamped" for an item that already carries the field. `migrate` is
    rerun on the queue it already migrated, and a second pass that restamped would
    make the age of every item the age of the last run.
    """
    if real_fields(block, "Born"):
        return block, "stamped"
    src = real_fields(block, "Source")
    if not src:
        # The two are not one reason: an item with no Source at all was born
        # before anything recorded provenance, and an item whose Source the
        # POSITION rule refuses is one repair away from an age. Reporting both as
        # "no Source" would send a reader looking for a field the item carries
        return block, ("unreadable" if markers(block, "Source")
                       else "none")
    born, why = source_birthdate(field_value(src[0]), today)
    if born is None:
        return block, why
    # spliced in immediately BEFORE **Source:**, which is where compose_item puts
    # it — a migrated item and a freshly added one then carry the same line, and
    # every later reader has one shape to read instead of two.
    #
    # That anchor is also what makes the result readable without a readback. The
    # segment lands contiguous with the one it displaces, carries the period the
    # chain rule asks of every segment but the last, and sits after the **Class:**
    # `real_fields` measures from — because Source itself qualified, or this
    # function would have returned two lines up.
    item = parse_item(block)
    item.insert_field(item.fields.index(item.field_at(src[0].start())),
                      f"**Born:** {born.isoformat()}.")
    return render_item(item), None


def item_age(block, today):
    """Days since the item was born, or None when it carries no readable stamp.

    None is the honest answer for a queue that predates the field, and it is what
    the display renders as `?` rather than as a zero: an item with no stamp is not
    an item born today.
    """
    got = real_fields(block, "Born")
    if not got:
        return None
    try:
        born = datetime.date.fromisoformat(field_value(got[0]))
    except ValueError:
        return None
    return (today - born).days


def age_cell(days):
    """The age column's cell: `12d`, or `?` for an item with no stamp."""
    return "?" if days is None else f"{days}d"


# --- commands -------------------------------------------------------------

def cmd_list(args):
    memdir = memory_dir(args.dir)
    content = read(os.path.join(memdir, "next-steps.md"))
    if content is None:
        fail("next-steps.md not found in " + memdir)
    opens = [text for kind, text in split_blocks(content) if kind == "item-open"]
    ids = [item_id(t) for t in opens]
    # the ID, not the spelling: T001 and T0001 are one number and two rows, and a
    # reader who cannot see that only the first is reachable will keep addressing
    # the second one
    ambiguous = ambiguous_ids(ids)
    # ONE date for the whole listing: asking datetime per item would let a run
    # spanning midnight print two different ages for two items of the same age
    today = datetime.date.today()
    rows = []
    for text, iid in zip(opens, ids):
        rows.append((item_label(text), item_class(text) or "?",
                     age_cell(item_age(text, today)), item_title(text),
                     marker_value(text, "Project", PROJECT_TAG_VALUE_RE),
                     displayed_claim(text),
                     iid if iid in ambiguous else None, displayed_blocker(text)))
    if not rows:
        print("(queue empty)")
        return
    width = label_column(r[0] for r in rows)
    grouped = group_by_tag([(project, (label, cls, age, title, mark, dup, blocked))
                            for label, cls, age, title, project, mark, dup, blocked in rows],
                           by_position=True)
    if grouped is None:
        for label, cls, age, title, _, mark, dup, blocked in rows:
            print(list_line(label, cls, age, title, mark, dup, blocked, width))
    else:
        # ONE width for the whole listing, measured before the grouping: per group
        # it would give two column positions to one screen, and the eye that reads
        # a column down the page is the whole reason there is a column
        for n, (project, entries) in enumerate(grouped):
            if n:
                print()
            print(f"## {project or 'no project'}")
            for label, cls, age, title, mark, dup, blocked in entries:
                print(list_line(label, cls, age, title, mark, dup, blocked, width))
    if any(r[0] == "----" for r in rows):
        print("\nitems without ID: run `tk-queue migrate` to assign them.")
    if ambiguous:
        print("\n" + AMBIGUOUS_NOTE)
    strange = [(item_label(t), unknown_class(t)) for t in opens if unknown_class(t)]
    if strange:
        print("\nclass values that are no class: "
              + ", ".join(f"{label} ({cls})" for label, cls in strange)
              + f" — the classes are {', '.join(CLASSES)}. No gate acts on any other "
              "value; `tk-queue edit <id> --class <CLASS>` sets one.")


def displayed_blocker(block):
    """`blocked by T006` for an item that names one at the writer's position, or
    "" — the same POSITION rule every gate reads a field through, so `list` and
    `pack` cannot disagree about whether an item is held back.

    Tolerant where the gate refuses, and ONLY in the safe direction — the reading
    `displayed_claim` already settled for the sibling field. Every shape `pack`
    excludes the item over is shown as held back; printing nothing there was the
    dangerous answer, because the caller who reads that exclusion reason then
    looks the item up in `list` and finds it standing free.

    A value that is not an id is shown VERBATIM rather than hidden: `pack`
    excludes the item for it, and a listing that printed nothing there would
    leave the caller reading `pack`'s reason with no way to see the field."""
    segs, markers = qualified_fields(block, "Blocked-by")
    if len(segs) > 1:
        return "blocked ambiguously — `tk-queue pack` says why"
    if not segs:
        return "a **Blocked-by:** marker no gate reads" if markers else ""
    return f"blocked by {field_value(segs[0])}"


def unknown_class(block):
    """The class value this item carries that is NOT one of CLASSES, or None.

    Printed in the class column, `URGENTE` reads exactly like a class this queue
    has: same place, same shape, and nothing anywhere saying that no gate will
    ever act on it. The value is still SHOWN — it is what the file says, and
    hiding it would send the reader to a file that disagrees with the screen —
    and it is NAMED, once, under the listing.
    """
    cls = item_class(block)
    return cls if cls and cls not in CLASSES else None


def label_column(labels):
    """The width the ID column needs — the widest label in the listing, never
    narrower than the canonical four.

    A label is the item's OWN spelling (item_label), so it is not one width: `T001`
    is four characters and `T0001` and `T1000` are five. Printed with two spaces
    after it, the wide one pushed the class column one character right and the
    reader lost the very alignment the columns exist for — on the rows most in
    need of a second look, since one of the two spellings of a duplicated ID is
    wide by definition.

    The 4 is the width an EMPTY listing falls back to, and it is not a floor:
    every label a queue can carry is already four characters or more (`----`, and
    `T` plus the three digits ID_SLOT demands), so nothing narrows below it. The
    package prints its two block headings over an empty queue, and asking `max`
    for the widest of no labels is how that run would end in a traceback.

    Widening is paid for only by the listings that have something to widen for: a
    queue whose labels are all canonical keeps the line every skill and every eye
    already reads.
    """
    return max([4] + [len(l) for l in labels])


def list_line(label, cls, age, title, mark, dup=None, blocked="", width=4):
    """The claim is appended, not given a column of its own: it is the exception
    (most items are free), and a column would indent every line of every queue for
    a field almost none of them carry.

    The AGE gets the opposite treatment, for the opposite reason: every item added
    from here on carries one, so it is the rule and not the exception, and a value
    read down a column is what makes "which of these is old" one glance instead of
    a line-by-line read. `?` marks the items that predate the field, and it is
    right-aligned with the rest so the digits line up under each other.

    `width` is the ID column's, from `label_column`. Its default is the canonical
    four, so a caller that has only one label to print still lands on the column
    every other listing uses.
    """
    return (f"{label:<{width}}  {cls:<10}  {age:>4}  {title}"
            + (f"  [{mark}]" if mark else "")
            # appended for the same reason as the claim, and AFTER it: an item is
            # far more often free than blocked, and the two can be true at once
            + (f"  [{blocked}]" if blocked else "")
            # `is not None`: T000 is an ID, and truthiness would drop its mark
            + (f"  [{AMBIGUOUS_MARK % dup}]" if dup is not None else ""))


# --- pack: the candidates for an unattended package ------------------------
# Until now the afk package was filtered BY EYE, from `list` output plus the
# prose in AFK.md. `pack` makes that filter executable — and, more importantly,
# makes every exclusion VISIBLE: an item filtered out silently is an item the
# user never learns about, and the two fields that do the filtering (Risk, Env)
# are exactly the ones nothing else in the report would mention.
#
# What it does NOT decide is the SIZE of the package (3-6 items, ~2h). That
# stays the orchestrator's judgment: the script hands over candidates in the
# queue's global order and the caller cuts.

PACK_TITLE = 60   # excluded lines carry a reason after the text; `list`'s 100 would bury it

# One repair per SHAPE, printed ONCE under the list rather than on every line it
# applies to. Measured on the real queues: six consecutive items carry the same
# continuation-line defect, and repeating a remedy that long six times buries the
# six reasons it sits beside — in output whose reader is a skill's prose. The IDs
# are in the lines above; the repair names the shape it lifts.
# `--why` is not decoration in this line: `cancel` REQUIRES it, so the remedy
# without it is refused by argparse — a dead end printed as an answer, which is
# the failure this whole block exists to avoid. The suite runs it verbatim
REPAIR_CANCEL = ("a chain no gate can read: `tk-queue cancel <id> --why \"<why>\"` + re-add "
                 "it clean. No per-field `edit` repairs this reliably — several are refused "
                 "on the very field at fault")
REPAIR_FOLD = ("fields off the first line: `tk-queue migrate` folds them up, every item in "
               "one pass, and names the ones it left alone — the same fold, per item, is "
               "`tk-queue edit <id> --text \"<the item's whole text>\"`. Where a marker and "
               "its value sit on different lines the fold cannot join them, and where a "
               "marker sits on a line it may not absorb — a bullet, a quote, anything that "
               "opens a Markdown block — it will not lift half a chain; the repair for both "
               "is `cancel` + re-add")
REPAIR_CLASSLESS = "no **Class:** at all: `tk-queue edit <id> --class AUTONOMOUS`"
# A class this queue does not have is not a state, and the difference decides
# what the caller does next: `class is DECISION` is the item waiting for a human
# and nothing to repair, while `class is URGENTE` is a value no gate will ever
# read — the item is out of every package on every machine until someone gives
# it one of the five.
# The first half is DERIVED from CLASS_ALIASES rather than respelled here: this
# is the printed remedy T134 is about, and the way it fails is by promising a
# repair the command does not make. Whatever `migrate` translates, this offers;
# whatever it names instead, the second half addresses.
REPAIR_UNKNOWN_CLASS = ("a class value that is no class: `tk-queue migrate` writes the "
                        "spellings it maps (" + class_alias_summary() + ") and NAMES the "
                        "rest; for those, `tk-queue edit <id> --class <CLASS>`, one of "
                        + ", ".join(CLASSES))
# The same shape with its fields still off the first line, and the ORDER is the
# whole of it: `edit --class` appends the anchor to a first line that has no
# chain, which strands those fields for good and stops `migrate` from ever
# folding them. `edit --class` refuses that item now, so printing REPAIR_CLASSLESS
# for it would be a dead end printed as an answer — the failure this block exists
# to avoid, and the suite runs this line verbatim
REPAIR_FOLD_THEN_CLASS = ("no **Class:** AND fields off the first line: `tk-queue "
                          "migrate` folds them up first — the class is refused until "
                          "they are on the line it anchors — then `tk-queue edit <id> "
                          "--class AUTONOMOUS`")
REPAIR_RELEASE = ("a claim whose session is gone: `tk-queue release <id>` hands the item "
                  "back without closing it, and prints whose claim it dropped")
REPAIR_MIGRATE = "an item with no ID: `tk-queue migrate` assigns one"

# The stable shape, in the help because a skill's prose reads this output: a
# format that drifts silently is a skill reading the wrong column and never
# saying so. The sample is a REAL run and the suite asserts it character for
# character against the queue that produces it — a documented shape nothing
# executes is the one that goes stale first, and this one documents an AC.
PACK_SAMPLE = """eligible (4 of 8, in queue order):
T007  S (~20min)    spec ambiente#171     the first ticket of the spec  [ambiente#172]  [repo: https://github.com/owner/code.git]
T008  M (~1h)       spec ambiente#171     the second one  [ambiente#173]
T009  S             avulso                the item's text  [repo: /srv/projects/exemplo]
T010  S             avulso (ambiente#159)  a lone ticket, under the floor

excluded (4):
T012  another item  — class is DECISION
T021  a ticket of a second spec  — lane de spec ocupada por ambiente#171; esta é ambiente#144
T022  and its sibling  — lane de spec ocupada por ambiente#171; esta é ambiente#144
T031  a legacy item  — no **Class:** field

repairs:
- no **Class:** at all: `tk-queue edit <id> --class AUTONOMOUS`
"""

PACK_FORMAT = """output format (stable — a skill's prose reads it). The sample below is a real
run, on a queue of eight items, asserted character for character by the suite:

""" + PACK_SAMPLE + """
Eligible: ID, Effort, LANE, text, and — appended in brackets, only for the items
that carry one — the **Ticket:** the item's PR closes, then the **Repo:** its code
lands in, labelled `[repo: …]`. `[?]` (and `[repo: ?]`) where the item carries the
marker and no reader may use its value: two in the chain, one off the position
rule, a value of the wrong shape. `[?]` rather than silence: an item whose ticket
cannot be read is not an item that never had one, and the PR that closes nothing is
the failure the field exists to prevent — the same for an address that would send a
worktree to the wrong repository. The repo is the one group that carries a LABEL,
because either group may be absent: a reader telling the two apart by POSITION
would read an address as a ticket the first time an item carried a repo and no
ticket. Effort is raw and never summed, because summing free text is the hidden
heuristic this queue's order deliberately has none of. Both groups are appended
rather than given a column for the reason the claim is in `list`: almost no item
carries one. `[duplicate ID <n>]` closes an eligible line when more than one open
item carries that number — the same mark `list` prints, and the ID column is as
wide as the widest LABEL of the run, so `T0001` beside `T001` moves no column.
Excluded: ID, text, the duplicate mark where there is one, and the reason, which
always carries the VALUE the filter read (`Env is 'desktop', this machine is
server`; `Risk: <the line>`): a marker absorbed out of an item's prose then reads
as an obviously wrong line instead of a silently missing item. The mark comes
BEFORE the reason on this line, because the reason ends the line by contract and
a mark past it reads as part of the reason. Repairs are listed once per defect
SHAPE, whatever the number of items carrying it, and the sentence saying only the
FIRST item under a duplicated ID is reachable is one of them.

Exclusion rules, in the order they are asked; the FIRST that applies is the
reason. Every field is read at the position a WRITER puts it — after **Class:**,
in the chain that ends the first line — never by searching the block:

  class      the chain must name AUTONOMOUS
  risk       no **Risk:** field
  env        no **Env:** field, or one naming THIS machine (the site file's identity)
  blocked    no **Blocked-by:** field, or one naming an item this queue no longer
             holds OPEN. The blocker closing is what lets the item back in — no
             re-edit — and the reason names the value, so the caller reads which
             item to chase. An unreadable value excludes, like Risk and Env: a
             dependency nobody can read is one nobody may declare met
  spec       one **Spec:** in the chain, whose value is a forge reference
             (`<repo>#<n>`) — the shape `--spec` refuses on the way in, asked again
             here because a hand edit is not the writer. Read at the writer's
             POSITION only: unlike Risk and Env above, a provenance marker sitting
             where no gate reads it does not exclude the item, because "no answer"
             here has a safe default (a PR of its own) where for Risk and Env it
             means unknown danger
  claimed    not held by a sibling session
  id         an item `migrate` never gave an ID to cannot be closed by ID
  ticket     not a ticket `--blocked` names. The CALLER's fact about the tracker,
             like `--spec-under-way` below and read at the same rung of the
             ladder: this command opens no network connection, so a ticket
             blocked on the forge is invisible to it. The item leaves the package
             AND its spec's count with it
  lane       not a ticket of a SECOND spec, nor of one `--spec-under-way`
             names — see below

LANE, the column an unattended orchestrator dispatches by, read from the queue
and never from the forge (this command opens no network connection):

  spec <ref>  a ticket of the package's spec (its **Spec:** field), implemented on
              one accumulated branch with one PR for the whole spec. The WHOLE
              reference, repo half included: two repos can carry one issue number,
              and the repo half is what names the branch
  avulso (<ref>)  a ticket of a spec UNDER the floor — one PR of its own, and the
              spec named all the same, because the open-PR check is asked about it
  avulso      an item that is no ticket of any spec — one PR of its own

Every reference is printed in ONE canonical spelling — repo lower-cased, issue
number without leading zeros — decided when the value is written and re-decided
when it is read, so two spellings can never count as two specs.

ONE spec per package, so there is one branch, one campaign and one tail. The lane
goes to the spec with the MOST tickets among the candidates, ties broken by QUEUE
ORDER. The accumulated branch is what pays for itself — one branch, one campaign,
one tail, over as many tickets as possible — and queue order alone spent it on the
spec that happened to be listed first, leaving the deeper one for a later package.
The spec must still reach the FLOOR of two tickets: one ticket costs a draft PR
and a three-step tail for a single item, so that ticket is dispatched `avulso`
instead. Asked of the candidates only: an item excluded for its class, its Risk or
`--blocked` is not in the package, cannot take its lane with it, and is not
counted for one either.

A ticket leaves the package for its lane in TWO cases, and the second half of the
reason is what tells them apart — one read the queue, the other read the remote:

  esta é <ref>          its spec would be a SECOND accumulated lane. The reason
                        names both references, the one holding the lane and this
                        item's own, so the caller reads which spec lost without
                        opening the queue. A spec under the floor is never excluded
                        this way, whoever holds the lane: the floor is a lane, never
                        a filter
  declarada em curso …  `--spec-under-way <ref>` named this spec, so the caller has
                        already found its branch on the remote. The spec is skipped
                        when the lane is elected and its tickets leave WHATEVER
                        their count — under the floor they would go out `avulso`,
                        which is a second pull request over that same branch's work

Either way those tickets are the next package's, not lost.

`--blocked` is repeatable and is the other half of that second call, asked of
TICKETS instead of specs: `<repo>#<n>`, the value of an item's **Ticket:** field.
The item leaves the package and its spec's count with it, so a spec whose second
ticket is blocked drops under the floor instead of holding a lane on a ticket
nobody can start — which is exactly what the count could not see while it counted
ITEMS and the block lived on the forge.

`--spec-under-way` is repeatable, and it is the one input this command cannot
derive: it reads the queue and opens no network connection. So the caller runs
`pack`, asks the remote about every Spec reference the report names — the lanes and
the `esta é <ref>` rung alike, since either may be elected once a spec is skipped —
and runs `pack` a second time carrying the answer. Without that second call the
election is blind: it re-elects the spec whose branch is already open, and every
other spec's tickets stay excluded behind a lane the package cannot use.

A **Risk:** or **Env:** marker the position rule may not read excludes the item
too, and is reported BEFORE any value: "there is a **Risk:** marker I am not
allowed to read" is not "there is no risk", unattended execution is the wrong
place to guess, and a defect outranks an ordinary state as the thing to say. The
two PROVENANCE fields answer a different question and so take the other rule —
see LANE and the `spec` rung. **Repo:** takes it too, and there is no rung for it
in the ladder above: its absence has a safe default — the caller names the
repository from outside, as every caller did before the field existed — so an
address this command may not read costs the item a mark and never its place.

**Deferred:** is deliberately NOT read here. `--deferred` is refused on every
class but DECISION and leaving that class drops it, so no path through this CLI
produces an AUTONOMOUS item carrying one. The invariant lives in the writer,
where one mutation holds it, instead of being re-asserted by every reader."""


def field_value(segment):
    """The value a field segment carries — marker and the closing period removed.

    One period, not `rstrip('.')`: a value ending in an ellipsis is a value, and a
    reader that eats all three prints something the file does not contain."""
    body = segment.value.strip()
    return re.sub(r"\.\Z", "", body).strip()


def pack_identity():
    """This machine's environment name, or None when there is NO site file.

    None is a decision, not an oversight: with no site file no environment exists,
    so an item naming one names another machine and an item naming none is local.
    Deliberately not an error — a queue on a machine that never wrote the file must
    still produce a package, and the overwhelming majority of items carry no Env at
    all. A file that EXISTS and is broken is the other case entirely and is refused
    verbatim: there, "no environments exist" would be a guess about a file the user
    can see, and every Env-bearing item would silently leave the package."""
    try:
        site = tk_site.load()
    except tk_site.SiteError as e:
        fail(str(e))
    return site.identity if site else None


def pack_classless_reason(block):
    """(why this item's class could not be READ, the repair for its shape).

    Three shapes, and one line covering all three would print a remedy that is
    refused for two of them — the dead end this file has already paid for once (see
    claim_unwritable_message, which discriminates the same three at the length a
    single-item refusal can afford). Not shared with it: what the two have in common
    is the CONDITION, spelled from the same `markers`, while the messages
    differ in kind — one line inside a list here, a paragraph of diagnosis there.

    The remedies are that function's, and each was RUN against the real queues
    before being printed."""
    found = [f for f in field_chain(block) if f.canonical == "Class"]
    if len(found) > 1:
        # asked FIRST: this item's markers are on the first line and in the chain, so
        # both questions below would answer "fine" and the line would name a break
        # that is not there
        return (f"{len(found)} **Class:** fields in the chain, so its value is "
                "ambiguous", REPAIR_CANCEL)
    shape = class_shape(block)
    if shape == CLASS_SHAPE_NONE:
        # two repairs under one shape, because the ORDER of the two commands is
        # what the item's readability turns on — and the wrong order is the one
        # this list used to print
        if fields_off_first_line(block):
            return ("no **Class:** field, and its fields sit off the first line",
                    REPAIR_FOLD_THEN_CLASS)
        return "no **Class:** field", REPAIR_CLASSLESS
    if shape == CLASS_SHAPE_OFF_LINE:
        return "**Class:** sits off the first line, where no gate reads it", REPAIR_FOLD
    return "the field chain breaks before **Class:**, so no gate reads it", REPAIR_CANCEL


# The ticket the CALLER found blocked on the tracker. Same source as
# `--spec-under-way` (LANE_UNDER_WAY, spelled with the lane reasons below) — a
# question this command cannot ask, opening no network connection — and so the
# same shape of reason: the value, and the flag that supplied it, because a
# reason that did not name the flag would read as a fact the queue holds.
# ENGLISH, unlike the two lane reasons it is printed beside, because it is read
# next to `blocked by T006, still open` in the same excluded block: one names an
# item this queue holds, the other a ticket on a forge, and two spellings of
# "blocked" in two languages is the collision to avoid.
#
# Spelled HERE, above its one reader, and not down among the lane reasons it is
# printed beside: it sat some seventy lines BELOW that reader, so the message of
# the rung was reachable only by a reader who already knew it existed.
TICKET_BLOCKED = "ticket %s is blocked on the forge; declared by --blocked"


def pack_exclusion(iid, block, identity, open_ids=frozenset(),
                   blocked=frozenset()):
    """(why this item stays OUT of the package, the repair) — or None when it is a
    candidate.

    Every field is read through `qualified_fields`, the POSITION rule the write
    gates use, and never by searching the block. The block absorbs its continuation
    lines, so a marker in the item's own prose reads as a field to anything looser;
    here that costs more than anywhere else, because the failure is SILENT — a junk
    Env value is not this machine's identity, so the item simply stops appearing in
    every package, on every machine, with nothing anywhere to say why. That is why
    the reason carries the value it read.

    Where the position rule refuses to read a marker the item is excluded rather
    than passed. The two directions are not symmetric: passing it dispatches, with
    nobody watching, an item that may carry a Risk line or another machine's Env.

    Refusals are per-ITEM, never per-run: one malformed item may not stop the other
    twenty from being packaged — the same choice `displayed_claim` makes for `list`
    — so nothing here calls `fail`."""
    cls = chain_class(block)
    if cls is not None and cls not in CLASSES:
        return (f"class is {cls!r}, which is none of {', '.join(CLASSES)}, so no gate "
                "reads it", REPAIR_UNKNOWN_CLASS)
    if cls != "AUTONOMOUS":
        return pack_classless_reason(block) if cls is None else (f"class is {cls}", None)

    # what the two gate fields have in common is asked once, for both, BEFORE either
    # value: a field the position rule cannot read is a defect, and a defect outranks
    # an ordinary state as the thing to say about the item
    field = {}
    # Risk and Env are here and the two PROVENANCE fields are not, and the split is
    # what each field ANSWERS. Risk and Env answer "is this safe to run unattended,
    # and here?" — so a marker the position rule may not read leaves that unknown,
    # and unknown-danger is refused: the item leaves the package rather than being
    # dispatched on a guess. Spec and Ticket answer "where does this go?" — and
    # there the absence of an answer HAS a safe default, the conservative topology
    # the queue had before this field existed: a PR of its own, no `closes` line.
    #
    # So provenance is read purely at the writer's position (`pack_ref`), never by
    # counting markers in the block, and the cost of the other choice was measured:
    # with Spec in this loop, ONE sibling item whose note merely CITED `**Spec:**`
    # in prose was excluded with a false reason — its chain named Spec once,
    # unambiguously — and, having left the candidate set, took the whole spec below
    # the floor, demoting its clean siblings' accumulated lane to `avulso`.
    for name in ("Risk", "Env", "Blocked-by"):
        segs, markers = qualified_fields(block, name)
        if markers > len(segs):
            return (f"a **{name}:** marker sits where no gate reads it, so what the item "
                    "really carries cannot be told", REPAIR_CANCEL)
        if len(segs) > 1:
            return (f"{len(segs)} **{name}:** fields in the chain, so its value is "
                    "ambiguous", REPAIR_CANCEL)
        field[name] = segs[0] if segs else None

    if field["Risk"] is not None:
        return "Risk: " + field_value(field["Risk"]), None
    if field["Env"] is not None:
        value = field_value(field["Env"])
        if value != identity:
            where = f"this machine is {identity}" if identity else "there is no site file"
            # QUOTED, the way `--env` is quoted when the roster refuses it: this value
            # is the one the position rule may have read out of absorbed prose, and
            # unquoted an empty one prints "Env is , ..." while a trailing blank prints
            # as a clean name. The whole reason the value is in the line is to make a
            # wrong one look wrong
            return f"Env is {value!r}, {where}", None
    if field["Blocked-by"] is not None:
        value = field_value(field["Blocked-by"])
        m = ID_INPUT_RE.fullmatch(value.strip())
        if m is None:
            # the writer's gate is not the only door — a hand edit, a foreign
            # tool, a merge — and here the safe default is the one Risk and Env
            # take: a dependency nobody can read is not a dependency anybody may
            # declare satisfied, so the item leaves rather than being dispatched
            return (f"Blocked-by is {value!r}, which is not an item id, so whether the "
                    "dependency is met cannot be told", REPAIR_CANCEL)
        if int(m.group(1)) in open_ids:
            # QUOTED like Env's, and for the same reason: a value absorbed out of
            # an item's prose then reads as an obviously wrong line instead of a
            # silently missing item. No repair line — the repair is closing the
            # blocker, which is work and not a queue defect
            return f"blocked by {value}, still open", None

    # the Spec's SHAPE, asked at the rung the help documents — after risk and env,
    # not before them. It sat with the marker defects for one round, and that put a
    # typo'd Spec AHEAD of a real **Risk:** line: the item was excluded either way,
    # but the reason printed was the typo and the risk nobody saw went unprinted, on
    # the one field that says unattended execution can do damage.
    #
    # `validate_ref` guards `cmd_add`, the only writer, and this asks again because
    # the writer is not the only door — a hand edit, a foreign tool, a merge.
    # Measured on a queue nothing but `add` should have written: two items carrying
    # `**Spec:** homeserver ambiente#999.` formed a REAL accumulated lane and pushed
    # two tickets of a genuine spec out of the package, naming an issue that existed
    # only in the typo.
    spec_segs = real_fields(block, "Spec")
    if len(spec_segs) > 1:
        return (f"{len(spec_segs)} **Spec:** fields in the chain, so its value is "
                "ambiguous", REPAIR_CANCEL)
    if spec_segs and pack_ref(block, "Spec") is None:
        return (f"Spec is {field_value(spec_segs[0])!r}, which is not a forge reference "
                "(`<repo>#<n>`), so the lane it names cannot be told from a typo",
                REPAIR_CANCEL)

    # asked of `displayed_claim`, so `list` and the package cannot disagree about who
    # holds an item — the package has always been built from exactly that display
    mark = displayed_claim(block)
    if mark:
        return mark, REPAIR_RELEASE
    if qualified_fields(block, "Claimed")[1]:
        return ("a **Claimed:** marker sits where no gate reads it, so whether the item is "
                "held cannot be told", REPAIR_CANCEL)

    # last, so it is reached only by an item that is otherwise ready: the ID is how
    # every other command names an item, and a package cannot close one without it
    if iid is None:
        return "no ID", REPAIR_MIGRATE

    # AFTER the ID, and last of all, because this rung is not the QUEUE's: it is
    # the caller's answer about the forge, the same source and so the same place
    # in the ladder as `--spec-under-way`. Everything above it the item's own
    # text answers, and a reason the file explains outranks one it cannot.
    #
    # Read through `pack_ref`, the reader the lane and the `closes` line already
    # share, so a **Ticket:** the position rule may not use matches no reference
    # and costs the item nothing — the safe default both provenance fields take,
    # and the one `pack_closes` takes for the same value one line later.
    ref = pack_ref(block, "Ticket") if blocked else None
    if ref is not None and ref in blocked:
        return TICKET_BLOCKED % ref, None
    return None


def pack_effort(block):
    """The Effort the caller sizes the package by, or '?' when it cannot be read.

    Read through the position rule like every other field, and never fatal: Effort
    decides nothing about eligibility, so an item missing it is a candidate whose
    cost the caller has to estimate — not an item to drop over a field the filter
    was never about."""
    segs, markers = qualified_fields(block, "Effort")
    if len(segs) != 1 or markers > len(segs):
        return "?"
    return field_value(segs[0])


# The two lanes an eligible item can be dispatched in, and the reason a ticket
# leaves the package because the accumulated one is taken. Spelled once here
# rather than composed at each site: a skill's prose reads this output, and the
# reason is asserted character for character by two tickets' criteria.
#
# PORTUGUESE, in an output whose every other string is English (`class is
# DECISION`, `Env is 'desktop', ...`), and not by preference: `lane de spec
# ocupada por #<n>` is the exact string #172 and #173 both demand, so it is not
# this slice's to translate. The two lane words follow it because they name the
# same three concepts — one term per concept beats one language per file, and a
# lane called `standalone` standing beside a reason about `lane de spec` would be
# two vocabularies for one feature. Translating all three together, in the ticket
# that owns the string, is the repair.
LANE_SOLO = "avulso"
# a ticket of a spec that did not reach the floor: the lane is the solo one, and
# the spec is NAMED all the same. Printing a bare `avulso` made it identical to a
# ticket of no spec at all, and the two are not the same thing to the caller that
# dispatches: the open-PR check (the orchestrator's, it needs the forge) has to
# know which spec to ask about, and a lone ticket of a spec whose branch is
# already open is exactly the case that check exists for.
LANE_SOLO_SPEC = "avulso (%s)"
LANE_SPEC = "spec %s"
# the taken spec first, then this item's own — both WHOLE references. Printing
# only the issue number (`#171`) was measured collapsing two real specs into one
# label: `a#171` and `b#171` are both write-legal, this account's tickets already
# span two repos, and the excluded ticket then read "occupied by #171" naming its
# OWN number. The repo half is also the half `spec/<m>-<slug>` is built from, so
# dropping it left the orchestrator unable to name the branch from this output.
LANE_TAKEN = "lane de spec ocupada por %s; esta é %s"
# The SAME rung, read from the other source. This command opens no network
# connection, so whether a spec's branch is already pushed is the one input the
# election cannot derive — and going blind on it was measured costing a whole
# package: `pack` re-elected the spec whose branch was open, the caller took that
# spec's tickets out afterwards, and the second spec stayed excluded behind a lane
# nobody in the package could use. The caller asks the remote and hands the answer
# back through `--spec-under-way`, so the election is made once, knowing.
#
# First half identical to LANE_TAKEN, because it is one rung and a skill's prose
# reads it as one. Second half different, and that is what the report needs: the
# two exclusions read different sources — the queue's order, and the remote — and a
# reader that cannot tell them apart cannot tell which spec lost why. The flag is
# NAMED in it for the reason every other reason here carries its value: the caller
# supplied this one, and a reason that did not say so would read as a fact the
# queue holds.
LANE_UNDER_WAY = "lane de spec ocupada por %s; declarada em curso por --spec-under-way"
# how many tickets of one spec earn the accumulated lane. Below it the ticket is
# dispatched as an ordinary PR: a draft PR, a merge per item and a three-step
# tail cost more than a single item is worth. A FLOOR, never a filter — the lone
# ticket stays in the package, in the other lane.
SPEC_LANE_FLOOR = 2


def pack_ref(block, field):
    """The forge reference this item's `field` carries, canonical — or None when
    it carries none this reader may use.

    ONE reader for BOTH provenance fields, and that is the whole point. Three
    rules meet on a reference — where it may be read from, whether it is
    well-formed, which spelling of it counts — and this slice's review found a
    new defect every round for as long as each site decided them separately:
    `**Spec:**` was gated on the way out and `**Ticket:**` was not, so a
    hand-edited ticket value spliced free text into a line a skill parses; and
    equality was raw, so two casings of one spec became two lanes. Both are the
    same defect wearing a different field's name.

    So the three rules are asked here, once, for whoever asks:

      position   `real_fields` — the chain after the **Class:** it anchors at,
                 never a search of the block.
      shape      `REF_RE`, again. The writer's gate is not the only door: a hand
                 edit, a foreign tool, a merge. `fullmatch`, not `match` — a
                 prefix match would read `repo#171 texto solto` as a well-formed
                 `repo#171` and let it name a lane.
      spelling   `canonical_ref`, so `Ambiente#171` and `ambiente#0171` are the
                 one reference they name, whichever door they came in through.

    A FOURTH rule joined them, and it joined them HERE rather than in a caller,
    which is the correction that finished this consolidation:

      one        a chain naming the field TWICE says two things, so it says
                 nothing this reader may pick between.

    `pack_closes` asked that fourth question itself, one line before calling this
    — and asked it of the whole BLOCK, where this asks it of the chain. Measured:
    an item whose real, well-placed `**Ticket:** ambiente#172` sat beside a
    continuation line merely CITING the marker in prose printed no ticket at all,
    because the block-wide count saw two. One rule, two spellings, and the looser
    one silently won: the shape this consolidation exists to end, surviving in the
    caller that was supposed to stop keeping its own copy.

    None is the answer to every shape this may not use, and it is never fatal:
    what None COSTS the item is decided by the caller, and the two callers decide
    differently on purpose (see pack_exclusion and pack_closes)."""
    segs = real_fields(block, field)
    if len(segs) != 1:
        return None
    value = field_value(segs[0])
    return canonical_ref(value) if REF_RE.fullmatch(value) else None


# TWO OF THE THREE ARE NOT `edit` FLAGS — Ticket and Repo — and the omission is
# deliberate — the same answer CLEARABLE gives about **Claimed:**. Provenance is
# written once, by whoever imports the ticket, and it is the one thing about an item
# that cannot change without the item becoming a different item: a ticket re-pointed
# at another issue is the work of another issue, and one re-pointed at another
# repository is work in another repository. The repair for a typo is the repair
# for any unreadable chain — `cancel` and re-add, which also puts the wrong
# `closes` on the record instead of erasing it. `edit --text` keeps all three:
# it preserves everything from the first field marker on.
#
# **Spec:** IS one, and the split is what each field ANSWERS. Ticket and Repo say
# where the item CAME FROM and where its code lands — facts that cannot change while
# it stays the same item. Spec says which LANE the package dispatches it in
# (`pack_lanes`), and a lane is a routing decision over a queue that MOVES: a spec
# closes, a track is resliced, an accumulated lane is re-elected, and the same ticket
# belongs under a different one. Add-only there protected nothing and cost the
# alternative measured on 2026-09-01 — 22 items of this project's own queue could not
# enter an accumulated lane, and the only route in was to close all 22 as DESCARTADO
# and re-add them, which erases 22 real birth dates to repair a routing field. The
# value passes the SAME gate `--spec` passes on the way in (`validate_ref`), so the
# one spelling per reference this file holds is not what relaxes here.


def pack_closes(block):
    """`  [<ref>]` — the ticket this item's PR closes, or "" when it has none.

    APPENDED, not a column of its own, for the reason `list_line` appends the
    claim: most items are born in conversation and carry no ticket, and a column
    would indent every line of every queue for a field almost none of them have.
    The brackets are what keep it findable at the end of a title whose own blanks
    `item_title` has already collapsed.

    It is here at all because `--ticket` had no reader. The field feeds the
    `closes` line of the package's PR (#171), the package is dispatched from THIS
    output, and a provenance nothing returns is the prose the field was added to
    replace.

    Read through `pack_ref`, the SAME reader the lane is built from, and that is
    the correction this function earned: with a reader of its own it had the
    position rule and no shape gate, and a hand-edited `**Ticket:** repo#1]
    injetado` spliced a second bracket group and free text into a line whose
    consumer is a skill's prose. Nothing about a ticket makes it need a looser
    rule than a spec.

    Three outcomes, not two, and the third is the one this function got wrong for
    a round. An unreadable Ticket may not cost the item its place — Ticket decides
    no lane, so excluding it would be the lane's answer to a field that is not the
    lane's (that IS how the two callers of one reader differ, and it is on purpose).
    But SILENCE is not the alternative: printing nothing made an item whose
    `**Ticket:**` no reader may use identical to an item that never had one, and
    those are not the same thing to whoever writes the PR — one wants no `closes`
    line, the other wants the one it cannot have. The merge then closes nothing and
    the ticket stays open with the work shipped, which is the failure `validate_ref`
    names as the reason the write gate exists.

    So the unreadable one is MARKED, and marked the way this file already marks a
    field it could not read where the answer is not exclusion: `pack_effort`
    prints `?`, and so does this.

      no FIELD at all    nothing is appended. The overwhelming majority of items.
      readable           `  [<ref>]`, canonical.
      field, no ref      `  [?]` — two in the chain, or a value that is not a
                         forge reference.

    Which of the three an item gets is decided at the WRITER's position, like
    everything else here, and the first measured attempt at it was not: asking
    `markers(block, "Ticket")` is the same whole-block question the
    pre-gate was condemned for, turned around. It printed `[?]` for an item whose
    only `**Ticket:**` was a continuation-line note CITING the marker — an item
    that never had a ticket at all, sent to whoever writes the PR as one whose
    ticket could not be read. `real_fields` is the question that separates them:
    it answers [] for prose, whatever the block says."""
    if not real_fields(block, "Ticket"):
        return ""
    ref = pack_ref(block, "Ticket")
    return f"  [{ref}]" if ref else "  [?]"


def pack_repo(block):
    """`  [repo: <address>]` — the repository this item's code lands in, or "" when
    it names none.

    LABELLED, where `pack_closes` is bare, and the label is what keeps the shape
    readable: both groups are appended to the same line, either one may be absent,
    and a caller that told them apart by POSITION would read an item's repository
    as its ticket the first time an item carried a repo and no ticket. Telling them
    apart by their SHAPE instead — a forge reference against a URL — is a rule
    nobody wrote down, in output whose consumer is a skill's prose.

    Read at the WRITER's position (`real_fields`) and gated on the SHAPE again on
    the way out, for the reason every other field is: a hand edit is not the
    writer, and `origin` sitting in the file is precisely the guess this field
    exists to end.

    The three rules are the three `pack_ref` consolidated — position, shape, one —
    and this asks them itself rather than calling it, for one reason: `pack_ref` is
    a reader of FORGE REFERENCES. Its shape is REF_RE and its answer is canonical
    (`canonical_ref`), and an address has neither — nothing here compares two
    repositories, so it has no canonical spelling to be reduced to. Passing a shape
    and a canonicaliser in would give that function two behaviours for its two
    callers, which is the thing consolidating it was meant to end. What must not
    diverge is the RULES, so they are named here in the same order and read from
    the same helpers.

    Three outcomes, the same three `pack_closes` gives and for the same reason:

      no FIELD at all    nothing is appended. The caller names the repository from
                         outside, as every caller did before this field existed.
      readable           `  [repo: <address>]`, exactly as written.
      field, no address  `  [repo: ?]` — two in the chain, or a value no reader
                         may use. MARKED and not silent: an item whose address
                         cannot be read is not an item that never had one, and the
                         two ask different things of whoever opens the worktree.

    What it never does is EXCLUDE. Absent has a safe default here — the caller
    names it, as before — where an unreadable **Risk:** or **Env:** means unknown
    danger; that is the same split the two provenance fields take, and the reason
    is the same one."""
    segs = real_fields(block, "Repo")
    if not segs:
        return ""
    # None, not "": a chain naming the field twice must reach the mark on its own
    # answer, and not by leaning on the shape's refusal of an empty string — that
    # coupling is one edit to REPO_RE away from printing `[repo: ]` for it
    value = field_value(segs[0]) if len(segs) == 1 else None
    if value is None or not REPO_RE.fullmatch(value):
        return "  [repo: ?]"
    return f"  [repo: {value}]"


def pack_lanes(candidates, under_way=frozenset()):
    """{label: lane} for the candidates that stay, and {label: reason} for the
    tickets the taken lane pushes out.

    `under_way` is the set of specs the CALLER has found already being worked —
    canonical references, from `--spec-under-way`. They are skipped when the lane
    is elected and their tickets leave the package, whatever their count.

    ONE spec per package — one branch, one draft PR, one campaign, one tail — and
    it is the spec with the MOST tickets among those that reach the floor, ties
    broken by QUEUE ORDER. The accumulated branch is the one thing here that pays
    for itself, so it goes to the DEEPEST spec; queue order, the only priority
    this file carries, is what settles a tie, and `max()` over a list built in the
    file's order does both because it returns the first maximal element.

    "That reaches the floor" is the half the spec left open, and the reading is
    the one that keeps every sentence of it true. A spec with a single ticket in
    the package is under the floor: it gets no accumulated lane, so it has no lane
    to occupy either. Letting it take the lane anyway — which is the literal
    first-ticket-wins reading — made `pack` report `lane de spec ocupada por #m`
    for a lane nobody was using, and a package holding one lone ticket and three
    tickets of a second spec ran ZERO accumulated lanes while excluding the three.
    That is the capacity the two coexisting lanes exist to stop leaving on the
    table (#171, US 27), and it costs nothing against "one spec per package"
    (US 28): the package still has one branch, one campaign, one tail.

    So a ticket leaves the package for its lane in two cases, and only the first
    is the queue's: its spec would be a SECOND accumulated lane. A spec under the
    floor is never excluded THAT way, whoever holds the lane: the floor is a lane,
    never a filter. The second case is `under_way`, below, and the floor is no
    shield there.

    Asked of the CANDIDATES only, after every other rule has run. An item already
    excluded for its class or its Risk is not in the package, so letting it take
    the package's lane with it would refuse a lane nothing is using.

    A spec `under_way` is asked FIRST, above the floor, and that order is the rule
    rather than a detail of the writing. Under the floor its ticket would be
    dispatched `avulso (<ref>)` — a pull request of its own, over the work the open
    branch is already carrying, which is the second pull request per spec the whole
    check exists to prevent. So the floor shields a ticket from the lane CONTEST and
    never from this: the two rungs answer different questions."""
    specs = [(n, pack_ref(block, "Spec")) for n, block in candidates]
    tickets = {}
    for _, ref in specs:
        if ref is not None:
            tickets[ref] = tickets.get(ref, 0) + 1
    contenders = [ref for _, ref in specs
                  if ref is not None and ref not in under_way
                  and tickets[ref] >= SPEC_LANE_FLOOR]
    taken = max(contenders, key=lambda ref: tickets[ref], default=None)
    lanes, pushed = {}, {}
    for n, ref in specs:
        if ref is not None and ref in under_way:
            pushed[n] = LANE_UNDER_WAY % ref
        elif ref is not None and ref == taken:
            lanes[n] = LANE_SPEC % taken
        elif ref is not None and tickets[ref] >= SPEC_LANE_FLOOR:
            # the only exclusion here: this spec would be a SECOND accumulated
            # lane. Under the floor it would be no lane at all, so it stays
            pushed[n] = LANE_TAKEN % (taken, ref)
        else:
            lanes[n] = LANE_SOLO if ref is None else LANE_SOLO_SPEC % ref
    return lanes, pushed


def cmd_pack(args):
    # Refused BEFORE the queue is opened, the way argparse refuses a bad flag: a
    # malformed reference is the caller's error whatever the queue holds, and the
    # same gate `--spec` passes through on the way in. Silence here would elect the
    # lane as though the caller had said nothing — the exact blindness the flag
    # exists to end, wearing a typo instead of a missing call.
    under_way = frozenset(validate_ref("spec-under-way", v)
                          for v in (args.spec_under_way or []))
    # the same gate, for the same reason: a malformed ticket reference matches no
    # item, so the ticket the caller called a second time to take OUT would be
    # dispatched — the blindness back, wearing a typo
    blocked = frozenset(validate_ref("blocked", v) for v in (args.blocked or []))
    memdir = memory_dir(args.dir)
    content = read(os.path.join(memdir, "next-steps.md"))
    if content is None:
        fail("next-steps.md not found in " + memdir)
    identity = pack_identity()
    # ONE pass over the queue, in its order, then the lane. The lane is the only
    # rule here that is not about the item alone — it needs to know which spec
    # came first and how many tickets it has — so the verdicts are collected
    # before any line is composed, and the two lists are built afterwards from
    # the same ordered rows. Emitting as we went and appending the lane
    # exclusions at the end would report them out of queue order, in a listing
    # whose whole contract is that its order IS the priority.
    # collected BEFORE the pass, because a blocker sits anywhere in the file: the
    # queue's order is priority, not dependency order, and an item may name one
    # that is listed below it. Read from the OPEN items alone, which is the whole
    # question a **Blocked-by:** asks — an id in the done-log is a dependency met,
    # and one this queue never had is a blocker that has already left it
    open_ids = frozenset(i for i in (item_id(t) for kind, t in split_blocks(content)
                                     if kind == "item-open") if i is not None)
    # the same ambiguity `list` marks, read the same way: the ID, not the
    # spelling. The package printed two rows under one number with nothing saying
    # only the first was reachable, so the reader who dispatched the second one
    # dispatched the first — the silence the mark on `list` exists to end, in the
    # output an unattended package is actually cut from
    ambiguous = ambiguous_ids([item_id(t) for kind, t in split_blocks(content)
                               if kind == "item-open"])
    rows, candidates = [], []
    for kind, text in split_blocks(content):
        if kind != "item-open":
            continue
        iid = item_id(text)
        # the item's own spelling, from item_label: `list` and the package must not
        # disagree about how an ID reads. It also keeps T000 an ID — the rule below
        # asks `iid is None`, and truthiness here packaged T000 under the `----` of
        # an item with no ID at all
        label = item_label(text)
        verdict = pack_exclusion(iid, text, identity, open_ids, blocked)
        # ROW POSITION, never the label, is what addresses an item here. A label is
        # not unique: `----` is every ID-less item's, and a duplicate ID is a shape
        # `list` warns about rather than refuses. Keying the lane by it was measured
        # on a queue with two `**T001**`: the genuine lane holder came back EXCLUDED,
        # by the entry of the item that shared its label, with a reason naming its
        # own spec as the one occupying the lane.
        rows.append((label, text, verdict))
        if verdict is None:
            candidates.append((len(rows) - 1, text))
    lanes, pushed = pack_lanes(candidates, under_way)

    eligible, excluded, repairs = [], [], []
    width = label_column(label for label, _, _ in rows)
    for n, (label, text, verdict) in enumerate(rows):
        dup = item_id(text) if item_id(text) in ambiguous else None
        mark = f"  [{AMBIGUOUS_MARK % dup}]" if dup is not None else ""
        if verdict is None and n not in pushed:
            # Effort is free text, so the column is padded and never TRUNCATED: a
            # long one pushes the title right, where a cut one would hide the very
            # wall-clock guess the caller sizes the package by. The lane sits
            # between them for the same reason: both columns keep their width and
            # only the title moves.
            eligible.append(f"{label:<{width}}  {pack_effort(text):<12}  "
                            f"{lanes[n]:<20}  {item_title(text)}"
                            + pack_closes(text) + pack_repo(text) + mark)
            continue
        # the lane is the LAST step of the ladder: it is asked only of an item
        # every earlier rule let through, so an exclusion here never hides one
        # about the item itself
        reason, repair = verdict if verdict is not None else (pushed[n], None)
        # the mark goes BEFORE the reason and not after it: the reason ends an
        # excluded line by contract, and a mark past it reads as part of the reason
        excluded.append(f"{label:<{width}}  {item_title(text, PACK_TITLE)}{mark}  — {reason}")
        if repair and repair not in repairs:
            repairs.append(repair)
    total = len(eligible) + len(excluded)
    print(f"eligible ({len(eligible)} of {total}, in queue order):")
    print("\n".join(eligible) if eligible else "(none)")
    print()
    print(f"excluded ({len(excluded)}):")
    print("\n".join(excluded) if excluded else "(none)")
    # the mark says an item is unreachable; this says what to do about it, and the
    # repair block is where the reader of this output looks for that. ONE spelling
    # shared with the listing, so the two cannot come to describe the ambiguity
    # differently
    if ambiguous:
        repairs.append(AMBIGUOUS_NOTE)
    if repairs:
        print("\nrepairs:")
        for r in repairs:
            print("- " + r)


def compose_item(args, iid):
    fields = [f"**Class:** {args.classe}."]
    # right after the class it qualifies, so the two are read together
    if args.deferred and not clears_field(args.deferred):
        fields.append(f"**Deferred:** {args.deferred}.")
    fields.append(f"**Effort:** {args.effort}.")
    if args.risk and not clears_field(args.risk):
        fields.append(f"**Risk:** {args.risk}.")
    # beside Risk: the two fields that decide whether this item can be picked up
    # unattended, and where. Absent = it runs wherever the queue lives
    if args.env and not clears_field(args.env):
        fields.append(f"**Env:** {args.env}.")
    # the third field `pack` gates on, and it sits with the other two: Risk says
    # whether this may run unattended, Env says where, and this says NOT YET
    if args.blocked_by and not clears_field(args.blocked_by):
        fields.append(f"**Blocked-by:** {args.blocked_by}.")
    fields.append(f"**Criterion:** {args.criterion}.")
    if args.project:
        fields.append(f"**Project:** {args.project}.")
    # beside **Source:**, the field they are a sharper version of: all three say
    # where the item came from. Absent unless the caller passed the flag — an
    # item born in conversation has no ticket, and an empty field would read as
    # a provenance that was looked for and not found
    # **Repo:** closes the run because it answers the question the two before it
    # raise: they name the TRACKER, and this names where the code goes
    for flag, field in ((args.ticket, "Ticket"), (args.spec, "Spec"),
                        (args.repo, "Repo")):
        if flag:
            fields.append(f"**{field}:** {flag}.")
    # BEFORE Source, and with the period every other segment carries: Source is
    # written last and is the one segment field_chain lets off the period rule, so
    # a field appended AFTER it would move that free pass onto a segment the rule
    # still applies to. The birth date is the script's own stamp, never the
    # caller's: `--source` may say the item was thought of in July, and the item
    # still entered this queue today. Backdating is `migrate`'s job, and it reads
    # Source to do it.
    fields.append(f"**Born:** {datetime.date.today().isoformat()}.")
    fields.append(f"**Source:** {args.source or datetime.date.today().isoformat()}")
    return f"- [ ] **T{iid:03d}** — {args.text.strip()} " + " ".join(fields) + "\n"


def check_ceiling(item, force):
    """The ceiling is measured on the BLOCK, and the refusal has to say so.

    A caller reading `item has 1011 chars` beside a text they know to be 641 long
    reads the number as the text's own, and cuts prose that was never the
    overflow: the other 370 chars were the field chain. Measured on 2026-09-03 —
    one round spent shortening the wrong half. So both halves are counted here,
    and the message names the one the cut has to come out of."""
    limit = CEILING_FORCED if force else CEILING
    if len(item) > limit:
        chain = field_chain(item)
        head = item.split("\n", 1)[0]
        # the field chain is a suffix of the FIRST line (compose_item); everything
        # else — the head, the text, any continuation line — is the other half
        fields = len(head) - chain[0].start() if chain else 0
        text = len(item) - fields
        fail(
            f"the item BLOCK has {len(item)} chars (ceiling {limit}) — the block is the "
            f"text AND its field chain, not the text alone: {text} chars of text and "
            f"{fields} of fields, so the cut comes out of the "
            f"{'text' if text >= fields else 'fields'}. An item is a pending "
            "action, not an essay: move the context to a memory file or wiki page "
            "and link it ([[slug]])." + ("" if force else " --force raises the ceiling to "
            f"{CEILING_FORCED} for the rare exception.")
        )


def check_field_ceilings(force, **fields):
    """Measure each field VALUE against the ceiling its NATURE earns it.

    Short fields (SHORT_FLAGS) are bounded by construction and get the small
    ceiling; everything else is prose and gets the larger one — and, for the
    item fields among them, the block ceiling too (see cmd_edit), because a
    per-field ceiling alone does not bound a call that passes several at once.
    Applied identically on `add`, `edit` and the closes, or a file could hold a
    value that no later command is allowed to write.
    """
    for name, val in fields.items():
        if not val:
            continue
        if name in SHORT_FLAGS:
            limit, forced = (FIELD_CEILING_SHORT_FORCED if force
                             else FIELD_CEILING_SHORT), FIELD_CEILING_SHORT_FORCED
        else:
            limit, forced = (FIELD_CEILING_FORCED if force
                             else FIELD_CEILING), FIELD_CEILING_FORCED
        if len(val) > limit:
            fail(f"--{name} has {len(val)} chars (field ceiling {limit}). A field is one "
                 "line: put the substance in a memory file or wiki page and link it "
                 "([[slug]])." + ("" if force else f" --force raises it to {forced}."))


# --- the WIP cap: how many open items this machine may hold at once ---------
# A queue is a WORKING SET, and an `add` is the cheapest action in this CLI: a
# session that cannot finish a finding enqueues it, and the queue grows faster
# than any session empties it. The cap is the whitelist form of the answer —
# REFUSE AT IT, ALWAYS, and let a human take an item out — and deliberately not
# a gate with an escape hatch. There is no `--force` here and `add --force` does
# not reach it: an unattended `--force` is a string no gate can judge, and this
# script has no signal of whether anyone is present to judge it. What stays open
# is the pair of sinks a review already prescribes, which write no new item:
# `edit --text` on an item that exists, and `handoff`.
#
# WHAT THE WHITELIST DOES NOT COVER: `HOME`. It resolves the site file and the
# roster both, so a home with a cap in it and no `~/.claude/projects` counts one
# queue as if it were the machine. That is not a way past THIS machine's cap —
# whoever chooses HOME chooses the site file the cap is written in, and could
# simply write no cap — but the shrunken count is said out loud in check_wip
# rather than left to look like a full sum.


def site_names_the_cap():
    """Whether the site file NAMES the cap, asked of a file that would not parse.

    The question is not "is the file good" — `tk_site` already answered no. It is
    whether the cap is in there at all, and it is asked the crudest way on
    purpose: the bytes, scanned for an assignment to the key. A file this reader
    cannot parse is one no parser should be pointed at twice.

    IT IS ASKED IN EVERY ENCODING THE FILE MAY BE IN, not only in UTF-8. UTF-8
    comes first and replaces each undecodable byte, so one bad byte in a comment
    cannot hide a cap two lines below it. Then the UTF-16 family, because there
    the key is spelled `m\\x00a\\x00x...` and no scan of the replaced UTF-8 text
    matches it: a site file saved as "Unicode" by a Windows editor — the shape
    T345 already proved realistic for these files — WOULD SET a cap that this
    function then reported absent, and the add went past it uncapped while the
    reason printed was that no cap was written. The gate is a whitelist, and a
    cap that disappears in silence is the one direction it may not fail in.

    A path that is not a plain file answers False: the FIFO that would hang and
    the directory that cannot be read hold no cap anyone can act on, and the
    caller that DOES need that file — `--env` — still refuses it loudly.
    """
    path = tk_site.site_path()
    if not os.path.isfile(path):
        return False
    try:
        with open(path, "rb") as f:
            raw = f.read()
    except OSError:
        # unreadable is not a cap anyone can act on, either way
        return False
    # EVERY key that gates an add, not only the total. `max-open-items` alone
    # missed a file whose only ceiling is the per-queue one: `max-open-items` is
    # a PREFIX of `max-open-items-per-queue`, so the `\s*=` after it does not
    # match that line, and a broken file carrying the brake and nothing else was
    # reported and waved past — the cap disappearing in silence, which is the one
    # direction this whitelist may not fail in. Measured while T360 was written.
    assignment = re.compile(r"\s*(?:" + "|".join(
        re.escape(k) for k in (WIP_KEY, PER_QUEUE_KEY, DISCOUNT_KEY)) + r")\s*=")
    for encoding, errors in (("utf-8", "replace"),) + WIDE_DECODINGS:
        try:
            text = raw.decode(encoding, errors)
        except (UnicodeDecodeError, UnicodeError):
            continue      # not this encoding; a cap written in another may still be here
        if any(assignment.match(line) for line in text.splitlines()):
            return True
    return False


def load_site():
    """The parsed site file, or None when this machine has none.

    A file that exists, carries the cap and is unusable is FATAL, and that is the
    whole reason this runs on every `add` rather than only when `--env` is passed:
    the cap lives in that file, and a half-read one is a cap that silently
    disappears. The message is tk_site's own — it names the file and the line,
    which is the diagnosis a reader has to act on.

    WITH NO CAP IN IT, the same file is only REPORTED. An absent cap has to be a
    perfect no-op, and this gate made a rotten site file fatal to a command that
    had always ignored it — measured on six shapes of rot, base exit 0 against
    HEAD exit 1, on machines that never chose a number. The file is still broken
    for every reader that needs it, `--env` included, and this one says so on the
    way past instead of taking the queue down with it.
    """
    try:
        return tk_site.load()
    except tk_site.SiteError as e:
        if site_names_the_cap():
            fail(str(e))
        print(f"tk-queue: {e}\n(it names none of `{WIP_KEY}`, `{PER_QUEUE_KEY}` or "
              f"`{DISCOUNT_KEY}`, so no cap gates this one — but the file stays broken "
              "for every command that reads it)", file=sys.stderr)
        return None


def open_items(memdir):
    """How many OPEN items a queue holds, read through `tk_site.read_text`.

    NOT this script's own `read`. Every directory counted here but one belongs
    to a project this session never opened, and a reader of somebody else's file
    needs the guards that reader already carries, each measured in its docstring:
    a non-UTF-8 byte, a queue that is a directory or a FIFO, and a U+FEFF in any
    position. Measured on this gate before the reader was shared — a SIBLING
    queue saved as UTF-16 turned every `add` on the machine into a raw
    UnicodeDecodeError, and one invisible U+FEFF glued to an item's marker
    under-counted its queue enough to wave an add past a full cap.

    Only the COUNTING path reads that way. `read` keeps its `utf-8-sig` and
    feeds the rewrite of the target queue, where dropping a character the user
    typed would edit their own file — a decision that is theirs and is parked as
    a queue item, not one this gate takes on the way past.

    Two of that reader's guards arrive here UNREACHABLE, and the suite says so
    rather than claiming them: `tk-roster` lists a project only when its queue is
    a plain FILE, so no directory or FIFO reaches this count from the roster —
    and the target queue's own is read by `read` further down `cmd_add`, which
    still hangs on a FIFO. That hang is older than this gate and is parked as a
    queue item of its own; inheriting the guard costs nothing and closes the door
    the day either of those changes.

    A queue file that is not there counts zero rather than failing: `tk-roster`
    answered that the queue existed a moment ago, and one that vanished between
    that answer and this read holds nothing. THAT RACE IS THE ONLY WAY INTO THAT
    BRANCH, and it is why no test drives it: `tk-roster` lists a project only
    where the queue is already a plain file, and the TARGET queue's absence is
    refused further up `cmd_add` — `read` fails with "next-steps.md not found"
    before this gate is reached. The branch is kept for the race, not for a
    first `add` into a brand-new queue: that add never gets here.

    Only the TARGET queue's lock is held while this runs, and taking every
    queue's would need a locking order across projects — two sessions on two
    queues would deadlock on the day they took them in opposite orders. The
    reads are not torn: the queue files are published by rename, so a count is
    of one committed state or the next. What the missing locks cost is a sibling
    session adding to ANOTHER queue between this count and our write. The bound
    is not one item: every add that races this one counts the same free slot, so
    k of them land k-1 items above the cap, and only the NEXT add on any queue
    refuses. Measured, four adds on four queues with nine open against a cap of
    ten: thirteen open, three above. A cap held to the item by cross-project
    locking is not worth a deadlock in the command every session runs.
    """
    path = os.path.join(memdir, "next-steps.md")
    try:
        content = tk_site.read_text(
            path, "A queue file is Markdown written by `tk-queue`",
            " — the offending byte arrived with text pasted from another encoding")
    except tk_site.SiteError as e:
        fail(str(e))
    if content is None:
        return 0
    return sum(1 for kind, _ in split_blocks(content) if kind == "item-open")


def wip_queues(site, memdir):
    """Every queue the cap counts: the ROSTER's, plus the one being written.

    The roster is what closes the `--dir` bypass. A cap that counted one queue
    is walked around by pointing `--dir` at another queue on the same machine —
    and the WIP is the same WIP, since the same person works both. The answer
    comes from `tk-roster`, loaded on FIRST USE by `roster()` and not at import:
    which queues exist is its question, and a second sweep here would be a
    second cap.

    `queues` and `excluded_by`, NOT `sweep`: this gate needs the queues, and
    `sweep`'s other half recovers the directory to open a session in by walking
    the filesystem once per queue, which is a price every `add` would pay for a
    path the cap never reads. That half also DROPS the queues no single
    directory resolves to, and a queue nothing can dispatch holds open items all
    the same. The site file's `fleet-allow`/`fleet-deny` are honoured through
    `excluded_by`, so a project the machine was told not to touch is not counted
    against the work it is allowed to do.

    The target queue is appended because it need not be on the roster at all:
    `--dir` names any directory, and a cap that counted only the roster would
    wave an add into a queue outside `~/.claude/projects` past a full machine.
    Directories are compared by realpath, so the roster's spelling of one and
    `--dir`'s spelling of the same one count once. EACH QUEUE COMES BACK WITH
    THAT REALPATH, `(dir, key)`: the refusal has to ask "is this the queue being
    written" a second time, and asking it with a second `os.path.realpath` walk
    would put two definitions of "the same queue" in one gate — the caller could
    drift from the dedup above it and nothing would say so.
    """
    tk_roster = roster()
    allow, deny = tk_roster.lists(site)
    dirs = [d for name, d in tk_roster.queues(tk_roster.projects_root())
            if not tk_roster.excluded_by(name, allow, deny)]
    dirs.append(memdir)
    seen, out = set(), []
    for d in dirs:
        key = os.path.realpath(d)
        if key in seen:
            continue
        seen.add(key)
        out.append((d, key))
    return out


WIP_REMEDY = (
    'Make room by taking an item OUT, which is the only thing that does: '
    '`tk-queue done <id> --how "<outcome + pointer>"` for one that is finished, '
    '`tk-queue cancel <id> --why "<why>"` for one that is not going to happen '
    "(`tk-queue list` shows the IDs). A finding that has to survive this session "
    "and cannot become an item of its own goes into an item that already exists "
    '(`tk-queue edit <id> --text "..."`) or into that item\'s briefing '
    "(`tk-queue handoff <id> ...`) — neither writes a new item, and both are what "
    "a review prescribes."
)


def check_wip(site, memdir):
    """Refuse the add when the open items already fill the cap.

    AT the cap, not past it: the cap is how many items may be OPEN, so the add
    that would make them cap+1 is the one refused. `--force` is not a parameter
    of this function, deliberately — see the note above load_site.
    """
    cap = site.ceilings.get(WIP_KEY) if site else None
    per_queue = site.ceilings.get(PER_QUEUE_KEY) if site else None
    auto = bool(site and site.open_items_auto)
    if cap is None and per_queue is None and not auto:
        return
    root = roster().projects_root()
    if not os.path.isdir(root):
        # HOME resolves BOTH the site file and the roster, so a home carrying a
        # cap and no projects directory counts ONE queue and calls it the
        # machine. Not a bypass — a different HOME is a different machine's
        # configuration, cap included — but it may not shrink the count in
        # silence, which is the one thing a whitelist gate cannot afford.
        # REFUSING outright here was considered and declined: a fresh machine
        # that has chosen a cap and holds no project yet would be unable to open
        # its first item, and refusing closes nothing, since whoever chooses the
        # HOME chooses the site file the cap is written in
        print(f"tk-queue: {root} does not exist, so `{WIP_KEY}` is counted over this "
              "queue alone and not over the machine.", file=sys.stderr)
    counts = [(d, key, open_items(d)) for d, key in wip_queues(site, memdir)]
    total = sum(n for _, _, n in counts)
    target = os.path.realpath(memdir)
    mine = sum(n for _, key, n in counts if key == target)
    # THE PER-QUEUE CAP IS ASKED FIRST, and that order is the rule rather than a
    # detail of the writing. The total does not see CONCENTRATION — 194 of 280
    # open items sat in two queues on 2026-09-04 — so a machine can be a long way
    # under its total while the queue in front of the caller is the problem. Asked
    # second, this queue's refusal would arrive only once every OTHER queue had
    # been pruned too, which is the opposite of the pressure it exists to create.
    # And it is the actionable one: the items to close are in the queue the caller
    # is already in.
    if per_queue is not None and mine >= per_queue:
        fail(f"{mine} open item(s) in this queue against a per-queue cap of "
             f"{per_queue}, so no new item enters it:\n  {memdir}\n\nThe cap is "
             f"`{PER_QUEUE_KEY}` in {site.path}, one number for every queue on this "
             "machine's roster, and the room has to be made HERE — the machine may be "
             "far under its total while this queue is full, which is the concentration "
             "the per-queue cap exists to answer. Neither --force nor another --dir gets "
             "past it, and raising the number is the user's call, not this session's. "
             + WIP_REMEDY)
    # DERIVED after the queues are counted, never in the site reader: how many
    # queues exist is the roster's question, and `wip_queues` has just answered it
    # for this very gate. `auto` is `(per-queue - discount) x N` — never
    # `per-queue x N`, which is the worst case, every queue at its maximum at
    # once; the discount is what says some may be, not all.
    if auto:
        discount = site.ceilings.get(DISCOUNT_KEY, 0)
        cap = (per_queue - discount) * len(counts)
    if cap is None or total < cap:
        return
    # WHERE the work is, WITHOUT writing another project's name into THE
    # REFUSAL — which is the routine output, printed on a healthy machine every
    # time the cap bites. It is not a claim about the whole command: the
    # diagnosis in `open_items` names the sibling queue whose file cannot be
    # read, path and all, and deliberately — that message exists to be acted on,
    # and a reader cannot fix a file nobody names. That path fires only on a
    # broken queue file, never on the routine refusal. The reader has to pick an
    # item to close, and the queue
    # they can pick it from is the one they are in — that one is named. The rest
    # is a count plus the command that names them on the machine itself: a
    # project directory carries a client's or a company's name, and this text
    # travels into transcripts and pull request bodies, which is the leak
    # `tk-hygiene` was already written against.
    # Only the queues that hold something: a machine's empty queues would bury
    # the two lines that matter
    others = [(d, n) for d, key, n in counts if key != target and n]
    where = f"\n  {mine:>4}  {memdir}"
    if others:
        where += (f"\n  {sum(n for _, n in others):>4}  in {len(others)} other queue(s) "
                  "on this machine (`tk-roster` lists them there)")
    # the DERIVED total says where it came from, or the reader looks for a number
    # that is written nowhere in the file
    how = (f"`{WIP_KEY} = {tk_site.WIP_AUTO}` in {site.path}, which is "
           f"({per_queue} - {site.ceilings.get(DISCOUNT_KEY, 0)}) x {len(counts)} "
           "queue(s)" if auto else f"`{WIP_KEY}` in {site.path}")
    fail(f"{total} open item(s) against a cap of {cap}, so no new item enters the "
         f"queue:{where}\n\nThe cap is {how}, and the count sums "
         "every queue on this machine's roster — so neither --force nor another --dir "
         "gets past it, and raising the number is the user's call, not this session's. "
         + WIP_REMEDY)


def cmd_add(args):
    memdir = memory_dir(args.dir)
    if args.classe not in CLASSES:
        fail(f"--class must be one of {', '.join(CLASSES)}")
    ensure_single_line(text=args.text, effort=args.effort, risk=args.risk,
                       criterion=args.criterion, source=args.source, project=args.project,
                       deferred=args.deferred)
    ensure_filled(text=args.text, effort=args.effort, criterion=args.criterion)
    if args.deferred is not None:
        # the flag was typed: an empty justification is a deferral nobody can read,
        # and argparse's own check only proves the flag was there
        ensure_filled(deferred=args.deferred)
    # the gate, in both directions. `--deferred none` is refused with the omission:
    # `none` DELETES a field everywhere else in this script, so honouring it here
    # would write a DECISION carrying no deferral — the gate open by way of the one
    # word that means "no field"
    if args.classe == DEFERRABLE_CLASS and (args.deferred is None or clears_field(args.deferred)):
        fail(deferral_gate_message("add"))
    if args.deferred is not None and args.classe != DEFERRABLE_CLASS:
        fail(deferral_class_message(f"This add is --class {args.classe}."))
    ensure_no_embedded_marker(text=args.text, effort=args.effort, risk=args.risk,
                              criterion=args.criterion, source=args.source,
                              deferred=args.deferred)
    check_field_ceilings(args.force, effort=args.effort, risk=args.risk,
                         criterion=args.criterion, project=args.project, source=args.source,
                         deferred=args.deferred, repo=args.repo)
    if args.project:
        validate_project(args.project)
    validate_env(args.env)
    # the gate HANDS BACK the canonical form, and the item is composed from it:
    # a file that holds one spelling per reference is what makes every later
    # comparison a plain `==` again
    args.ticket = validate_ref("ticket", args.ticket)
    args.spec = validate_ref("spec", args.spec)
    args.blocked_by = validate_blocker(args.blocked_by)
    # returned UNCHANGED where the two above are canonicalised — validate_repo says why
    args.repo = validate_repo(args.repo)
    path = os.path.join(memdir, "next-steps.md")
    content = read(path)
    if content is None:
        fail("next-steps.md not found in " + memdir + " (first kickoff creates it)")
    # the WIP cap. It sits above `max_id` for reading order, not for safety:
    # `max_id` only READS, nothing is reserved, and a refusal below it would
    # leave no hole in the sequence — the claim that it would was written here
    # and was never true. `add --force` reaches this line exactly like every
    # other add: see the note above load_site
    check_wip(load_site(), memdir)
    iid = max_id(memdir) + 1
    item = compose_item(args, iid)
    check_ceiling(item, args.force)
    # after every rejection path: a warning about a tag the queue then refuses
    # announces an adoption that never happened
    if args.project:
        existing = open_project_tags(content)
        if args.project not in existing:
            used = ", ".join(sorted(existing)) if existing else "(none yet)"
            print(f"tk-queue: warning: new project tag '{args.project}' — "
                  f"tags already in use: {used}", file=sys.stderr)
    # insert after the last open/done item; else append at EOF
    blocks = split_blocks(content)
    last = max((i for i, (k, _) in enumerate(blocks) if k.startswith("item")), default=None)
    if last is None:
        content = content.rstrip("\n") + "\n\n" + item
    else:
        out = []
        for i, (_, text) in enumerate(blocks):
            out.append(text)
            if i == last:
                out.append(("" if text.endswith("\n\n") else "\n") + item)
        content = "".join(out)
    write_atomic(path, content)
    print(f"added T{iid:03d}: {item_title(item)}")


# --- handoff: the briefing that lives and dies with the item ---------------
# `handoff-<id>.md` sits in the queue's OWN directory, beside next-steps.md and
# done-log.md: the queue already resolves that address, so the briefing needs no
# new data dir and follows `--dir` for free. Naming it by ID is what makes a
# re-wrap-up idempotent — it overwrites, never accumulates — and it is what lets
# a close know which file belongs to which item without reading any of them.
#
# WHEN a full briefing is written instead of the default one-line handoff is not
# decided here. That is prose, and it lives in the wrap-up/kickoff skills; this
# script only refuses one that would be unreadable, and collects one nothing
# reaches any more.

# (flag dest, heading, mandatory). Fields 1-3 are the contract's mandatory half;
# 4-5 may be absent, and an absent optional field writes no section at all — an
# empty heading reads as "nothing known here", which is not the same claim.
HANDOFF_FIELDS = (
    ("objective", "Objective", True),
    ("state", "State", True),
    ("blockers", "Blockers and notes", True),
    ("skills", "Suggested skills", False),
    ("pitfalls", "Known pitfalls", False),
)
# A `[[handoff-T00N]]` link in an item's text is the briefing's ONLY discovery
# path — it is deliberately absent from MEMORY.md — so it is also the only thing
# that can tell a close whether some OTHER item still needs the file. A campaign
# is exactly that shape: one briefing named for the anchor item, pointed at by
# every item in the campaign.
#
# Two tolerances, both ONE-WAY — they can only make MORE items count as holding a
# briefing, never fewer, so each can DELAY a collection and neither can cause a
# wrong deletion:
#   digits    any count, not the writer's `{3,}` — measured: `[[handoff-T1]]` was
#             invisible here, and the close printed "handoff-T001.md removed" while
#             a second item still pointed at it. A link is written by a human or a
#             skill's prose, not by this script, so the reader may not demand the
#             writer's zero-padding.
#   position  anywhere in the block, prose and continuation lines included. Unlike
#             an ID (read only where a WRITER puts one), the link HAS no writer
#             position — #68 puts it in the item's text — so an item that merely
#             quotes the format holds the briefing until its own close, which then
#             collects it.
HANDOFF_REF_RE = re.compile(r"\[\[handoff-T([0-9]+)\]\]")
# Levels 1-2 FRAME the five fields; a value carrying one of its own moves a field
# boundary. Deeper headings are left alone on purpose: sub-structure inside a
# field is prose, and refusing it would make the file harder to write than the
# conversation it replaces.
HANDOFF_HEADING_RE = re.compile(r"^#{1,2} .*$", re.M)

# The stable shape, in the help because another session's prose reads this file.
# The sample is a REAL run — the suite composes it through the same command and
# asserts the file character for character — because a documented shape nothing
# executes is the one that goes stale first, and this one documents an AC.
HANDOFF_SAMPLE = """# Handoff T007 — the item's text

## Objective

ship the parser

## State

grammar merged in PR #12

## Blockers and notes

none
"""

HANDOFF_FORMAT = """output format (stable — the next session's prose reads this file). Written by

  tk-queue handoff 7 --objective "ship the parser" \\
      --state "grammar merged in PR #12" --blockers "none"

on an item whose text is "the item's text", and asserted character for character
by the suite:

""" + HANDOFF_SAMPLE + """
The file is `handoff-T007.md`, in the same directory as next-steps.md — the one
`--dir` resolves. Writing it again for the same ID OVERWRITES it: a re-run of the
wrap-up leaves one briefing, never two.

The five fields, in this order. 1-3 are mandatory and must carry text ('none'
written out is an answer; an empty string is refused). 4-5 are optional and are
omitted entirely when not given:

  --objective   where this front is going
  --state       what is done and decided, by path/URL, without duplicating it
  --blockers    blockers and notes — the field the next session rediscovers if it
                is missing, so 'none' is spelled out rather than left off
  --skills      skills the next session should reach for
  --pitfalls    known traps

A value may not carry a `# ` or `## ` heading of its own: those frame the fields,
and one inside a value moves a boundary — the composed file is read back and
refused when its headings are not the ones it was composed with. `###` or deeper
is sub-structure, and passes.

LIFECYCLE. The briefing dies with the item: `done`, `cancel`, `migrate` and an
`edit` that drops the last pointer to it all delete it in the same command. It
survives that close only while an OPEN item still reaches it — its own item, or one
pointing at it with `[[handoff-T007]]`, in the text or in any field — which is what
keeps a campaign's briefing alive until its last item closes. No path through this
CLI leaves behind a briefing this command WROTE and nothing reaches.

Only what this command wrote. A file already sitting at that name without a
`# Handoff T007` first line is never deleted and never overwritten — the write is
refused (`--force` overrides it) and the collection prints a warning and leaves it
where it is, because a memory dir holds unrelated files and `os.remove` is not
undone by re-running anything."""


def handoff_name(iid):
    return f"handoff-T{iid:03d}.md"


def handoff_path(memdir, iid):
    return os.path.join(memdir, handoff_name(iid))


def handoff_ours(memdir, n):
    """Whether the file at this briefing's name was written HERE.

    The name is the contract, but the name is also all `os.remove` needs, and a
    remove is not undone by re-running anything: measured — a note that happened to
    be called `handoff-T001.md`, in a memory dir that holds dozens of unrelated
    files, was deleted by closing T001, and this command never wrote it.

    The header is asked of the ID only, never of the item's text: an item edited
    after its briefing was written has a different title, and a briefing this
    command DID write must stay collectable. Unreadable counts as NOT ours — the
    safe direction, since the cost of keeping a stranger's file is a file kept.

    KNOWN LIMIT, under a DUPLICATE ID. Everything here addresses a briefing by ID,
    so two open items sharing one ID share a briefing: closing the first keeps the
    file (the second still reaches it) and the second then reads a briefing written
    about the first. The residue is the safe one — a file kept, never a wrong
    delete — and no refusal is offered because none could name a remedy that WORKS:
    `cancel <id>` resolves to the first occurrence too, and hand-editing the queue
    is what this script exists to replace. It is the duplicate ID that has to go.
    """
    try:
        with open(handoff_path(memdir, n), encoding="utf-8") as f:
            return f.readline().startswith(f"# Handoff T{n:03d}")
    except OSError:
        return False


def handoff_refs(block):
    """Every briefing ID the block points at."""
    return {int(m.group(1)) for m in HANDOFF_REF_RE.finditer(block)}


def compose_handoff(iid, block, values):
    """The file text — composed in full BEFORE anything is asked of it."""
    out = [f"# Handoff T{iid:03d} — {item_title(block)}\n"]
    for dest, heading, mandatory in HANDOFF_FIELDS:
        body = (values.get(dest) or "").strip()
        if not body and not mandatory:
            continue
        out.append(f"\n## {heading}\n\n{body}\n")
    return "".join(out)


def handoff_readback(text):
    """(heading line, the body under it) for every level-1/2 heading the composed
    briefing gives BACK — the gate itself, asked of the text that WOULD be written.

    Asking the flags instead is the guess: a --state whose second line reads
    `## Blockers and notes` passes every per-flag check and still writes a file
    whose reader finds the mandatory third field inside the second, with the real
    one below it — a briefing that validated and cannot be read. Only the
    composed text knows where the boundaries ended up, so only the composed text
    can be asked whether they are where they were put. Same shape as
    claim_readback, for the same reason.
    """
    marks = list(HANDOFF_HEADING_RE.finditer(text))
    out = []
    for i, m in enumerate(marks):
        end = marks[i + 1].start() if i + 1 < len(marks) else len(text)
        out.append((m.group(0), text[m.end():end].strip()))
    return out


def handoff_gate(candidate, iid, block, values):
    """Refuse a briefing the reader would not read as the contract."""
    composed = [f"# Handoff T{iid:03d} — {item_title(block)}"]
    composed += [f"## {h}" for d, h, mandatory in HANDOFF_FIELDS
                 if mandatory or (values.get(d) or "").strip()]
    got = handoff_readback(candidate)
    heads = [h for h, _ in got]
    if heads != composed:
        fail("the briefing does not read back as the five fields it was composed as.\n"
             f"  composed: {' | '.join(composed)}\n"
             f"  reads as: {' | '.join(heads)}\n"
             "A field value may not carry a '# ' or '## ' heading of its own — those "
             "frame the fields. Use '###' or deeper for sub-structure inside a field, "
             "and re-run. Nothing was written.")
    blank = [h for h, body in got[1:] if not body]
    if blank:
        empty = [f"--{d}" for d, h, _ in HANDOFF_FIELDS if f"## {h}" in blank]
        # the remedy is a COMMAND, so the flags are spelled as one — `--objective,
        # --state "none"` reads as a list and argparse refuses it for the very flag
        # it names. Two empty fields is the case the suite now runs
        remedy = " ".join(f'{f} "none"' for f in empty)
        verb = "carries" if len(empty) == 1 else "carry"
        fail(f"{', '.join(empty)} {verb} no text. The briefing's mandatory fields are "
             "the three the next session cannot work without, and an empty one is not "
             "'nothing to say' — it is a field nobody filled. Re-run spelling the empty "
             f"answer out: {remedy}. Nothing was written.")


def handoff_holders(content, n):
    """The labels of the OPEN items that still reach the briefing named for T<n>:
    the item it is named for, if it is still open, and every open item pointing at
    it. Labels, not IDs — and the label is the one `item_label` reads off the
    block, for the reason every other display reads it that way: `f"T{iid:03d}"`
    rebuilds the label from the NUMBER, so a holder spelled `T0001` in the file
    was reported as `T001`, and the reader who went looking for T001 found either
    nothing or a different item. An item `migrate` has not numbered yet holds a
    briefing exactly as much as a numbered one, and has no spelling to print."""
    out = []
    for kind, text in split_blocks(content):
        if kind != "item-open":
            continue
        iid = item_id(text)
        if iid == n or n in handoff_refs(text):
            # an item `migrate` has not numbered yet cannot be closed, claimed or
            # edited by ID — but it is OPEN and it points at this briefing, so it
            # HOLDS it. Skipping it was a wrong delete, the one direction this
            # reader may not fail in, so it is labelled rather than dropped (and
            # `f"T{None:03d}"` is the crash that skipping was hiding)
            out.append(item_label(text) if iid is not None else "an unnumbered item")
    return out


def handoff_candidates(iid, block):
    """The briefings ONE item can take with it: its own, and every one its text
    points at. An item with no ID (a legacy line `migrate` is closing) has no
    briefing of its own, and still carries pointers.

    This bound is what collects a campaign without ever touching a file the item
    has nothing to do with — a sweep of the directory would collect a briefing
    written seconds earlier for an item still to be claimed.
    """
    return handoff_refs(block) | ({iid} if iid is not None else set())


def handoff_collect(memdir, after, ids, dry_run=False):
    """Delete every briefing among `ids` that `after` no longer reaches, and say
    what became of each.

    `dry_run` suppresses the deletion and NOTHING else: the same lines are
    printed, so a preview's report is the report of the run it previews. Saying
    "would be removed" here would make the two reports differ in the one place a
    caller compares them, and the preview announces itself on stderr instead.

    `after` is the queue as the command will LEAVE it, passed in rather than
    re-read: asked of the queue as it was FOUND, the item being closed still
    reaches its own briefing and nothing is ever collected. Kept is reported too —
    a campaign's briefing outliving its anchor is a fact the caller acts on.

    Called LAST, after the command's own writes. Every order has a crash window;
    this one's residue is a file left behind, which is inert and removable by hand,
    while deleting first would destroy a briefing whose item is still open. Same
    rule the queue's two writes answer to: never lose, stay rerun-visible.
    """
    remove, kept = [], []
    for n in sorted(ids):
        if not os.path.exists(handoff_path(memdir, n)):
            continue
        if not handoff_ours(memdir, n):
            print(f"tk-queue: warning: {handoff_name(n)} was not written by "
                  f"`tk-queue handoff` (no `# Handoff T{n:03d}` header), so it is left "
                  f"alone: {handoff_path(memdir, n)}", file=sys.stderr)
            continue
        holders = handoff_holders(after, n)
        (kept if holders else remove).append((n, holders))
    for n, holders in kept:
        print(f"{handoff_name(n)} kept — still reached by " + ", ".join(holders))
    for n, _ in remove:
        # a plain remove, and deliberately not a best-effort one: everything that
        # reaches here is a regular readable file whose header was just read, this
        # command holds the queue's exclusive lock, and tk-queue is the only writer
        # of this directory. A directory or a stranger's file sitting at the name —
        # the case that argued for a try/except — is turned away by handoff_ours
        # above, and a guard no test can reach is the one that rots
        if not dry_run:
            os.remove(handoff_path(memdir, n))
        print(f"{handoff_name(n)} removed")


def cmd_handoff(args):
    memdir = memory_dir(args.dir)
    # an item that is not open cannot be handed off: the briefing would be an
    # orphan the moment it was written, and no close would ever collect it
    content, block, start = find_open_item(memdir, args.id)
    values = {dest: getattr(args, dest, None) for dest, _, _ in HANDOFF_FIELDS}
    candidate = compose_handoff(args.id, block, values)
    handoff_gate(candidate, args.id, block, values)
    path = handoff_path(memdir, args.id)
    existed = os.path.exists(path)
    # the write side of the same rule: overwriting a stranger's file that merely
    # shares the name is the same loss as deleting it, and `os.replace` also eats a
    # symlink sitting there. A re-write of OUR briefing is the idempotent case and
    # passes untouched
    if existed and not handoff_ours(memdir, args.id) and not args.force:
        fail(f"{handoff_name(args.id)} already exists and was not written by this "
             f"command (no `# Handoff T{args.id:03d}` header) — writing the briefing "
             f"would destroy it. Nothing was written. Move it out of the way, or "
             f"overwrite it on purpose with --force: {path}")
    write_atomic(path, candidate)
    print(("rewrote " if existed else "wrote ") + path)
    # the item is the briefing's only discovery path, and the only thing a close
    # reads to know a sibling still needs it. A warning, not a refusal: the link
    # is the caller's text to write, and refusing here would make the two commands
    # ordering-sensitive for no gain
    if args.id not in handoff_refs(block):
        # The text is quoted for a SHELL and never truncated. Both were measured
        # here: an item whose text carries a double quote printed a command the
        # shell splits somewhere else entirely, and item_title's default limit
        # turned the remedy for a long item into `edit --text "<half of it>…"` —
        # a remedy that runs, reports success, and eats the item's own text.
        link = f"[[handoff-T{args.id:03d}]]"
        fixed = f"{item_text(block, limit=10 ** 6)} {link}"
        # …and it carries --force when the item it rewrites would cross the BLOCK
        # ceiling. Measured: on a 719-char item the printed command was refused by
        # the very ceiling adding the link crosses — a remedy that runs only after
        # the caller repairs it is a remedy nobody checked. The estimate is the
        # block plus the link, and it can only OVER-state the result (item_title
        # collapses whitespace), so --force is printed needlessly before it is
        # ever omitted when needed
        forced = " --force" if len(block) + len(link) + 1 > CEILING else ""
        print(f"tk-queue: warning: T{args.id:03d} does not point at "
              f"[[handoff-T{args.id:03d}]] — the item is this briefing's only "
              f"discovery path. Add it with `tk-queue edit {args.id} --text "
              f"{shlex.quote(fixed)}{forced}`.", file=sys.stderr)


def finish_close(memdir, content, block, start, iid):
    """The QUEUE half of a close: the item leaves next-steps, and whatever
    briefing it left behind goes with it.

    Both routes out of `close_item` end here — the one that has just written the
    log line, and the replay that found it already written — so an interrupted
    close is finished by exactly the code that finishes an ordinary one.
    """
    remove_block(memdir, content, block, start)
    # excise is pure and remove_block recomputes it from these same three values,
    # so this is the queue that was just written — not a second read of it
    handoff_collect(memdir, excise(content, block, start),
                    handoff_candidates(iid, block))


def close_item(args, marker, outcome, marker_flag):
    memdir = memory_dir(args.dir)
    content, block, start = find_open_item(memdir, args.id)
    # the item's OWN spelling: the log line OUTLIVES the item and is the only
    # record left of it, so an ID re-rendered from the number writes a permanent
    # entry under a name no item ever carried — ungreppable, and readable as
    # another item's history
    label = item_label(block)
    log = read(os.path.join(memdir, "done-log.md")) or DONE_LOG_TEMPLATE
    # Asked BEFORE the flag guards below, and that order is the point: on a
    # replay the outcome is discarded unwritten, so a refusal over its shape or
    # its length would leave the queue half-closed with no command able to
    # finish it. The flags are reported as discarded instead.
    if interrupted_close(log, iid=args.id):
        warn_once(f"{label} already has a done-log entry and is still open in "
                  "next-steps.md — the queue half of a close a crash interrupted. "
                  "Finishing that half; the entry already in the log stands, and "
                  f"no second one is written (--{marker_flag} is discarded).")
        print(f"{label} → out of the queue; its done-log entry was already written")
        finish_close(memdir, content, block, start, args.id)
        return
    ensure_single_line(outcome=outcome, summary=args.summary)
    # --summary and the outcome share the log entry's FIRST line with the project
    # tag appended below, so a marker in them can be read as that tag. --note is
    # deliberately NOT guarded: it lands on continuation lines, which log_entry_tag
    # never reads — and a real note in this queue's log quotes "**Project:**" while
    # describing this very field, which is the case LOG_TAG_RE exists to tolerate
    ensure_no_embedded_marker(summary=args.summary, outcome=outcome)
    # the log line is a line too, and it is the record that OUTLIVES the item —
    # same ceiling, same reason. --note is the one that can legitimately carry
    # substance (it exists for work that left no pointer), which is what --force is for
    check_field_ceilings(args.force, **{marker_flag: outcome},
                         summary=args.summary, note=args.note)
    today = datetime.date.today().isoformat()
    # read the tag from the ORIGINAL block: item_text strips every field, and
    # --summary replaces the text wholesale, so by then the tag is already gone
    tag = marker_value(block, "Project", PROJECT_TAG_VALUE_RE)
    text = args.summary or item_text(block, limit=400)
    line = f"- {today} — {marker} — {label} {text} — {outcome}"
    if tag:
        # appended last so LOG_TAG_RE's end-of-line anchor reads this field, not
        # any earlier lookalike in the item's own text
        line += f" **Project:** {tag}."
    if args.note:
        line += "\n  " + args.note.replace("\n", "\n  ")
    append_done_line(memdir, log, line, today)
    # before the queue half, as it has always been: this line and the briefing's
    # own are ONE report, and the item leaving the log is what it leads with
    print(f"{label} → done-log as {marker} ({today})")
    finish_close(memdir, content, block, start, args.id)


def cmd_done(args):
    close_item(args, "FEITO", args.how, "how")


def cmd_cancel(args):
    close_item(args, "DESCARTADO", args.why, "why")


def deferral_for_edit(args, block):
    """What `edit` should do to the Deferred field: None (leave it), FIELD_CLEAR
    (remove it), or the new justification. Refuses every route to a DECISION item
    with no deferral on the record — the gate is on the RESULT, not on the flag,
    or each of these reaches by two commands what `add` refuses in one:

      --class DECISION with no deferral anywhere        (the #70 two-command bypass)
      --class DECISION --deferred none                  (set the class, delete the field)
      --deferred none on an item that stays a DECISION  (delete the field, keep the class)

    The gate fires on the CHANGE to DECISION, never on an unrelated edit of an item
    that already is one: every DECISION item in a real queue predates this field, and
    a gate that fired on `edit <id> --effort L` would make them all uneditable.
    """
    label = item_label(block)
    setting = args.deferred is not None and not clears_field(args.deferred)
    clearing = args.deferred is not None and clears_field(args.deferred)
    seg, stray = deferral_field(block)
    # Refused, not guessed — and only when the command's outcome depends on the
    # deferral (a --class or a --deferred). Reading a stray marker as the field
    # would let prose satisfy the gate; clearing it would delete that prose. An
    # edit that touches neither flag never consults the deferral, so it stays
    # allowed: over-refusing here would make the item uneditable instead.
    #
    # T165: since review#3 added the generic marker-outside-the-chain guard
    # further down in `cmd_edit`, all four call shapes below refuse WITHOUT this
    # one too (measured) — it is kept only because ITS message names the real
    # problem (the deferral's position) instead of the generic one.
    if stray and (args.classe or args.deferred is not None):
        fail(f"{label} carries a **Deferred:** marker away from the position a "
             "deferral is written in (right after **Class:**) — in the item's own prose, "
             "or on a continuation line. Nothing was changed: reading it as the field "
             "would let prose satisfy the gate, and clearing it would DELETE that prose. "
             "Close the item with `cancel` and re-add it clean.")
    has = seg is not None
    result_class = args.classe or chain_class(block)
    if args.classe == DEFERRABLE_CLASS and not (setting or (has and not clearing)):
        fail(deferral_gate_message("edit"))
    if setting and result_class != DEFERRABLE_CLASS:
        fail(deferral_class_message(
            f"{label} is {result_class or 'carrying no unambiguous class'}."))
    if clearing and result_class == DEFERRABLE_CLASS:
        fail(f"{label} would stay a {DEFERRABLE_CLASS} with no deferral on the "
             "record. A deferral is dropped by leaving the class: pass the new "
             "`--class` in the same command (the field goes with it).")
    if args.deferred is None and args.classe and args.classe != DEFERRABLE_CLASS and has:
        # a deferral left on an item that is no longer a DECISION is the stale-Risk
        # failure again: true when written, still read by every later filter
        print(f"tk-queue: {label} is no longer a {DEFERRABLE_CLASS} — its "
              "**Deferred:** field goes with the class", file=sys.stderr)
        return FIELD_CLEAR
    return args.deferred


def splice_item(memdir, content, block, start, new):
    """Write `new` in place of the block AT `start`.

    NEVER `content.replace(block, new, 1)`: an item's text is not unique in the
    file — one that documents the queue's own format quotes another item's line
    verbatim — and the substring search rewrites whichever copy comes first (see
    excise). The offset comes from find_open_item, which walks the blocks.

    Three callers now (`edit`, `claim`, `release`), one splice: the addressing bug
    above cost a real corruption once, and it is not a bug each command should get
    its own chance to reintroduce.
    """
    write_atomic(os.path.join(memdir, "next-steps.md"), splice(content, block, start, new))


def splice(content, block, start, new):
    """The file `splice_item` WRITES, without writing it — so a caller that must
    also reason about the result reads the same bytes the file gets, rather than
    recomposing them and drifting on the newline below."""
    if not new.endswith("\n"):
        new += "\n"
    return content[:start] + new + content[start + len(block):]


def cmd_edit(args):
    memdir = memory_dir(args.dir)
    content, block, start = find_open_item(memdir, args.id)
    label = item_label(block)
    ensure_single_line(text=args.text, effort=args.effort, risk=args.risk,
                       criterion=args.criterion, project=args.project,
                       deferred=args.deferred)
    if args.deferred is not None:
        ensure_filled(deferred=args.deferred)
    ensure_no_embedded_marker(text=args.text, effort=args.effort, risk=args.risk,
                              criterion=args.criterion, deferred=args.deferred)
    check_field_ceilings(args.force, effort=args.effort, risk=args.risk,
                         criterion=args.criterion, project=args.project,
                         deferred=args.deferred)
    if args.project:
        validate_project(args.project)
    validate_env(args.env)
    # before the deferral gate, which reasons about the resulting CLASS: a typo'd
    # class reaching the gate first is answered "T001 is DECISON — pass --class
    # DECISION", which sends the caller after the wrong mistake. `add` validates
    # the class first for the same reason
    if args.classe and args.classe not in CLASSES:
        fail(f"--class must be one of {', '.join(CLASSES)}")
    deferred_flag = deferral_for_edit(args, block)
    args.blocked_by = validate_blocker(args.blocked_by)
    # canonicalised the way `cmd_add` canonicalises it, and by the same gate: an
    # edit writing `Ambiente#0171` into a queue whose adds wrote `ambiente#171`
    # puts two spellings of one reference in one file, and the lane count that
    # compares them is a plain `==` only because no door writes the second one
    args.spec = validate_ref("spec", args.spec)
    new = block
    if args.text:
        head_m = CANONICAL_HEAD_RE.match(block)
        if not head_m:
            fail(f"{label} has a non-canonical head — use cancel + add instead of --text")
        # keep everything from the first READABLE field marker on, whichever field
        # that is — anchoring on Class alone would silently drop Effort/Risk/Source
        # of items that never carried a Class field.
        #
        # `markers`, not a regex of its own, and that is the whole code-span rule
        # reaching the writer that cuts the line. Measured on the shape this slice
        # exists to serve — an item whose text QUOTES a marker: the raw regex cut
        # the tail inside the code span, so the opening backtick stayed in the text
        # being replaced and the closing one did not. `--text` then wrote the
        # quotation back as a REAL marker sitting before the chain, duplicated the
        # prose around it, and pushed the item out of `pack` into the very
        # cancel-and-re-add dead end the rule exists to open a way out of.
        marks = markers(block)
        tail = block[marks[0][1]:] if marks else ""
        new = head_m.group(0) + args.text.strip() + (" " + tail if tail else "\n")
    flags = ((args.classe, "Class"), (args.effort, "Effort"),
             (args.risk, "Risk"), (args.env, "Env"),
             (args.criterion, "Criterion"),
             (args.project, "Project"), (deferred_flag, "Deferred"),
             (args.blocked_by, "Blocked-by"),
             # LAST, after every field that already had a flag: an absent field is
             # APPENDED at the end of the chain by this loop, so a new flag inserted
             # higher up would move where the older ones land on the same item
             (args.spec, "Spec"))
    promoted_names = []
    for flag, field in flags:
        if not flag:
            continue
        # the real field is the one in the item's FIELD CHAIN — never merely the
        # last marker in the block, which lands on a continuation note (see
        # field_chain) — and, among the chain, the one the POSITION rule reads: a
        # segment BEFORE the **Class:** the chain begins at is the item's own prose
        # wearing the field's shape (see real_fields). Ambiguity is refused, not
        # guessed: the caller fixes a refusal with one command, and cannot undo
        # deleted text.
        #
        # The count and the locator ask DIFFERENT questions on purpose. Counting over
        # the whole chain keeps a prose segment visible where it makes the command
        # ambiguous — the item that quotes **Project:** in its text AND carries a real
        # one — instead of letting the position rule quietly resolve it; the locator
        # is positional, so the same prose can never be the segment that gets written.
        in_chain = [f for f in field_chain(new) if f.canonical == field]
        found = real_fields(new, field)
        if len(in_chain) > 1:
            fail(f"{label} carries {len(in_chain)} **{field}:** fields in its field "
                 f"chain, so `--{field.lower()}` is ambiguous. Nothing was changed. Close "
                 "it with `cancel` and re-add it clean.")
        # `markers`, not a regex of its own: a marker the reader does not read is
        # not a marker this guard may refuse over. Quoted, it used to block
        # `--project tk` on an item that merely CITES the field and carries no real
        # one, prescribing cancel-and-re-add — the opposite of what quoting the
        # prose is for.
        if not found and markers(new, field):
            # the class-less item gets its own sentence, because the generic one
            # below would send the caller looking for a **Class:** the item has
            # not got. `--class` is exempt: it is the flag that GIVES the item
            # one, it is never located in the chain of an item that carries none,
            # and refusing it here would lock the repair out of its own population
            if field != "Class" and not real_fields(new, "Class"):
                fail(f"{label} names no **Class:** in its field chain, so nothing in that "
                     "chain can be told from the item's own prose wearing a field's name — "
                     f"`--{field.lower()}` would rewrite that prose (and with `none`, DELETE "
                     "it). Nothing was changed. Close the item with `cancel` and re-add it "
                     f"clean. `tk-queue edit {label} --class <CLASS>` is not the remedy "
                     "for this either: it gives the item a class, which is worth doing, and "
                     "the anchor it writes makes EVERY segment now ending that line a "
                     f"field — this **{field}:** included. Run it only where that whole run "
                     "really is fields; it names them when it does.")
            fail(f"{label} has a **{field}:** marker OUTSIDE its field chain — in "
                 "the item's own prose (before the **Class:** the chain begins at) or on "
                 "a continuation line — and none inside it. Nothing was changed. Editing "
                 "that marker would rewrite prose you did not point at (and on "
                 "`--risk none`, DELETE it). Close the item with `cancel` and re-add it "
                 "clean.")
        if field in CLEARABLE and clears_field(flag):
            # The same anchor rule as the append branch below, and the same
            # population — the class-less legacy item — reached by the branch that
            # takes an early `continue` and never gets there. Measured: `edit T009
            # --risk none` on an item with no **Class:** printed "T009 updated" and
            # wrote nothing, and so did `--env none` and `--deferred none`. Three
            # commands exiting 0 over a file none of them touched.
            #
            # A refusal, not "nothing needed clearing": `real_fields` answers [] for
            # EVERY field while the anchor is missing, so `found` being empty is not
            # the command reading an item that carries no Risk — it is the command
            # unable to look. Reporting the absence would be asserting what it could
            # not check, and this is the DELETING path, where being wrong is the
            # expensive direction. (The guard above already refuses the class-less
            # item that does carry the marker, naming the deletion; what reaches
            # here is the one that carries none, and it gets the same verdict for
            # the same reason.) It is also what the non-clearable flags do one
            # branch to the right, so every flag but `--class` — which is the one
            # that GIVES the anchor — now answers this item alike.
            if not real_fields(new, "Class"):
                fail(f"{label} names no **Class:** in its field chain, so no segment of "
                     f"that chain is a real **{field}:** for any reader — `--{field.lower()} "
                     f"{FIELD_CLEAR}` cannot tell an item that carries none from one whose "
                     "fields no gate may read, and it is the flag that DELETES. Nothing was "
                     f"changed. Run `tk-queue edit {label} --class <CLASS> "
                     f"--{field.lower()} {FIELD_CLEAR}` — one command, class first, and the "
                     "clear reads a chain that anchors.")
            if found:
                new = clear_field_segment(new, found[0])
            continue
        if found:
            # located and written through ONE parse: the reader that found the
            # segment and the writer that replaces it read the same structure,
            # which is what stops the two from answering differently
            item = parse_item(new)
            item.set_field(item.field_at(found[0].start()), field, flag)
            new = render_item(item)
        else:
            # A chain with no **Class:** anchors nothing, so a field appended to it
            # is a field no reader may ever honour: real_fields and qualified_fields
            # both answer [] while the anchor is missing, so what this would write
            # is a field no gate and no report may read — not later, NOW. Measured:
            # `--effort L` printed "updated", `--class AUTONOMOUS` printed "updated",
            # and `pack` went on showing the item's Effort as `?`. Since T169 the
            # second half of that no longer holds — the anchor is written AHEAD of
            # the chain, so a `--class` arriving afterwards does rescue what was
            # appended here — and the refusal stands on the first half alone: one
            # command with `--class` in it costs the caller nothing and never leaves
            # the item in a state its own reports cannot see. The refusal above
            # covers the item that carries the marker already; this covers the one
            # that carries no marker at all — the class-less legacy item.
            if field != "Class" and not real_fields(new, "Class"):
                fail(f"{label} names no **Class:** in its field chain, so an appended "
                     f"**{field}:** is a field no gate and no report may read. Nothing was "
                     "changed. Run "
                     f"`tk-queue edit {label} --class <CLASS> --{field.lower()} "
                     # the caller's own value, whole and quoted: a remedy that says
                     # <value> makes every caller retype what they just typed, and a
                     # remedy built from a truncated one repairs something else
                     f"{shlex.quote(flag)}` — one command, class first, and the field "
                     "lands inside the chain.")
            if field == "Class":
                # THE FOLD COMES FIRST. With no chain on the first line the anchor
                # is APPENDED there (write_class_segment), and the item's real
                # fields are on a continuation line — so the write leaves them
                # exactly where no reader may use them, and takes the last route
                # out with it: `fold_chain_onto_first_line` returns early on an
                # item whose chain already reaches a **Class:**, and `edit --text`
                # anchors its tail on the first marker in the block, so neither
                # folds the item afterwards. Measured, each step exit 0: `pack`
                # excluded the item and printed `edit <id> --class AUTONOMOUS` as
                # the repair, that command took it, and `pack` then listed the
                # item ELIGIBLE with Effort `?` — a candidate for an unattended
                # package whose own fields nothing can read, with `cancel` +
                # re-add the only way back. Refused before the write, because
                # after it the refusal has nothing left to protect.
                if fields_off_first_line(new):
                    fail(f"{label} carries field markers OFF its first line, so the "
                         "class would be appended to a line with no chain on it while "
                         "the item's real fields stay where no gate and no report may "
                         "read them — and the anchor then stops `migrate` from folding "
                         "them up, which is the only command that would have. Nothing "
                         "was changed. Fold first, then give it the class: `tk-queue "
                         f"migrate` (every item carrying this shape in one pass, and it "
                         "names the ones it could not fold), then `tk-queue edit "
                         f"{label} --class {flag}`. Where the fold does not lift this — "
                         "a marker and its value on different lines — close the item "
                         "with `cancel` and re-add it clean.")
                # Composed FIRST, then handed to the same reader every gate uses.
                # Asking the block as it ARRIVED whether it looks right is the proxy
                # this file has already paid for (claim_readback): appending the
                # anchor CHANGES the chain the question is about. What is required
                # back is exact — the anchor, then the run that was already there,
                # in that order — because "a Class is in the chain somewhere" is
                # true of the write this slice exists to stop.
                candidate, promoted = write_class_segment(new, flag)
                back = [f.canonical for f in field_chain(candidate)]
                if back != ["Class"] + promoted or chain_class(candidate) != flag:
                    fail(f"{label} would not read back as a {flag} item carrying "
                         f"{len(promoted)} field(s) after the anchor. Nothing was "
                         "changed. Close it with `cancel` and re-add it clean.")
                # A PROMOTION ENDS THE CALL. Every segment the anchor just made
                # readable is a REAL field for the rest of this loop, so a later
                # flag finds and rewrites it — and the run the anchor leads is the
                # one run this command cannot tell from the item's own prose. Both
                # directions measured on one item, in one command, exit 0: `edit
                # T033 --class AUTONOMOUS --project tk` overwrote four words of the
                # title, and `--risk none` DELETED the imitating segment whole. The
                # warning below fires at the same moment and cannot substitute for
                # this: its remedy is `cancel` + re-add, advice that arrives after
                # the only copy of the text is gone. The class still goes in — it
                # is worth doing, it is what the refusal for every other flag on
                # this population already prescribes — but ALONE, so the caller
                # reads the warning before the flag that would rewrite what it
                # names. Nothing is written here: the whole call is refused, and
                # the caller re-runs it in the two commands the message prints.
                others = [f for f, name in flags if f and name != "Class"]
                if promoted and others:
                    names = ", ".join(f"**{n}:**" for n in promoted)
                    fail(f"{label} already ended its first line with {names}, so the "
                         "class goes AHEAD of that run and every segment in it becomes a "
                         "real field — including any that is the item's own prose quoting "
                         "a marker, which this command cannot tell apart. The other flags "
                         "in this call would then rewrite that run (and with "
                         f"`{FIELD_CLEAR}`, DELETE part of it). Nothing was changed. Run "
                         f"`tk-queue edit {label} --class {flag}` alone, read the segments "
                         "it names on stderr, and run the rest only if that whole run "
                         "really is fields — `cancel` + re-add is the repair if it is not.")
                new = candidate
                promoted_names = promoted
                continue
            # append at the end of the FIRST line, where the chain lives. Appending
            # after a continuation line would put the field outside the chain — i.e.
            # write, right now, the very shape the guard above refuses to touch later
            new = append_to_first_line(new, f"**{field}:** {flag}.")
    # The BLOCK ceiling guards the item's prose, so every PROSE flag is measured
    # against it — --text and the free-text fields alike — and only when the edit
    # grows the item, since shrinking a legacy oversized item must always be allowed.
    #
    # Exempt: the short fields (--class/--effort/--project), and ONLY they. That
    # exemption is T071 and it is what the ceiling costs: gating them meant a legacy
    # oversized item (T001, T002, T013 in the real queue) needed --force merely to
    # gain a project tag, which trains the caller to type --force on edits and
    # disarms the guard where it does matter. Their own small field ceiling is what
    # bounds them, and since a field is REPLACED when present, repeated edits cannot
    # accumulate — the exemption's total cost is bounded by 3 short fields.
    #
    # Both halves of this rule were bypasses before they existed, each measured:
    # one free-text field alone took a 100-char item to 1014 chars (no --force), and
    # after the per-field ceiling closed that, three free-text fields at 199 chars in
    # ONE call still took an item to 709 past a 700 ceiling.
    if any(getattr(args, f, None) for f in FREE_TEXT_FLAGS) and len(new) > len(block):
        check_ceiling(new, args.force)
    splice_item(memdir, content, block, start, new)
    # AFTER the write, and that is the rule the rest of this file already keeps:
    # compose, check, act. Printed before it, this announced a promotion that
    # `check_ceiling` then refused — the caller reading a past-tense warning about
    # a write that never happened, exit 1, file untouched.
    if promoted_names:
        names = ", ".join(f"**{n}:**" for n in promoted_names)
        print(f"tk-queue: warning: {label} already ended its first line with "
              f"{names}, so the class went AHEAD of that run — the one position "
              "where a gate reads it. Check the list: a segment there that is "
              "the item's own prose quoting a marker now counts as a field, and "
              "`cancel` + re-add is the repair for that.", file=sys.stderr)
    print(f"{label} updated")
    # An edit is the only command other than a close that changes WHICH briefings
    # an item reaches, and dropping the last pointer to one is a closing path in
    # disguise. Measured before this existed: anchor closed (its briefing kept for
    # the sibling), the sibling's --text rewritten without the link, the sibling
    # closed — and the briefing outlived an EMPTY queue, with nothing left that
    # could ever carry the pointer that collects it.
    dropped = handoff_refs(block) - handoff_refs(new)
    if dropped:
        handoff_collect(memdir, splice(content, block, start, new), dropped)


def cmd_claim(args):
    """Take the item, so a sibling session on the same queue does not work it too.

    The check and the write happen under the SAME queue lock every mutating command
    takes (see main), and that is what makes "exactly one of two racing sessions
    wins" true rather than merely likely: find_open_item's read is authoritative
    while the lock is held, so no second writer can land between reading that the
    item is free and marking it.

    A second claim is refused even when it comes from the SAME owner. Letting it
    through would only refresh the timestamp, and the timestamp is how a reader
    tells a live claim from one a dead session left behind — a claim that renews
    itself on every attempt never looks stale.
    """
    memdir = memory_dir(args.dir)
    validate_owner(args.owner)
    content, block, start = find_open_item(memdir, args.id)
    label = item_label(block)
    held = claim_segment(block)
    if held is not None:
        fail(f"{label} is already {claim_mark(*claim_value(held))}. Nothing was "
             "changed: a claim is how two sessions on one queue avoid executing the same "
             "item. Take another one — or, if that session is gone, hand this one back "
             f"with `tk-queue release {label}`.")
    stamp = now_stamp()
    # appended at the end of the FIRST line, where the chain lives — the same place
    # `edit` puts a field it did not find, and the only place claim_segment reads
    # one back from. The class is the one field NOT written here: it is the anchor
    # the rest are measured from, so it goes ahead of the run (write_class_segment)
    new = append_to_first_line(
        block, f"**Claimed:** {args.owner}{CLAIM_SINCE}{stamp}.")
    # THE gate, and it is asked of what would be WRITTEN — compose first, then require
    # the reader to find exactly the claim composed. Every proxy for this question
    # (does the chain carry a Class?) is a guess about a chain the write itself
    # changes, and three of them in a row let the same bug through. AFTER the stray
    # and ambiguity refusals, which are the terminal ones: preempting those printed a
    # remedy the caller ran, MUTATING the file, with the real refusal landing anyway.
    if len(claim_readback(new)) != 1:
        fail(claim_unwritable_message(block, new))
    # NOT measured against the BLOCK ceiling, on the same rule as the short fields
    # (T071): the value is bounded by construction — OWNER_RE plus a fixed-width
    # stamp — and a legacy oversized item is precisely the one a session must be
    # able to take without being taught to type --force on ordinary commands.
    splice_item(memdir, content, block, start, new)
    print(f"{label} claimed by {args.owner} ({stamp})")


def cmd_release(args):
    """Hand a claimed item back to the queue WITHOUT closing it.

    Does not demand the owner's name, deliberately: a session that died holding a
    claim would otherwise leave the item unreachable by anyone, which is the
    failure mode of a lock with no timeout — and this queue's whole point is that
    nothing goes silently unreachable. What keeps a wrongful release visible is the
    report below, which names WHOSE claim was dropped.
    """
    memdir = memory_dir(args.dir)
    content, block, start = find_open_item(memdir, args.id)
    label = item_label(block)
    # No host check here, and the asymmetry is the point: WRITING a claim needs a
    # chain that can hold one, READING one does not. A chain-less item carrying a
    # marker is already answered by the stray guard inside claim_segment, and one
    # carrying none simply holds no claim — which is what the honest no-op below
    # says. Demanding a host of `release` would refuse the second population a
    # diagnosis about a claim it does not have.
    held = claim_segment(block)
    if held is None:
        # an honest no-op, like `bump` on an item already at the top. Reporting a
        # release that did not happen is what would send the caller on to work an
        # item somebody else is still holding
        print(f"{label} carries no claim — nothing to release")
        return
    mark = claim_mark(*claim_value(held))
    splice_item(memdir, content, block, start, clear_field_segment(block, held))
    print(f"{label} released — it was {mark}")


def cmd_bump(args):
    """Move an item to the TOP of the file's global order.

    Priority IS that order — no score, no hidden heuristic — so this command is
    the only way it changes: `add` puts a new item at the end, and the package
    modes take the filtered top. Top means "before the first item block", the
    mirror of what `add` does at the other end; a `##` heading the item lands
    under is cosmetic (`list` groups by the **Project:** field, never by the
    heading), and everything that is not the item's own block — frontmatter,
    headings, prose between items — is preserved verbatim.
    """
    memdir = memory_dir(args.dir)
    content, block, start = find_open_item(memdir, args.id)
    label = item_label(block)
    path = os.path.join(memdir, "next-steps.md")
    first = ITEM_START.search(content)
    if first and first.start() == start:
        # already there: return without writing, so a no-op bump cannot churn the
        # file's spacing (repeat it ten times and the file is still byte-identical)
        print(f"{label} is already at the top of the queue")
        return
    without = excise(content, block, start)
    dest = ITEM_START.search(without)
    at = dest.start() if dest else len(without)
    tail = without[at:]
    # separate it from the item it now precedes, the way `add` separates the item
    # it follows — unless the block already ends in a blank line, or there is no
    # tail to separate from
    moved = block if block.endswith("\n\n") or not tail else block + "\n"
    write_atomic(path, without[:at] + moved + tail)
    print(f"{label} → top of the queue")


def cmd_report(args):
    dirs = (
        sorted(
            d
            for d in (os.path.join(PROJECTS_ROOT, p, "memory") for p in os.listdir(PROJECTS_ROOT))
            if os.path.exists(os.path.join(d, "done-log.md"))
        )
        if args.all
        else [memory_dir(args.dir)]
    )
    entry = re.compile("^" + LOG_LINE + r".*(?:\n  .*)*", re.M)
    for d in dirs:
        content = read(os.path.join(d, "done-log.md"))
        if content is None:
            continue
        lines = [
            m.group(0).rstrip("\n")
            for m in entry.finditer(content)
            if not args.since or m.group(1) >= args.since
        ]
        if not lines:
            continue
        proj = os.path.basename(os.path.dirname(d))
        print(f"\n### {proj}")
        # two different axes: `###` is the memory dir (one per project repo),
        # `####` the --project tag WITHIN it — the root queue mixes several
        grouped = group_by_tag([(log_entry_tag(ln), ln) for ln in lines])
        if grouped is None:
            print("\n".join(lines))     # no tag anywhere: identical to before
            continue
        for n, (tag, entries) in enumerate(grouped):
            if n:
                print()
            print(f"#### {tag or 'no project'}")
            print("\n".join(entries))


# --- the fold: a field chain that sits off the first line, lifted onto it ---
# A real minority of the open items across the real queues carry the LEGACY
# shape — a fraction measured once, at write time, and never a fixed count:
# the queues keep growing, so a number written here would go stale on its own
# — the item's text on the first line and its `**Field:** value.` chain on a
# continuation line. field_chain reads the run that ENDS THE FIRST LINE and
# nothing else (its docstring says why), so to every gate those items have no
# fields at all: `pack` excludes them, `claim` refuses them, every per-field
# `edit` is refused on them. `migrate` is where the repair belongs — it is
# already the command that rewrites legacy shapes into what the readers
# expect, and the per-item alternative is retyping the item's whole text into
# `edit --text`, once per item, by hand.

# printed with the IDs it applies to, never per item: the shapes each covers are
# several and the sentences are long. TWO reasons, not one: a report that melted
# them into a single line would name the split-chain item under a sentence about
# guessing values, and the reader repairs the wrong thing
FOLD_REFUSAL = "folding would have to GUESS which text is a field value"
FOLD_SPLIT_REFUSAL = ("a **Field:** marker would stay off the first line, and a chain "
                      "the fold only half lifts is not a chain any gate can read")
# "a line the join would absorb", the same words FOLD_HARDBREAK_REFUSAL locates
# its line with, and not "a line between the head and the chain": that geometry
# is the WALK's alone. The wrapped path joins a chain that shares its line with
# the prose it wrapped out of, so there is nothing between the head and the
# chain there — the refused line often IS the one carrying the chain. Measured
# on a sibling queue's T010, which read the sentence, looked for a line that
# does not exist in its item, and could not tell which line to repair. The
# reason was right and only the address was written for the older path.
FOLD_PROSE_REFUSAL = ("a line the join would absorb is not the hard-wrapped prose the fold "
                      "may take, and absorbing a shape nobody recognised is how structure "
                      "is lost in silence")
FOLD_PROSE_MARKER_REFUSAL = (
    "a **Field:** marker sits outside the chain the join would produce, so the fold "
    "would promote the item's own prose to a field — quote that marker in a code "
    "span (`**Class:**`) and the fold reads the rest as the chain it is")
FOLD_HARDBREAK_REFUSAL = (
    "a line the join would absorb ends in a HARD line break (two spaces, or a "
    "backslash), which is a break the author wrote and the join cannot carry — the "
    "item is left with its rendering intact")
FOLD_SETEXT_REFUSAL = (
    "a setext underline promotes the WHOLE paragraph above it, and the join would "
    "absorb part of that paragraph into the first line and leave the rest under the "
    "underline — half a heading in each place")
FOLD_BLOCK_REFUSAL = ("a continuation line OPENS a Markdown block, and the wrapped fold has "
                      "no chain to lift over it — joining would flatten a list the author "
                      "wrote, so it declines instead of choosing which lines survive")
# the two refusals the LIFT owns — the shape where the first line already carries
# a chain the gates read, so the run is relocated onto it and nothing between the
# two is absorbed. Neither may borrow FOLD_HARDBREAK_REFUSAL's sentence: that one
# addresses a line the join ABSORBS, and here the line keeps its place. Naming a
# geometry the item does not have is the defect C-15 repaired.
FOLD_SECOND_CLASS_REFUSAL = (
    "the lift would put a SECOND **Class:** in the chain, and a chain naming two "
    "classes is one no gate reads at all — the item is left with the class it "
    "already has")
FOLD_ORPHAN_BREAK_REFUSAL = (
    "the line the field is lifted out from under ends in a HARD line break (two "
    "spaces, or a backslash), and the lift leaves that break with nothing to break "
    "before — the item is left with its rendering intact")


# every line-shape that OPENS a Markdown block, from CommonMark's block-start
# set: a list item, an ordered item, a heading, a quote, a fence, a table row, a
# thematic break, raw HTML. A line that opens none of these and continues the
# paragraph above renders identically joined or wrapped — that is the whole
# licence the join has.
#
# What this list is NOT is a safety net. It was written as one — "a shape missing
# from it merely keeps its own line, nothing is lost" — and that sentence was
# false in the direction that mattered: a shape missing from it was ABSORBED, so
# every block shape not enumerated here reproduced the flattening the enumeration
# was written to stop. Four were found by reading, in one round: a setext
# underline, a link reference definition, a footnote definition, and a GFM table
# whose head row carries no leading pipe. The four are enumerated below, and the
# fifth nobody has read yet is what `absorption_audit` exists for.
BLOCK_START_RE = re.compile(r"""
      [-*+][ \t]           # a bullet
    | [0-9]+[.)][ \t]      # an ordered item
    | \#{1,6}[ \t]        # a heading
    | >                    # a quote
    | ```|~~~              # a fence
    | \|                   # a table row
    | (?:-[ \t]*){3,}$     # a thematic break, in its three spellings
    | (?:\*[ \t]*){3,}$
    | (?:_[ \t]*){3,}$
    | <                    # raw HTML
""", re.X)


# the four shapes the CommonMark set above does not reach, each one measured
# FLATTENED and reported as folded by the round that wrote that set
SETEXT_UNDERLINE_RE = re.compile(r"\A(?:=+|-+)[ \t]*\Z")
# `[label]: destino` and `[^label]: nota` — a link reference definition and a GFM
# footnote definition. `[[wiki-link]]) segue a frase` is NOT one of these and must
# not be read as one: real wrapped prose opens that way in the queues
REFERENCE_DEF_RE = re.compile(r"\A\[\^?[^\]\n]*\]:")
# a GFM delimiter row — the line that turns the row ABOVE it into a table head,
# and the only thing that tells `col A | col B` from a sentence with a pipe in it
TABLE_DELIMITER_RE = re.compile(r"\A\|?[ \t]*:?-+:?[ \t]*(?:\|[ \t]*:?-+:?[ \t]*)+\|?\Z")


def opens_a_block(lines, j):
    """Whether `lines[j]` OPENS a Markdown block instead of continuing the
    paragraph above it — asked with the NEXT line in hand, because two shapes
    cannot be told without it.

    True keeps the line where the user wrote it, with its own indentation. The
    answer is a shape question and nothing else; whether an absorption is
    LICENSED is a second question, asked by `absorption_audit`, which is
    deliberately not this function and does not consult it.

    Both lookaheads read the SAME relationship from the two sides it has. A GFM
    delimiter row turns the row above it into a table head; a setext underline
    turns the line above it into a heading. Reading only the line that carries the
    marker — which is what this did — protects the marker and loses what it marks:
    the underline was kept, the title above it passed both of `absorption_audit`'s
    licences (plain prose by its first character, past the wrap column by its
    geometry) and was absorbed into the head, so the item came out with its title
    merged into unrelated prose AND an underline left underlining nothing, reported
    as folded. Measured, on a file with no other copy.

    Which reading `---` gets, said rather than left to the next reader: directly
    under a line of paragraph text it is a setext H2, and after a blank or at the
    start of the run it is a thematic break — the precedence CommonMark gives it,
    and the one taken here. The ambiguity costs nothing either way, because both
    readings answer True for the `---` LINE (the thematic break through
    BLOCK_START_RE, the underline through SETEXT_UNDERLINE_RE). It decides only
    what happens to the line ABOVE, and the lookahead below is reached only when
    that line is paragraph text — a blank and every enumerated block shape have
    already returned True above it — which is exactly the condition under which the
    setext reading is the right one.

    What it does NOT reach, and the limit is the same one the head line already
    has: an underline promotes the WHOLE paragraph above it, so a title hard-wrapped
    over two lines keeps only its last line and the earlier ones are still absorbed.
    """
    line = lines[j]
    stripped = line.strip()
    indent = len(line) - len(line.lstrip())
    if not stripped:
        return True                      # split_blocks ends the item at a blank line
    if indent >= 4 or "\t" in line[:indent]:
        return True                      # an indented code block
    if BLOCK_START_RE.match(stripped):
        return True
    if SETEXT_UNDERLINE_RE.match(stripped) or REFERENCE_DEF_RE.match(stripped):
        return True
    nxt = lines[j + 1] if j + 1 < len(lines) else ""
    if SETEXT_UNDERLINE_RE.match(nxt.strip()):
        return True                      # this line is the heading that underlines
    return "|" in stripped and bool(TABLE_DELIMITER_RE.match(nxt.strip()))


# CommonMark's hard line break, in BOTH its spellings — the author asking for a
# `<br>` where a wrap would render a space. Two or more spaces ending the line,
# and a backslash ending it: the spec gives the second as the visible spelling
# of the first, for exactly the reason that trailing spaces are invisible in an
# editor, and an author who wants the break to SHOW in the source writes it that
# way. Reading only the spaces meant the visible spelling died at the join in
# silence, under a line reporting the item as folded — the same defect the
# spaces arm exists to stop, on the shape an author who cared enough to be
# explicit wrote.
#
# Never a tab, in either arm: the spec counts spaces, and a tab there is
# trailing whitespace nobody meant as anything.
#
# What this OVER-refuses is a line ending in an escaped backslash (`\\`), which
# renders as a lone backslash and breaks nothing, and a Windows path that ends a
# line. Both are the recoverable direction — a refusal costs one command, and a
# flattened queue file has no other copy — and neither is worth counting
# backslashes for.
#
# It is not in BLOCK_START_RE because it opens no block; it is a property of the
# break ABOVE the next line, which is why `absorption_audit` and not
# `opens_a_block` is what asks about it.
HARD_BREAK_RE = re.compile(r"(?: {2,}|\\)\Z")


def promoted_by_setext(lines, j):
    """Whether `lines[j]` is paragraph text that a setext underline directly below
    turns into a heading — the ONE reason `opens_a_block` stops the walk that the
    fold may not simply obey.

    `opens_a_block` answers True for that line, and the walk then keeps it and
    everything under it. That is the right answer when the line is the whole
    paragraph. It is the wrong one when the paragraph runs FURTHER UP, because the
    underline promotes all of it and the walk absorbs the earlier lines into the
    head — half the heading on the first line, half still under the underline. The
    limit was declared in `opens_a_block` and had no test; this is the question
    that makes it decidable.

    The shape question is asked of the same function with the LOOKAHEAD removed —
    `lines[: j + 1]` leaves no next line for it to read — rather than by respelling
    what paragraph text is. A second spelling of that is how a shape one of them
    enumerated stops being a shape to the other.
    """
    nxt = lines[j + 1] if j + 1 < len(lines) else ""
    return (bool(SETEXT_UNDERLINE_RE.match(nxt.strip()))
            and not opens_a_block(lines[: j + 1], j))


# the column below which a line break was the AUTHOR's and not a wrapper's. 72 is
# the narrowest column anyone hard-wraps prose to; every break the fold absorbs
# across the real queues is 93 columns or wider, and the flattened shapes measured
# by hand break at 55 and below. Character count, not display width: the margin
# between 72 and 93 is many times what a wide glyph miscounts.
WRAP_COLUMN_FLOOR = 72
# what a hard-wrapped line may OPEN with. Read off the corpus, not guessed: across
# every continuation line the fold absorbs in the real queues, the first character
# is a letter, a digit, a code span, an opening parenthesis or bracket, a bold
# marker, a quote or an emoji — and nothing else. `!!! nota`, `: definicao`, `$$`,
# `%%`, `+++`, `===` and `--- | ---` are none of those.
PROSE_OPENERS = "`([*\"'“”‘’«»"
# the one residue shape the whitelist above cannot refuse, because it opens the
# way a sentence opens: a `chave: valor` metadata line — loose YAML, a pandoc or
# PHP-Extra definition, a `Fonte:` / `Status:` note the author gave its own line.
# The whitelist refuses `!!! nota`, `:::`, `$$`, `%%`, `+++`, `===` and `--- | ---`
# by their first character; this one is letters, so only the SHAPE of the line
# betrays it. Measured flattened, and reported as folded, under a head long enough
# for the geometry licence to pass — which is the whole residue T168 decided.
#
# Enumerated here, and not in `opens_a_block`, on purpose: it is not a claim about
# what Markdown DOES with the line. CommonMark reads it as paragraph text, so a
# real parser would absorb it exactly as the audit did — the reason a parser was
# weighed for this and did not win. It is the audit refusing a break the AUTHOR
# made rather than a wrapper.
#
# The key is ONE token and the colon must end the line or be followed by
# whitespace, which is what keeps `https://exemplo.com` and `14:30` out — in
# neither does a space follow the colon.
#
# The shape alone is NOT enough, and the corpus said so before this shipped: the
# rule as first written fired on a real item (T004 of another project's
# queue), whose wrapped sentence happens to resume `wiki: `_wiki/...`` at 92
# columns. No fold was lost there — that item is refused for another reason
# anyway — but the REASON printed was wrong, and a reader repairing an item by a
# report that calls its prose a structure repairs the wrong thing. So the caller
# also requires the line to be one the AUTHOR ended — its own width stopping
# short of WRAP_COLUMN_FLOOR. A metadata line is short by
# construction; a wrapped line that merely starts with a word and a colon runs to
# the wrap column, and is prose. Both readings are geometry, and neither can be
# influenced by what anybody did or did not enumerate.
#
# What survives as over-refusal is a SHORT wrapped line that resumes with
# `palavra: ` — the last line of a paragraph, say. That is one item repaired by
# hand, the recoverable direction, and the same default the rest of this audit
# inverted: a refusal costs one command, a flattened queue file has no other copy.
METADATA_LINE_RE = re.compile(r"\A[^\W\d_][\w-]{0,31}:(?:[ \t]|\Z)")


def absorption_audit(lines, j):
    """Why the fold may NOT absorb `lines[1:j]`, or None when it may.

    The second question, and the point of it is that it is not the first. The
    round before this one classified each line by SHAPE and then re-asked the same
    shape question as a readback, which could not fail: a shape the classifier did
    not recognise was absorbed, and the readback counted zero block-openers on
    both sides and agreed. A readback that re-asks the question that made the
    decision protects the decisions it was written for and nothing else.

    So this asks three questions `opens_a_block` cannot answer for itself, and a NO
    from any of them refuses the whole item rather than flattening it:

      geometry  the break above the line is one a WRAPPER would make — the line
                above plus this line's first word overruns WRAP_COLUMN_FLOOR.
                Arithmetic on line lengths; no shape enters it, so a shape nobody
                enumerated is still caught whenever the author put it on a line
                they chose to break, which is what structural lines are.
      opening   the line begins the way hard-wrapped prose begins, by a whitelist
                read off the corpus. It is what refuses the whole family of rules,
                underlines, fences and delimiters at once — `===`, `$$`, `:::`,
                `+++`, `%%`, `--- | ---`, `!!! nota` — enumerated or not, because
                none of them opens with a character a sentence opens with.
      metadata  the line is not a SHORT `chave: valor` (METADATA_LINE_RE, plus a
                width the author and not a wrapper chose). The one structural shape
                the whitelist cannot reach, because it opens with a letter; it
                needed its own question and gets one.
      hard break no absorbed line ends in one. Two trailing spaces are the author
                asking Markdown for a line break where a wrap renders a space, so
                the join does not merely relocate the text — it changes how the
                item RENDERS, in the user's own prose, and reports the item as
                folded while doing it. There is no preserving answer: a join is
                exactly the operation that destroys a line break. Asked over the
                lines the join touches that have a line under them — a break at the
                very end of the block breaks nothing. That range starts at the HEAD
                line, index 0, and not at 1 like the geometry loop below it: the
                head is joined ONTO rather than absorbed, but the first absorbed
                line lands right after its break, so the break dies there too.

    What it does NOT catch, said plainly rather than left for the next reader to
    measure: a shape that is unenumerated AND opens like a sentence AND is not a
    `chave: valor` AND sits past the wrap column. `chave: valor` under a full line
    was the realistic member of that set and is now refused; what is left is a
    shape nobody in this corpus has written, and the reason it is TOLERATED rather
    than chased is the decision recorded in T168 — enumerating Markdown shapes
    without a parser did not converge (three review rounds, a new shape each), and
    a parser reads the metadata line as paragraph text too, so it would not have
    closed this one. The DEFAULT is what carries the rest: an item the audit
    cannot license is left exactly as it is and named in the report, because a
    refusal costs one command and a flattened queue file has no other copy.
    """
    for k in range(min(j, len(lines) - 1)):
        if HARD_BREAK_RE.search(lines[k]):
            return FOLD_HARDBREAK_REFUSAL
    for k in range(1, j):
        prev, line = lines[k - 1], lines[k]
        stripped = line.strip()
        # any whitespace, not a blank: a tab inside the line would otherwise
        # make the first "word" longer, the break wider, and the absorption
        # easier — the permissive direction, which is the destructive one
        first_word = stripped.split(None, 1)[0]
        if len(prev.rstrip()) + 1 + len(first_word) <= WRAP_COLUMN_FLOOR:
            return FOLD_PROSE_REFUSAL
        opener = stripped[0]
        if not (opener.isalnum() or opener in PROSE_OPENERS
                or unicodedata.category(opener) == "So"):
            return FOLD_PROSE_REFUSAL
        if METADATA_LINE_RE.match(stripped) and len(stripped) <= WRAP_COLUMN_FLOOR:
            return FOLD_PROSE_REFUSAL
    return None


def field_run(line):
    """The `**Field:** value` segments a line consists ENTIRELY of, or None when
    it carries anything else — the one line-shape the fold is allowed to move.

    "Entirely" means the segments TILE the stripped line: the first starts at
    its first character and each one begins where the last ended. Both halves
    are load-bearing, and each refuses a line whose fold would invent a field:

      opens with prose   `AUTONOMOUS. **Effort:** M.` — a value continued from
                         the line above, the shape the repairs text names as
                         unfoldable. Folded, the marker left dangling above takes
                         whatever follows as its value, and where what follows is
                         a NOTE the item acquires a field nobody wrote.
      stops short        a non-field bold after the last field. The value would
                         end there and the rest is prose the fold would carry
                         into the chain.

    Not asked: whether a note trails the LAST field on this same line. There the
    value grammar has already absorbed it, identically before and after the fold
    — the fold neither creates that ambiguity nor cures it.

    "Stops short" is now asked of the BOLD rather than of the segments. A value
    runs to the next marker or to the end of the line, so a bold that is no
    marker can no longer stop a segment — it is absorbed into a value, and the
    fold would carry that prose into the chain. So every `**` on the line has to
    belong to a marker, which is the same refusal, spelled where the grammar
    left it.
    """
    text = line.strip()
    segs = field_segments(text)
    if not segs or segs[0][1] != 0:
        return None
    bold = [(start, start + len(f"**{name}:**")) for name, start, _ in segs]
    if any(not any(s <= m.start() < e for s, e in bold)
           for m in re.finditer(r"\*\*", text)):
        return None
    return [text[start:end] for _, start, end in segs]


def fold_wrapped(lines, block):
    """(folded, why) for the item whose chain SHARES a line with the prose it
    wrapped out of — the second fold path, tried when the first declines.

    The walk above lifts a chain that already owns its lines. It was right to
    refuse this population: there is no run of field lines to relocate, and the
    chain is not whole until the hard wrap is undone. Measured across the twelve
    real queues, that population is 15 of the 17 items the walk left alone —
    `**Classe:**` ending a line with `EXTERNA. **Esforço:** P.` opening the next,
    a chain that starts mid-sentence, a value carrying a parenthetical.

    So the licence is a different one, and the four gates below are what make
    undoing a wrap safe on a file that has no other copy:

      structure   NOTHING may open a Markdown block. Where the walk lifts a chain
                  OVER a list and keeps it, this path has no chain to lift, so it
                  declines instead of choosing which lines to flatten — under its
                  OWN reason, since "guessing a field value" is not what it did
                  and a reader repairs the shape the sentence names.
      a marker    the LAST line has to carry one. A trailing note carries none,
                  and joined it lands inside the last field's value — greedy by
                  grammar — so `**Source:** 2026-08-13 nota solta.` becomes what
                  every reader reports as the source. This gate is why that item
                  is still left alone and named, and it costs the two items whose
                  last line is a WRAPPED VALUE: undecidable from the text, and a
                  refusal costs one command.
      the class   the joined chain has to name a class some gate reads — one of
                  CLASSES, or a spelling CLASS_ALIASES maps. A note absorbed as a
                  class value produces a word that is neither, which is the exact
                  failure the old refusal feared, caught after the join and before
                  anything is written.
      the position the chain has to BEGIN at **Class:**, with every readable
                  marker inside it. A segment ahead of the anchor is prose wearing
                  a field's name — `ver a **Deferred:** nota de contexto.` ends in
                  a period, so it joins the chain from the left — and the join
                  would promote it. That is the T164/T166 population, refused with
                  the code-span remedy named rather than folded.

    The geometry audit runs last, over every continuation line: a break far short
    of the wrap column is a line the author ENDED, not one an editor wrapped.
    """
    if any(opens_a_block(lines, k) for k in range(1, len(lines))):
        return None, FOLD_BLOCK_REFUSAL
    if not markers(lines[-1]):
        return None, FOLD_REFUSAL
    folded = (" ".join(ln.strip() for ln in lines)
              + block[len(block.rstrip("\n")):])
    cls = chain_class(folded)
    if cls is None or (cls not in CLASSES and cls.upper() not in CLASS_ALIASES):
        return None, FOLD_REFUSAL
    # asked AFTER the class, so an item that has no class at all is reported as
    # the different thing it is
    chain = field_chain(folded)
    if len(markers(folded)) != len(chain) or chain[0].canonical != "Class":
        return None, FOLD_PROSE_MARKER_REFUSAL
    refusal = absorption_audit(lines, len(lines))
    if refusal:
        return None, refusal
    return folded, None


def fold_chain_onto_first_line(block):
    """(the block with its field chain lifted onto the first line, why it was
    left alone) — at most one of the two is not None, and (None, None) means
    there was nothing to fold.

    The fold moves the FIELD LINES and nothing else. Every other line of the
    block — a nested list, a paragraph, an indented note — stays a line, with
    its own indentation, exactly where the user wrote it. Only the chain
    changes address, and the price is one of order: the fields end the first
    line, so they now precede prose that used to sit above them, which is where
    compose_item puts them on every item this queue writes itself.

    That price is the cheap half of the choice. The expensive half was measured:
    joining EVERY line of the block into one — which is what this did — turned
    one real item's five-item bulleted list, and another's eleven-line note,
    into one run-on line each, and reported both as folded. No
    character was lost, so the old docstring's claim ("the join moves no character
    of prose") stayed true while the structure that made the text readable was
    gone, in the user's only copy. 8 of the 11 items this command folded across the
    real queues carried prose in between.

    The fold RELOCATES a chain that is already whole; it never CONSTRUCTS one.
    That line is drawn by reading the item twice: the segments its trailing FIELD
    LINES already carry, and what `field_chain` reads back out of the folded line.
    Equal, every reader gets exactly the strings it got before, from a position the
    position rule can finally see. Different, the fold would have invented a field,
    so nothing is moved and the caller is told which items were left: a marker in
    the item's own prose joins the chain from the left (the shape
    claim_unwritable_message already refuses to promise the fold for), and a chain
    spread across a line that opens mid-value takes the wrong text as its value.

    Which lines may be absorbed is decided TWICE, by two questions that do not
    share an answer. `opens_a_block` reads the SHAPE, and it is an enumeration, so
    it is wrong about whatever nobody has enumerated yet. `absorption_audit` reads
    the GEOMETRY of the break and the character the line opens with, neither of
    which the enumeration can influence, and it refuses the item outright when it
    cannot license what the shape reading absorbed. The default is what changed:
    an unrecognised shape used to be absorbed — that is, flattened — and now it is
    left exactly as it is and named in the report.

    A marker left among the lines that stay is refused outright. Nothing here can
    tell the rest of a split chain from prose that merely quotes a marker, and
    lifting half a chain leaves an item that reads as repaired and is not.
    """
    lines = block.rstrip("\n").split("\n")
    if not fields_off_first_line(block):
        # nothing to lift, and this is the ONE question that says so: no field
        # marker sits off the first line. An item with no fields at all is a
        # different defect with a different repair (`edit --class`), and
        # reporting it here would name the fold for a shape the fold is not about.
        #
        # It used to be asked twice, and the first asking was about the CLASS: a
        # chain reaching **Class:** ended the run, on the grounds that the item
        # was already in the shape every gate reads. True of the class, and of
        # nothing else — a SECOND field left on a continuation line sits outside
        # the chain, where no gate reads it either, and that item was passed over
        # in silence. Measured on a sibling queue's T004, whose **Born:**
        # has been below the chain since it was written: `list` shows its age as
        # `?`, every `migrate` skips it, and no command anywhere says why. Once
        # the orphan is what decides, the class question answers nothing this one
        # does not, so it is gone rather than kept as decoration — the same
        # treatment the two prose readbacks at the end of this function got.
        return None, None
    run, first = [], len(lines)
    for line in reversed(lines[1:]):
        segs = field_run(line)
        if segs is None:
            break
        run[:0] = segs
        first -= 1
    if not run:
        # not a refusal yet: the walk found no run of field LINES, which is what
        # the second path is for
        return fold_wrapped(lines, block)
    # what the first line ALREADY carries — the chain the exit at the top passes
    # over when nothing is orphaned below it. Empty for the population the walk
    # was written for, a head of plain prose. Read only where the head's chain
    # is one the gates themselves read — it names exactly one **Class:** — so an
    # item whose chain no gate can read (naming none, or two) keeps taking the
    # path it took before, with the readback answering for the run alone.
    already = ([f.text for f in field_chain(block)]
               if chain_class(block) is not None else [])
    # the lines BETWEEN the head and the field run, split where the paragraph
    # stops: the wrapped ones are absorbed, and everything from the first block
    # onwards keeps its own line, in its own place, with its own indentation.
    #
    # A head that already carries a chain absorbs NOTHING, and the window is
    # empty rather than narrowed by a shape question: a line joined onto that
    # line lands between the chain and the run being lifted, which breaks the
    # very chain the fold is relocating. There is nothing to weigh — every line
    # below the head keeps its place, and only the orphaned field moves.
    j = 1
    while j < first and not already and not opens_a_block(lines, j):
        j += 1
    # the walk stopped at a line a setext underline promotes, with at least one
    # line above it already inside the absorbed run: the underline promotes the
    # WHOLE paragraph, so the join would put part of that heading on the first
    # line and leave the rest under the underline. At j == 1 nothing is absorbed
    # and the heading is whole where it stands, which is the shape
    # TestASetextTitleIsKeptWithItsUnderline pins and this may not disturb
    if 1 < j < first and promoted_by_setext(lines, j):
        return None, FOLD_SETEXT_REFUSAL
    refusal = absorption_audit(lines, j)
    if refusal:
        return None, refusal
    if already and 1 < first and HARD_BREAK_RE.search(lines[first - 1]):
        # the audit above asks about the lines the join ABSORBS, and with a head
        # that already carries a chain it absorbs none: `j` is 1, so it reaches
        # the head alone. The line the run is lifted out from UNDER keeps its
        # place and still loses its break, because what the break broke before
        # has moved onto the first line. Asked here rather than widened into the
        # audit, whose range is the join's and not the lift's.
        return None, FOLD_ORPHAN_BREAK_REFUSAL
    kept = lines[j:first]
    if markers("\n".join(kept)):
        return None, FOLD_SPLIT_REFUSAL
    head = " ".join([ln.strip() for ln in lines[:j]] + [ln.strip() for ln in lines[first:]])
    folded = "\n".join([head] + kept) + block[len(block.rstrip("\n")):]
    # rstripped, and only rstripped: a segment runs up to the next marker, so it
    # carries the blank that separated it from that marker — one
    # blank where the line ended, none where the file's newline used to be. A
    # raw comparison called that whitespace a changed value and refused an item
    # whose chain merely spanned two continuation lines. Absorbed PROSE, the
    # thing this gate is for, is not whitespace and still shows.
    chain = field_chain(folded)
    # `already` is in the expected chain for the same reason it emptied the
    # absorption window above: the segments the first line brought are part of
    # what the join gives back, and a readback that left them out would read
    # every orphan lifted onto a chain as a fold that invented a field
    if ([seg.rstrip() for seg in already + run]
            != [f.text.rstrip() for f in chain]):
        # the join gave back a chain the run does not account for — a segment
        # came from the head, so this is the wrapped shape, not a relocation
        return fold_wrapped(lines, block)
    if already and chain_class(folded) is None:
        # `already` says the head's chain named exactly one class, and the chain
        # read back above is `already + run`, so None here means the run brought a
        # SECOND **Class:** — the one answer `chain_class` refuses to guess. The
        # item is readable as it stands and unreadable folded: `list` and every
        # gate would show its class as `?` afterwards, in the user's only copy,
        # under a line reporting the item as folded. The lift may cost the item
        # its line breaks only by refusing; it may never cost it its class.
        #
        # The CLASS alone, and the asymmetry is the point: an orphan repeating
        # any OTHER field of the head lands in the chain twice, and `edit
        # --<field>` then refuses that item — loudly, naming the field, with
        # every value still on the screen. That is the recoverable direction. A
        # doubled class is the silent one: `list` and `pack` go back to showing
        # `?` and no command anywhere says why, which is the defect this fold was
        # widened to repair.
        return None, FOLD_SECOND_CLASS_REFUSAL
    # Where the PROSE readback used to be. It stood here as two comparisons, and a
    # review measured both unable to fail — so they are gone rather than kept as
    # decoration, and what each was for now sits where it can:
    #   structure  it counted BLOCK_START_RE hits before and after the join. That
    #              is the question that DECIDED the join, so it agreed with every
    #              decision, right or wrong: for a shape the regex never matched
    #              both counts were zero and the check passed on a flattened item.
    #              Its job — refusing rather than flattening when the shape reading
    #              is wrong — is `absorption_audit`, which asks about geometry and
    #              about how the line opens, two things the shape reading cannot
    #              answer for itself.
    #   verbatim   `back[1:] != lines[j:first]` was unreachable by construction:
    #              `back` is built by joining `kept`, and `kept` IS `lines[j:first]`,
    #              so the two sides were the same object's text on every input. The
    #              nesting it meant to protect is protected by not stripping `kept`
    #              in the first place, which is one line above and is mutation-tested.
    return folded, None


def group_by_reason(pairs):
    """(reason, [labels]) for `migrate`'s two refusal reports, in INSERTION order.

    Grouped BY REASON and not by item: each reason names the repair its items
    need, and a reader acts on the sentence once for the whole group. Insertion
    order, so a queue carrying one reason reads exactly as it did before there
    were two.

    One implementation, two callers — the fold's refusals and the backdating's —
    because a report where one half groups and the other half does not is a
    report whose reader learns two shapes for one idea.
    """
    grouped = {}
    for label, why in pairs:
        grouped.setdefault(why, []).append(label)
    return grouped.items()


def cmd_migrate(args):
    memdir = memory_dir(args.dir)
    # ONE gate for every write this command makes through `write_atomic` — the
    # queue and the done-log. The flag is read HERE, once, and both call sites
    # below say `save(...)` without asking again: a `--dry-run` that tested the
    # flag at each write is a `--dry-run` a third write silently escapes, and the
    # file it would escape into holds the user's own prose and has no other copy.
    # The command's remaining disk effect is a DELETE, and it is gated inside
    # `handoff_collect` rather than here — that function is also the collection of
    # `done`, `cancel` and `edit`, which have no preview to offer, so the flag
    # travels to it as an argument instead of closing over this binding.
    # Announced on stderr, where the resolved queue dir already goes: stdout
    # carries the report, and the report must be the real run's, byte for byte.
    # The queue's exclusive lock is still taken, dry or not — previewing a queue
    # another writer is mid-rewrite of would preview a state that never existed — so
    # a queue no command has ever written gains an EMPTY `.tk-queue.lock`. It is the
    # one name a preview can bring into existence, and it never carries a byte.
    save = (lambda _path, _text: None) if args.dry_run else write_atomic
    if args.dry_run:
        print(f"tk-queue: --dry-run: writes {DRY_RUN_WRITES}. The report below is what a "
              "real `migrate` prints, on this queue, right now", file=sys.stderr)
    path = os.path.join(memdir, "next-steps.md")
    content = read(path)
    if content is None:
        fail("next-steps.md not found in " + memdir)
    blocks = split_blocks(content)
    done = [t for k, t in blocks if k == "item-done"]
    fresh, replayed = [], []
    nid = max_id(memdir)
    if done:
        log_path = os.path.join(memdir, "done-log.md")
        log = read(log_path) or DONE_LOG_TEMPLATE
        header = "## Migrado do next-steps (histórico pré-log)"
        # A crash between this write and the queue write below leaves every moved
        # block in BOTH files, and the rerun is what repairs that — so it moves
        # only what the log does not already carry. Measured before the check
        # existed: the rerun wrote a second copy of every block and put it ABOVE
        # the first, so the log stopped being a history in order.
        for text in done:
            (replayed if interrupted_close(log, moved=text) else fresh).append(text)
        if replayed:
            warn_once(f"{len(replayed)} [x] item(s) are already in {log_path} and still "
                      "in next-steps.md — the queue half of a `migrate` a crash "
                      "interrupted. Finishing that half; the log is not written again, "
                      "and its order is left as it stands.")
        if fresh:
            migrated = "\n".join(t.rstrip("\n") for t in fresh)
            if header in log:
                log = log.replace(header, header + "\n\n" + migrated + "\n", 1)
            else:
                log = log.rstrip("\n") + f"\n\n{header}\n\nMovidos verbatim em {datetime.date.today().isoformat()}; itens carregam as próprias datas.\n\n{migrated}\n"
            save(log_path, log)
    out, dropped_prev = [], False
    folded, left_alone = [], []
    translated, untranslated = [], []
    backdated, undated = [], []
    today = datetime.date.today()
    for kind, text in blocks:
        if kind == "item-done":
            dropped_prev = True
            continue
        if kind == "item-open":
            # the fold first, the ID after: the head is the same before and
            # after either, and reading the LABEL last is what makes an item
            # that gained both appear under the number it now carries, instead
            # of as `----`
            new, refusal = fold_chain_onto_first_line(text)
            text = new if new is not None else text
            if item_id(text) is None:
                nid += 1
                text = text.replace("- [ ] ", f"- [ ] **T{nid:03d}** — ", 1)
            if new is not None:
                folded.append(item_label(text))
            elif refusal:
                left_alone.append((item_label(text), refusal))
            # AFTER the fold, which is what puts a legacy chain on the line
            # `chain_class` reads: a class value on a continuation line is not a
            # class value to any reader, so translating it there would repair
            # nothing and report a repair
            text, pair = translate_class_value(text)
            if pair:
                translated.append(f"{item_label(text)} ({pair[0]} \u2192 {pair[1]})")
            else:
                unmapped = chain_class(text)
                if unmapped is not None and unmapped not in CLASSES:
                    untranslated.append((item_label(text), NO_CLASS_MAPPING))
            # AFTER the fold and the ID: the fold is what puts a legacy item's
            # fields where a field can be placed beside them, and the label is
            # read once the item carries the number it will be reported under.
            # An item that already has a stamp is left alone — this command is
            # rerun, and a second pass must not restamp what the first one dated
            text, why = backdate(text, today)
            if why is None:
                backdated.append(item_label(text))
            elif why != "stamped":
                undated.append((item_label(text), NO_BIRTHDATE[why]))
        if dropped_prev and out:
            # collapse surplus blank lines only at the junction left by the removal
            prev = out.pop()
            nb = len(prev) - len(prev.rstrip("\n"))
            na = len(text) - len(text.lstrip("\n"))
            if nb + na > 2:
                prev, text = prev.rstrip("\n") + "\n\n", text.lstrip("\n")
            out.append(prev)
        dropped_prev = False
        out.append(text)
    new_content = "".join(out)
    if dropped_prev:  # file ended on a removed block
        new_content = new_content.rstrip("\n") + "\n"
    save(path, new_content)
    # `fresh`, never `done`: a rerun after a crash moves nothing, and a count of
    # what it FOUND would report a write it did not make
    print(f"{len(fresh)} [x] item(s) → done-log; IDs assigned up to T{nid:03d}")
    # Both lines carry the IDs, and the second one is the whole reason this
    # command may rewrite the file at all: a run that folded most of a queue and
    # stayed silent about the items it could not would be reporting success for a
    # job half done, on text that has no other copy.
    if folded:
        print(f"{len(folded)} item(s) with fields off the first line: folded up, where "
              "every gate reads them — " + ", ".join(folded))
    if left_alone:
        for why, labels in group_by_reason(left_alone):
            print(f"{len(labels)} item(s) left exactly as they are: {why} — "
                  + ", ".join(labels) + ". Close each with `cancel` and re-add it clean.")
    # The class values, in the same two shapes: what was translated, and what was
    # NAMED instead. An unmapped value leaves the item out of every package on
    # every machine, and a silent decline is an item nobody learns about
    if translated:
        print(f"{len(translated)} item(s) with a class value in Portuguese: written as "
              "the enum every gate reads — " + ", ".join(translated))
    if untranslated:
        for why, labels in group_by_reason(untranslated):
            print(f"{len(labels)} item(s) left with a class value no gate reads: {why} — "
                  + ", ".join(labels) + ". Give each one of "
                  + ", ".join(CLASSES) + " with `tk-queue edit <id> --class <CLASS>`.")
    # The backdating, reported in the same two shapes as the fold: what it did,
    # and — grouped by reason — what it declined to do. The second half is the
    # load-bearing one. An item this command leaves undated shows `?` in `list`
    # forever and nothing else ever says why, so a silent decline is an age the
    # reader will keep waiting for
    if backdated:
        print(f"{len(backdated)} item(s) backdated from **Source:** — "
              + ", ".join(backdated))
    if undated:
        for why, labels in group_by_reason(undated):
            print(f"{len(labels)} item(s) left with no age: {why} — "
                  + ", ".join(labels) + ". Nothing here invents one.")
    # `migrate` closes items too — that is its whole job — so it is a closing path,
    # and a closing path that left the briefing behind would be the orphan every
    # other one refuses to make. Measured: a legacy [x] item with a briefing left
    # it standing beside an empty queue.
    ids = set()
    for text in done:
        ids |= handoff_candidates(item_id(text), text)
    handoff_collect(memdir, new_content, ids, dry_run=args.dry_run)


def main():
    common = argparse.ArgumentParser(add_help=False)
    common.add_argument("--dir", default=argparse.SUPPRESS,
                        help="memory dir override (default: resolved from cwd)")
    p = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog="tk-queue", description=__doc__.splitlines()[0])
    p.add_argument("--dir", default=None, help=argparse.SUPPRESS)
    sub = p.add_subparsers(dest="cmd", required=True)

    sub.add_parser("list", parents=[common], help="open items with IDs")

    a = sub.add_parser("add", parents=[common], help="new item in the queue")
    a.add_argument("text")
    a.add_argument("--class", dest="classe", required=True, help="|".join(CLASSES))
    a.add_argument("--effort", required=True, help="S/M/L + wall-clock guess, e.g. 'M (~30min)'")
    a.add_argument("--risk", help="only when unsupervised execution can do damage "
                   "('none' writes no Risk line)")
    a.add_argument("--criterion", required=True,
                   help="acceptance: 'A: <command that proves it>' or 'B: user verdict'")
    a.add_argument("--deferred", help=f"why the decision could not be asked — REQUIRED by "
                   f"--class {DEFERRABLE_CLASS}, refused on every other class ('afk' in an "
                   "unattended session)")
    a.add_argument("--env", help="environment the item runs in — a name from the site "
                   "file's roster (~/.claude/tk/env), matched exactly; omit it for the "
                   "machine that owns this queue ('none' writes no Env line)")
    a.add_argument("--project", help="optional short slug tagging which project this item "
                   "belongs to, e.g. 'ambiente', 'tk' (warns on stderr if unseen in this queue)")
    a.add_argument("--blocked-by", metavar="T<n>",
                   help="an item of THIS queue that has to close first. `pack` leaves "
                        "this item OUT of the package while that one is open, naming the "
                        "value in the reason, and lets it back in the moment the blocker "
                        "is closed — no re-edit. Any id spelling (T006, t006, 006, 6) is "
                        "stored as `T006`. It is NOT checked against the queue: a blocker "
                        "already closed and archived has left it, and that field is then "
                        f"a satisfied dependency, not a typo. '{FIELD_CLEAR}' writes no "
                        "line")
    a.add_argument("--source", help="origin (default: today's date)")
    a.add_argument("--ticket", help="the forge ticket this item implements, `<repo>#<n>` "
                                    "— feeds the `closes` line of its PR. ONE spelling is "
                                    "stored: repo lower-cased, number without leading "
                                    "zeros (`Ambiente#0171` is written `ambiente#171`), "
                                    "`owner/repo#n` refused. The shape is exact on the "
                                    "way in and asked again on the way out — `pack` marks "
                                    "a hand-edited one `[?]`")
    a.add_argument("--spec", help="the spec the ticket belongs to, `<repo>#<n>` — decides "
                                  "the item's LANE in the afk package (see `pack --help`). "
                                  "Same one spelling as `--ticket`: two casings of one "
                                  "repo would be two specs of one ticket each, and two "
                                  "branches for one spec")
    a.add_argument("--repo", help="the repository this item's CODE lands in. A WHITELIST "
                                  "of five shapes: https://<host>/<path>, "
                                  "ssh://git@<host>/<path>, git@<host>:<path>, "
                                  "file:///<path>, or an ABSOLUTE path "
                                  "(/root/.claude/skills, C:\\clones\\repo). A `~/` "
                                  "address is refused (HOME differs per reader), a "
                                  "`<user>@` half on an http(s) URL is refused (it would "
                                  "store a credential this file reprints), and so are a "
                                  "remote NAME like `origin` and a relative path. The "
                                  "tracker `--ticket`/`--spec` name is routinely another "
                                  "repo (see `pack --help`)")
    a.add_argument("--force", action="store_true",
                   help=f"raise BOTH ceilings for this one call: the item BLOCK, from "
                        f"{CEILING} chars to {CEILING_FORCED}, and each field VALUE, "
                        f"from {FIELD_CEILING_SHORT}/{FIELD_CEILING} to "
                        f"{FIELD_CEILING_SHORT_FORCED}/{FIELD_CEILING_FORCED} (short "
                        "fields/prose). It does NOT reach the WIP cap, which has no way "
                        "through by design")

    d = sub.add_parser("done", parents=[common], help="conclude: move the item to the done-log")
    d.add_argument("id", type=parse_id)
    d.add_argument("--how", required=True, help="outcome + pointer: 'PR #82', 'commit abc123', '[[slug]]'")
    d.add_argument("--summary", help="replaces the item text on the log line")
    d.add_argument("--note", help="extra substance when there is NO pointer (the line is the only record)")
    d.add_argument("--force", action="store_true",
                   help=f"raise BOTH ceilings for this one call: each field VALUE from "
                        f"{FIELD_CEILING_SHORT}/{FIELD_CEILING} to "
                        f"{FIELD_CEILING_SHORT_FORCED}/{FIELD_CEILING_FORCED} (short "
                        f"fields/prose), and the item BLOCK from {CEILING} to "
                        f"{CEILING_FORCED}")

    c = sub.add_parser("cancel", parents=[common],
                       help="discard: move the item to the done-log as DESCARTADO",
                       description="Discard an item: it moves to the done-log as "
                       "DESCARTADO. FUSING two items into one, the order is `edit "
                       "--text` on the survivor FIRST and `cancel` on the source only "
                       "then. The two commands are not one transaction, and the "
                       "700-char block ceiling can refuse the edit that carries the "
                       "union — cancelled first, that content is out of the queue when "
                       "the refusal arrives. Measured three times in the consolidation "
                       "of 2026-09-02.")
    c.add_argument("id", type=parse_id)
    c.add_argument("--why", required=True)
    c.add_argument("--summary")
    c.add_argument("--note")
    c.add_argument("--force", action="store_true",
                   help=f"raise BOTH ceilings for this one call: each field VALUE from "
                        f"{FIELD_CEILING_SHORT}/{FIELD_CEILING} to "
                        f"{FIELD_CEILING_SHORT_FORCED}/{FIELD_CEILING_FORCED} (short "
                        f"fields/prose), and the item BLOCK from {CEILING} to "
                        f"{CEILING_FORCED}")

    e = sub.add_parser("edit", parents=[common], help="edit fields of an open item")
    e.add_argument("id", type=parse_id)
    e.add_argument("--text", help="REPLACES the item's text — everything between the "
                   "`- [ ] **T<id>** —` head and the first field marker, continuation "
                   "prose included; it never appends, so a fusion passes the union of "
                   "both texts. FUSING two items: this `edit` FIRST, then `cancel` the "
                   "source. The two are not one transaction, and the block ceiling can "
                   "refuse the edit carrying the union — done in the other order, the "
                   "source is already in the done-log when the refusal lands")
    e.add_argument("--class", dest="classe",
                   help="replaces the whole class VALUE. On an item that carries "
                   "none the anchor is written AHEAD of any fields already ending "
                   "the first line — the only position a gate reads them from — and "
                   "those fields are named on stderr, and any OTHER flag in that "
                   "same call is refused — it would rewrite the run the anchor just "
                   "promoted, before you have read the names. Refused too, and "
                   "nothing written, on an item whose fields sit off the first line "
                   "(`migrate` folds them first) and on one carrying a **Deferred:** "
                   "marker away from its position. An item with no fields at all "
                   "promotes nothing, and takes its class and its fields in one call")
    e.add_argument("--effort")
    e.add_argument("--risk", help="new Risk line, or 'none' to DELETE the field "
                   "(re-triage of an obsolete Risk)")
    e.add_argument("--criterion")
    e.add_argument("--deferred", help=f"justification demanded by `--class "
                   f"{DEFERRABLE_CLASS}`; it is dropped by leaving that class, never by "
                   "'none'")
    e.add_argument("--env", help="new environment (a name from the site file's roster), "
                   "or 'none' to DELETE the field — the item then runs wherever the "
                   "queue does")
    e.add_argument("--project", help="assign/change the project tag")
    e.add_argument("--blocked-by", metavar="T<n>",
                   help=f"the item that has to close first, or '{FIELD_CLEAR}' to DELETE "
                        "the field — the blocker that was cancelled rather than closed. "
                        "See `add --help` for the shape and for what `pack` does with it")
    e.add_argument("--spec", help="the spec this ticket belongs to, `<repo>#<n>` — "
                   "written here, or REWRITTEN. It is the only field of its group with "
                   "a flag on this command: **Ticket:** and **Repo:** say where the item "
                   "came from and where its code lands, which cannot change while it "
                   "stays the same item, and their repair is `cancel` + re-add. **Spec:** "
                   "is the item's LANE in the afk package (see `pack --help`) — a routing "
                   "decision over a queue that moves. Same shape and same one spelling as "
                   "on the way in (`Ambiente#0171` is stored `ambiente#171`); "
                   f"'{FIELD_CLEAR}' does NOT clear it — a lane is changed, never emptied")
    e.add_argument("--force", action="store_true",
                   help=f"raise BOTH ceilings for this one call: the item BLOCK from "
                        f"{CEILING} to {CEILING_FORCED}, and each field VALUE from "
                        f"{FIELD_CEILING_SHORT}/{FIELD_CEILING} to "
                        f"{FIELD_CEILING_SHORT_FORCED}/{FIELD_CEILING_FORCED} (short "
                        "fields/prose)")

    cl = sub.add_parser("claim", parents=[common],
                        help="take an item, so a sibling session does not work it too")
    cl.add_argument("id", type=parse_id)
    cl.add_argument("--as", dest="owner", required=True,
                    help="who is taking it — a session or host label STARTING with a "
                         "letter or a digit, then letters, digits, '.', '-', '_', at "
                         "most 32 chars, no spaces ('.local' and '_alpha' are refused "
                         "for their first character): it is written beside the moment "
                         "of the claim and both are read back")

    rl = sub.add_parser("release", parents=[common],
                        help="hand a claimed item back to the queue without closing it")
    rl.add_argument("id", type=parse_id)

    hf = sub.add_parser("handoff", parents=[common],
                        help="write the item's briefing — the file the next session "
                             "reads; it is deleted when the item is closed",
                        epilog=HANDOFF_FORMAT,
                        formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter)
    hf.add_argument("id", type=parse_id)
    hf.add_argument("--objective", required=True,
                    help="field 1 — where this front is going")
    hf.add_argument("--state", required=True, help="field 2 — what is done and "
                    "decided, by path/URL, without duplicating it")
    hf.add_argument("--blockers", required=True, help="field 3 — blockers and "
                    "notes; spell 'none' out rather than leave it off")
    hf.add_argument("--skills", help="field 4 (optional) — skills the next "
                    "session should reach for")
    hf.add_argument("--pitfalls", help="field 5 (optional) — known traps")
    hf.add_argument("--force", action="store_true",
                    help="overwrite a file of this name that this command did not write")

    b = sub.add_parser("bump", parents=[common],
                       help="move an item to the top of the queue's global order")
    b.add_argument("id", type=parse_id)

    r = sub.add_parser("report", parents=[common], help="done-log entries")
    r.add_argument("--since", help="YYYY-MM-DD")
    r.add_argument("--all", action="store_true", help="sweeps every project")

    pk = sub.add_parser("pack", parents=[common],
                        help="candidates for an unattended package: the eligible items in "
                             "queue order, and every exclusion with the value that caused it",
                        epilog=PACK_FORMAT,
                        formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter)
    pk.add_argument("--spec-under-way", action="append", metavar="<repo>#<n>",
                    help="a spec the caller has already found a branch for on the remote, "
                         "`<repo>#<n>` — REPEATABLE. It is skipped when the accumulated "
                         "lane is elected, and its tickets leave the package on the lane "
                         "rung whatever their count. This command opens no network "
                         "connection, so this flag is the only way it can know (see LANE)")
    pk.add_argument("--blocked", action="append", metavar="<repo>#<n>",
                    help="a TICKET the caller has found blocked on the tracker, "
                         "`<repo>#<n>` — REPEATABLE. The item carrying it leaves the "
                         "package, and its spec's ticket COUNT with it, so a spec held "
                         "up by one blocked ticket stops winning the accumulated lane "
                         "on that ticket's strength (see LANE)")

    mg = sub.add_parser("migrate", parents=[common],
                        help="move legacy [x] items to the done-log, assign IDs to open "
                             "ones, and fold a field chain that sits off the first line "
                             "onto it")
    mg.add_argument("--dry-run", action="store_true",
                    help=f"print the report a real run would print and write {DRY_RUN_WRITES}. "
                         "The preview to read BEFORE letting this rewrite the text of a "
                         "real queue")

    args = p.parse_args()
    handler = {
        "list": cmd_list,
        "add": cmd_add,
        "done": cmd_done,
        "cancel": cmd_cancel,
        "edit": cmd_edit,
        "claim": cmd_claim,
        "release": cmd_release,
        "handoff": cmd_handoff,
        "bump": cmd_bump,
        "report": cmd_report,
        "pack": cmd_pack,
        "migrate": cmd_migrate,
    }[args.cmd]
    # Which queue this command is aimed at is INFERRED — from --dir, or from the
    # cwd when it is absent — and an inference nobody sees is an inference nobody
    # checks. A caller whose cwd is not where they think (the agent's shell keeps
    # its cwd between calls) edits a homonymous item in ANOTHER project's queue
    # and is told "T019 updated", which reads as success. The same wrong cwd
    # answers a `list` with another project's two items and says nothing at all,
    # which is why READING commands announce too: a read is what a decision is
    # made from, and silence there is the same inference gone unchecked.
    # stderr, so it never contaminates the stdout that `add` and `pack` callers
    # parse, and BEFORE the work, so a command that then fails or blocks on the
    # lock has still said where it was aiming.
    #
    # `report` is the ONE command with no single dir to name: it sweeps every
    # project's queue under PROJECTS_ROOT by construction, so there is no
    # inference for it to expose and no dir of its own to resolve.
    memdir = None if args.cmd == "report" else memory_dir(args.dir)
    if memdir is not None:
        print(f"tk-queue: queue: {memdir}", file=sys.stderr)
    if args.cmd not in READERS:
        # taken BEFORE the handler's first read, released after its last write:
        # a lock around the write alone would still let a stale snapshot win
        with queue_lock(memdir):
            handler(args)
    else:
        handler(args)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
